null,"drive_file_id":null,"drive_folder_id":null,"drive_url":null,"daybook_asset_id":null,"errors":null,"warnings":null,"failed_stage":null,"error_code":null,"resolved_source_ids":null,"retry_count":0,"initiated_by":"seed","started_at":"2026-05-13T17:09:30.274Z","completed_at":"2026-05-13T17:09:42.127Z","notion_retries":null,"compiled_sections":null,"generated_filename":null,"notion_upload_id":null},{"id":"258ac05e000000000000000000000000","production_spec_id":"442595af000000000000000000000000","operation":"validate_and_compile","status":"complete","dry_run":false,"payload_version":null,"compiled_prompt":null,"prompt_hash":null,"compiled_prompt_status":null,"visual_asset_notion_id":null,"asset_id":"WS-WYC-V01-HP004-MASTER","asset_version":"v001","provider":"dalle3","model_name":"dall-e-3","model_version":null,"generation_settings":null,"seed":null,"provider_request_id":null,"cost_usd":null,"drive_file_id":null,"drive_folder_id":null,"drive_url":null,"daybook_asset_id":null,"errors":null,"warnings":null,"failed_stage":null,"error_code":null,"resolved_source_ids":null,"retry_count":0,"initiated_by":"seed","started_at":"2026-05-11T17:09:30.275Z","completed_at":"2026-05-11T17:09:41.986Z","notion_retries":null,"compiled_sections":null,"generated_filename":null,"notion_upload_id":null},{"id":"16034468000000000000000000000000","production_spec_id":"61ff000f000000000000000000000000","operation":"validate_and_compile","status":"complete","dry_run":false,"payload_version":null,"compiled_prompt":null,"prompt_hash":null,"compiled_prompt_status":null,"visual_asset_notion_id":null,"asset_id":"WS-WYC-V01-CP001-MASTER","asset_version":"v001","provider":"dalle3","model_name":"dall-e-3","model_version":null,"generation_settings":null,"seed":null,"provider_request_id":null,"cost_usd":null,"drive_file_id":null,"drive_folder_id":null,"drive_url":null,"daybook_asset_id":null,"errors":null,"warnings":null,"failed_stage":null,"error_code":null,"resolved_source_ids":null,"retry_count":0,"initiated_by":"seed","started_at":"2026-05-08T17:09:30.275Z","completed_at":"2026-05-08T17:09:35.770Z","notion_retries":null,"compiled_sections":null,"generated_filename":null,"notion_upload_id":null},{"id":"bf6a39b7-67d4-46e8-9433-4b0c7ccc4cd6","production_spec_id":"3ae04eb4-bf02-8121-9ab4-fe5c91a6d41a","operation":"validate_and_compile","status":"compiled","dry_run":true,"payload_version":"PP-2.0","compiled_prompt":"[WORLD AND COLLECTION CONTEXT]\nWorld: Wychcombe\n\n[STYLE SYSTEM]\nSource\nThis record represents the approved working standard documented in Volume I Visual Language: The Curator's Desk.\nGoverning scope\nApplies to all Volume I Hero Papers, coordinating papers, journal cards, ephemera, pockets, envelopes, tags, labels, hidden discoveries, packaging, and marketing assets unless a documented design decision states otherwise.\nIntegrity rule\nProduction Specification records must reference this Visual Style Guide through the Style Guide relation. Prompt modules such as Curator's Desk belong only in the Prompt Modules relation.\n\n[COMPONENT REQUIREMENTS]\nApproved Flagship Component Standard\n1. Governing Principle\nA Hero Paper is the flagship visual anchor of a volume or major section. It should communicate the world, emotional tone, setting, season, and narrative promise at a glance while remaining useful as a printable journal paper.\nIt must not become a conventional poster, book cover, or isolated illustration that loses practical value when cropped, folded, layered, or partially obscured.\n2. Purpose\nHero Papers:\nEstablish the narrative identity of a volume\nIntroduce the dominant emotional tone\nReveal the collection's most important setting, artifact, or visual motif\nGuide coordinating paper, journal card, and ephemera development\nCreate the primary visual used in product previews and collection navigation\nProvide a memorable entry point into the world\n3. Narrative Role\nA Hero Paper is the first visual sentence of the volume.\nIt should answer, without explanatory text:\nWhere are we?\nWhat season or time are we experiencing?\nWhat emotional atmosphere governs this volume?\nWhich objects, places, or botanical elements matter here?\nWhat story might be waiting just outside the frame?\nThe paper should imply narrative movement without resolving the story.\n4. Asset Intent\nPrimary intents:\nFoundation\nStorytelling\nPossible secondary intents:\nBotanical Study\nCurator Collection\nInteractive\nLayering\nMixed Media\nThe Hero Paper must remain a practical asset even when its primary intent is storytelling.\n5. Narrative Density\nThe default is Suggestive.\nEvidence-Rich Hero Papers require stricter canon and legibility review.\n6. Reuse Level\nThe default is Volume.\nA Hero Paper may be classified as:\nSeries when it represents the entire series rather than one volume\nVolume when it is the flagship artwork of one volume\nUnique when reuse would diminish a singular narrative reveal or canon artifact\nHero Papers should rarely be Universal or World-level assets because they are intended to provide distinctive identity.\n7. Canon Dependency\nThe default is Supports Canon.\nUse:\nNone only for purely atmospheric, non-specific scene work\nSupports Canon when the Hero Paper reinforces approved setting and lore\nCanon Reference when it directly depicts a governed person, place, artifact, institution, emblem, or event\nCanon Defining when it introduces a new visual truth that becomes binding for future work\nCanon Defining Hero Papers require explicit approval before release.\n8. Required Creative Definition\nBefore prompt assembly, each Hero Paper production item must define:\nScene title\nEmotional tone\nFocal subject\nSupporting motifs\nForeground elements\nMiddle-ground elements\nBackground environment\nSeason\nTime of day or lighting condition\nWeather or atmospheric condition, when relevant\nNarrative Density\nAsset Intent\nReuse Level\nCanon Dependency\nRelationship to companion papers and ephemera\nA Hero Paper must not enter generation with only a broad theme.\n9. Composition Model\nHero Papers should use intentional visual hierarchy.\nPrimary Focal Subject\nOne dominant visual idea should anchor the paper. This may be:\nA place\nA desk or room\nA greenhouse\nA garden gate\nA botanical specimen arrangement\nA meaningful object\nA view into a landscape\nA canon-linked artifact\nSupporting Motifs\nSecondary details should deepen the scene without competing with the focal subject.\nDepth\nWhen scene-based, use:\nForeground for tactile or framing elements\nMiddle ground for the focal narrative subject\nBackground for environmental context and atmosphere\nNegative Space\nPreserve areas where the paper can be written on, layered over, cropped, or folded without destroying the image's meaning.\n10. Crop and Construction Tolerance\nA Hero Paper must remain usable when:\nCropped to 8.5 × 11 or A4\nCropped to A5 or half-page\nFolded into signatures\nPartially covered by pockets or ephemera\nUsed behind journal cards\nTrimmed for covers or marketing panels\nIrreplaceable focal details should remain at least 0.25 inch from the edge unless edge placement is intentional and approved.\n11. Visual Goals\nEvery Hero Paper should demonstrate:\nImmediate recognition of setting or narrative identity\nEmotional clarity\nHistorical and material authenticity\nFlagship-level detail\nControlled visual hierarchy\nAtmospheric depth\nBelievable lighting\nCohesion with the collection palette\nStrong crop tolerance\nPractical usability as journal paper\nNo obvious AI artifacts\nA Hero Paper should be memorable without becoming theatrical or visually noisy.\n12. Prompt Governance\nEvery Hero Paper must link to its governing Prompt Modules and Style Guide.\nThe prompt package should define:\nScene or subject\nEmotional tone\nFocal hierarchy\nForeground, middle ground, and background\nLighting and atmosphere\nMaterials\nEra and world constraints\nBotanical or architectural accuracy requirements\nNarrative Density\nCanon Dependency\nPrint and crop requirements\nNegative constraints\nWorld-specific and volume-specific prompt modules are mandatory for Volume or Unique reuse classifications.\n13. Master Production Standard\nPixel dimensions are the controlling quality requirement. The file must also report 300 DPI.\n14. Asset Family\nEvery approved Hero Paper requires:\nDerived crops inherit the master asset's provenance and governance.\n15. Companion Asset Relationship\nA Hero Paper should inform, but not be duplicated by:\nCoordinating Decorative Papers\nJournal Cards\nEphemera Sheets\nPockets\nEnvelopes\nTags\nLabels\nMarketing Assets\nCompanion assets may reuse palette, texture, motifs, and environmental cues. They should not merely shrink or repeat the Hero Paper unless the derivative use is intentional and documented.\n16. Botanical and Architectural Accuracy\nWhen present:\nBotanicals must be plausible for the governed location, season, and setting\nArchitecture must be structurally believable and consistent with the world\nFurniture, tools, fixtures, labels, signage, and household objects must match the era and narrative context\nWritten text must be legible, intentional, or omitted\nNo modern objects may appear unless specifically governed\nAccuracy review should focus on what is visible enough to become a lasting visual fact.\n17. Lighting and Atmosphere\nLighting should support the emotional tone and preserve print clarity.\nAvoid:\nExcessive darkness\nCrushed shadows\nBlown highlights\nUnmotivated glow\nConflicting light sources\nCinematic effects that make the paper unusable as a printed surface\nAtmosphere should deepen the world without obscuring focal information.\n18. Daybook Contract\nDaybook must be able to retrieve or derive:\nComponent Type\nCollection, World, Series, and Volume\nScene title\nEmotional tone\nFocal subject\nSupporting motifs\nSeason and time of day\nAsset Intent\nNarrative Density\nReuse Level\nCanon Dependency\nPrompt version and linked Prompt Modules\nStyle Guide version\nMaster dimensions and DPI\nOutput formats\nFilename and revision\nApproval status\nAsset-family members\nCompanion-asset relationships\nCanon and production-pipeline relationships\nDaybook must not publish a Hero Paper with unresolved Canon Defining status, incomplete metadata, or missing required derivatives.\n19. Filename Standard\n{WORLD}_{VOLUME}_HP_{SEQ}_Master.png\n{WORLD}_{VOLUME}_HP_{SEQ}_Kit.png\n{WORLD}_{VOLUME}_HP_{SEQ}_Print.pdf\n{WORLD}_{VOLUME}_HP_{SEQ}_Preview.jpg\n{WORLD}_{VOLUME}_HP_{SEQ}_Thumb.jpg\nExample:\nWC_V01_HP_001_Master.png\n20. Quality Assurance Checklist\nCreative Review\nVisual Review\nProduction Review\nGovernance Review\n21. Definition of Done\nA Hero Paper is complete only when:\nThe flagship master is approved\nNarrative identity and emotional tone are clear\nRequired companion direction is documented\nAll required derivatives are generated\nPrint QA is passed\nPrompt, Style Guide, Canon, Visual Asset, and Pipeline relationships are complete where applicable\nFilename and metadata validate successfully\nDaybook can publish the asset without manual correction or an unresolved creative decision\n22. Version and Decision Control\nAll changes to this specification require:\nA new version number\nA decision summary\nA reason for change\nAn impact assessment\nApproval by the designated authority\nThis specification inherits the governance structure established by CS-001 while adding Hero Paper requirements for narrative identity, focal hierarchy, companion-asset direction, and canon-sensitive scene composition.\n\n[3AE04EB4-BF02-81AD-A281-D353BD9A704E]\nPurpose\nProvides the stable visual DNA for Wychcombe across canon references, environments, characters, artifacts, and product artwork.\nPrompt Assembly Position\nPlace this module first, before subject and composition instructions.\nContinuity Rules\nAtmosphere is restrained, not melodramatic.\nHistorical detail must feel inhabited and specific.\nMystery is communicated through evidence, omission, and arrangement.\nAvoid visual spectacle that overwhelms narrative meaning.\nAge should appear through use, repair, fading, patina, and custody rather than indiscriminate damage.\n\n[3AE04EB4-BF02-81AF-A302-C9A5EF68876E]\nPurpose\nDefines the recurring visual system for curated evidence layouts associated with the Wychcombe Journal Collection.\nRequired Behaviors\nObjects must imply relationships.\nAt least one focal object should anchor the composition.\nSupporting items should reinforce a clue, person, place, or time period.\nSurfaces should show realistic handling and age.\nNegative space should remain available for readability and product use.\nRecommended Uses\nVolume mood boards\nEphemera collections\nArtifact presentation\nProduct covers and marketing crops\nCanon-reference still lifes\n\n[3AE04EB4-BF02-81B0-AD10-EAD1DADD761B]\nMaterial Standard\nMaterials should remain visually distinct. Paper should not resemble leather; tarnished brass should not resemble painted gold; faded ink should remain legible where the story requires it.\nAging Standard\nAge is evidence. Each mark should suggest use, storage, repair, ownership, or time rather than serve as indiscriminate decoration.\nProduct Application\nFor printable ephemera, maintain sufficient edge clarity and tonal separation for cutting, layering, and home printing.\n\n[3AE04EB4-BF02-8131-849E-CAA5C7E908F9]\nLighting Intent\nWychcombe lighting should feel observed rather than staged.\nPreferred Conditions\nOvercast daylight through old glass\nSide light across paper and wood grain\nLate-afternoon warmth kept muted\nSmall practical lamps within otherwise natural scenes\nSoft shadow transitions with readable detail\nContinuity Rule\nLighting may create uncertainty, but it must not conceal essential canonical features of a subject.\n\n[3AE04EB4-BF02-81A2-9123-C82C280737AC]\nPurpose\nProvides the mandatory exclusion layer for Wychcombe image generation.\nGovernance Rules\nDo not remove a constraint merely to improve visual drama.\nAdd temporary record-specific exclusions in the asset prompt.\nRevise this module only when a formal visual-style decision changes.\nDo not allow an image model to answer unresolved canon questions through invented visual details.\nPrompt Assembly Position\nPlace this module last.\n\n[SHARED PROMPT]\nasset_role: foundation layer and primary wide-scene carrier for the Volume I Hero sequence materials: aged oak or walnut table; heavy cotton rag and laid papers with subtle deckled edges; leather and cloth bindings; brass tools; cotton or silk ribbon; graphite and iron-gall ink; faded map pigments visual_language: period-believable archival research scene within the Wychcombe world, rendered as an expansive working table rather than a decorative antique still life palette: aged walnut and oak browns, antique ivory, softened sepia, faded map pigments, muted botanical greens, restrained brass warmth, and low-saturation archival neutrals visual_hierarchy: Primary: partially unfolded estate map or garden plan paired with one open journal · Secondary: folded correspondence, ledger or catalog book, and botanical plate · Tertiary: brass dividers, spectacles, ribbon-tied packet, paper weights, bound journals, margin annotations, and indexing marks composition_rule: preserve clear focal hierarchy around the map and open journal, broad connective spacing across the long table, and stretches of table wood, map margin, and blank paper for crop tolerance narrative_tone: expanding understanding, careful comparison, connected histories, quiet responsibility, and the first glimpse of a larger system print_rule: 3600 x 3600 px; 12 x 12 in; 300 DPI minimum; sRGB IEC61966-2.1 master; PNG master plus printable PDF derivative; preserve 0.25 in focal safety area and crop-safe negative space; no hairlines below 0.5 pt; visible text must be approved and intentionally legible or omitted text_rule: visible names, dates, places, map labels, and annotations must be approved, intentionally legible, or omitted; no pseudo-text canon_rule: support approved Wychcombe canon without fixing unapproved geography or introducing new institutions, events, ownership facts, or chronology\n\n[FRONT PROMPT]\nprimary_focal_area: partially unfolded estate map or garden plan paired with one open journal secondary_narrative_cluster: folded correspondence, a ledger or catalog book, and a botanical plate arranged to imply active comparison across sources supporting_objects: brass dividers or compass; reading spectacles; ribbon-tied packet; paper weights; bound journals from different periods; margin annotations; indexing marks view_angle: directly overhead or gently elevated wide table view background_surface: long aged oak or walnut library table with visible but restrained grain and usable quiet areas lighting: natural library daylight or restrained warm non-electrical lamp light, with believable direction and no theatrical effects composition: broad connective research scene rather than an intimate desk still life; preserve clear focal hierarchy around the map and journal, with stretches of table wood, map margin, and blank paper for crop tolerance output_type: 12 x 12 inch square Hero Paper at 300 DPI with crop-safe composition and print-ready derivatives\n\n[NEGATIVE PROMPT]\nexclude modern printed maps, contemporary typography, office clips, ballpoint pens, plastic rulers, electrical lamps, modern bindings, generic fantasy props, malformed books or tools, cluttered composition, dramatic mystery lighting, heavy darkness, magical effects, unapproved names, dates, places, institutions, events, ownership facts, fixed geography, pseudo-text, repeated objects, hard digital texture, and obvious AI artifacts\n\n[CANON POLICY]\nCanon Dependency: Supports Canon\nCanon Record: Living Memory (Accepted)\n\n[PRINT AND OUTPUT REQUIREMENTS]\nReview Criteria: Reads as a working research table rather than a staged library still life; map, journal, and correspondence details remain period-appropriate; clear focal hierarchy and crop tolerance; no modern bindings, printed typography, electrical lamps, ballpoint pens, office supplies, or generic fantasy props; distinct from the Curator's Desk while feeling part of the same world; no obvious AI artifacts.","prompt_hash":"45848369fb3ff31a3c6c93ed50dfbe72c4a4fb0cd5dd0c0037cc8354e6f51ac6","compiled_prompt_status":"Compiled","visual_asset_notion_id":null,"asset_id":"WS-WYC-V01-HP3AE04E-MASTER","asset_version":"v001","provider":null,"model_name":null,"model_version":null,"generation_settings":null,"seed":null,"provider_request_id":null,"cost_usd":null,"drive_file_id":null,"drive_folder_id":null,"drive_url":null,"daybook_asset_id":null,"errors":null,"warnings":[],"failed_stage":null,"error_code":null,"resolved_source_ids":{"world":"3ae04eb4-bf02-81c0-818e-d1517d790e04","world_name":"Wychcombe","style_guide":"3af04eb4-bf02-8192-ab45-f5142ce79ad8","canon_records":["3ae04eb4-bf02-81e7-acda-fe513f6adb2e"],"component_spec":"3ae04eb4-bf02-8113-80c1-e013ee28eafe","prompt_modules":["3ae04eb4-bf02-81ad-a281-d353bd9a704e","3ae04eb4-bf02-81af-a302-c9a5ef68876e","3ae04eb4-bf02-81b0-ad10-ead1dadd761b","3ae04eb4-bf02-8131-849e-caa5c7e908f9","3ae04eb4-bf02-81a2-9123-c82c280737ac"],"production_spec":"3ae04eb4-bf02-8121-9ab4-fe5c91a6d41a","world_notion_id":"3ae04eb4-bf02-81c0-818e-d1517d790e04"},"retry_count":0,"initiated_by":"7e27c46a-7464-491a-9eb0-f23f52cc117b","started_at":"2026-08-05T01:32:53.681Z","completed_at":"2026-08-05T01:32:59.056Z","notion_retries":null,"compiled_sections":null,"generated_filename":null,"notion_upload_id":null},{"id":"a5bb12e7-06a2-49e7-b064-dab8931ede29","production_spec_id":"3ae04eb4-bf02-8121-9ab4-fe5c91a6d41a","operation":"validate_and_compile","status":"compiled","dry_run":true,"payload_version":"PP-2.0","compiled_prompt":"[WORLD AND COLLECTION CONTEXT]\nWorld: Wychcombe\n\n[STYLE SYSTEM]\nSource\nThis record represents the approved working standard documented in Volume I Visual Language: The Curator's Desk.\nGoverning scope\nApplies to all Volume I Hero Papers, coordinating papers, journal cards, ephemera, pockets, envelopes, tags, labels, hidden discoveries, packaging, and marketing assets unless a documented design decision states otherwise.\nIntegrity rule\nProduction Specification records must reference this Visual Style Guide through the Style Guide relation. Prompt modules such as Curator's Desk belong only in the Prompt Modules relation.\n\n[COMPONENT REQUIREMENTS]\nApproved Flagship Component Standard\n1. Governing Principle\nA Hero Paper is the flagship visual anchor of a volume or major section. It should communicate the world, emotional tone, setting, season, and narrative promise at a glance while remaining useful as a printable journal paper.\nIt must not become a conventional poster, book cover, or isolated illustration that loses practical value when cropped, folded, layered, or partially obscured.\n2. Purpose\nHero Papers:\nEstablish the narrative identity of a volume\nIntroduce the dominant emotional tone\nReveal the collection's most important setting, artifact, or visual motif\nGuide coordinating paper, journal card, and ephemera development\nCreate the primary visual used in product previews and collection navigation\nProvide a memorable entry point into the world\n3. Narrative Role\nA Hero Paper is the first visual sentence of the volume.\nIt should answer, without explanatory text:\nWhere are we?\nWhat season or time are we experiencing?\nWhat emotional atmosphere governs this volume?\nWhich objects, places, or botanical elements matter here?\nWhat story might be waiting just outside the frame?\nThe paper should imply narrative movement without resolving the story.\n4. Asset Intent\nPrimary intents:\nFoundation\nStorytelling\nPossible secondary intents:\nBotanical Study\nCurator Collection\nInteractive\nLayering\nMixed Media\nThe Hero Paper must remain a practical asset even when its primary intent is storytelling.\n5. Narrative Density\nThe default is Suggestive.\nEvidence-Rich Hero Papers require stricter canon and legibility review.\n6. Reuse Level\nThe default is Volume.\nA Hero Paper may be classified as:\nSeries when it represents the entire series rather than one volume\nVolume when it is the flagship artwork of one volume\nUnique when reuse would diminish a singular narrative reveal or canon artifact\nHero Papers should rarely be Universal or World-level assets because they are intended to provide distinctive identity.\n7. Canon Dependency\nThe default is Supports Canon.\nUse:\nNone only for purely atmospheric, non-specific scene work\nSupports Canon when the Hero Paper reinforces approved setting and lore\nCanon Reference when it directly depicts a governed person, place, artifact, institution, emblem, or event\nCanon Defining when it introduces a new visual truth that becomes binding for future work\nCanon Defining Hero Papers require explicit approval before release.\n8. Required Creative Definition\nBefore prompt assembly, each Hero Paper production item must define:\nScene title\nEmotional tone\nFocal subject\nSupporting motifs\nForeground elements\nMiddle-ground elements\nBackground environment\nSeason\nTime of day or lighting condition\nWeather or atmospheric condition, when relevant\nNarrative Density\nAsset Intent\nReuse Level\nCanon Dependency\nRelationship to companion papers and ephemera\nA Hero Paper must not enter generation with only a broad theme.\n9. Composition Model\nHero Papers should use intentional visual hierarchy.\nPrimary Focal Subject\nOne dominant visual idea should anchor the paper. This may be:\nA place\nA desk or room\nA greenhouse\nA garden gate\nA botanical specimen arrangement\nA meaningful object\nA view into a landscape\nA canon-linked artifact\nSupporting Motifs\nSecondary details should deepen the scene without competing with the focal subject.\nDepth\nWhen scene-based, use:\nForeground for tactile or framing elements\nMiddle ground for the focal narrative subject\nBackground for environmental context and atmosphere\nNegative Space\nPreserve areas where the paper can be written on, layered over, cropped, or folded without destroying the image's meaning.\n10. Crop and Construction Tolerance\nA Hero Paper must remain usable when:\nCropped to 8.5 × 11 or A4\nCropped to A5 or half-page\nFolded into signatures\nPartially covered by pockets or ephemera\nUsed behind journal cards\nTrimmed for covers or marketing panels\nIrreplaceable focal details should remain at least 0.25 inch from the edge unless edge placement is intentional and approved.\n11. Visual Goals\nEvery Hero Paper should demonstrate:\nImmediate recognition of setting or narrative identity\nEmotional clarity\nHistorical and material authenticity\nFlagship-level detail\nControlled visual hierarchy\nAtmospheric depth\nBelievable lighting\nCohesion with the collection palette\nStrong crop tolerance\nPractical usability as journal paper\nNo obvious AI artifacts\nA Hero Paper should be memorable without becoming theatrical or visually noisy.\n12. Prompt Governance\nEvery Hero Paper must link to its governing Prompt Modules and Style Guide.\nThe prompt package should define:\nScene or subject\nEmotional tone\nFocal hierarchy\nForeground, middle ground, and background\nLighting and atmosphere\nMaterials\nEra and world constraints\nBotanical or architectural accuracy requirements\nNarrative Density\nCanon Dependency\nPrint and crop requirements\nNegative constraints\nWorld-specific and volume-specific prompt modules are mandatory for Volume or Unique reuse classifications.\n13. Master Production Standard\nPixel dimensions are the controlling quality requirement. The file must also report 300 DPI.\n14. Asset Family\nEvery approved Hero Paper requires:\nDerived crops inherit the master asset's provenance and governance.\n15. Companion Asset Relationship\nA Hero Paper should inform, but not be duplicated by:\nCoordinating Decorative Papers\nJournal Cards\nEphemera Sheets\nPockets\nEnvelopes\nTags\nLabels\nMarketing Assets\nCompanion assets may reuse palette, texture, motifs, and environmental cues. They should not merely shrink or repeat the Hero Paper unless the derivative use is intentional and documented.\n16. Botanical and Architectural Accuracy\nWhen present:\nBotanicals must be plausible for the governed location, season, and setting\nArchitecture must be structurally believable and consistent with the world\nFurniture, tools, fixtures, labels, signage, and household objects must match the era and narrative context\nWritten text must be legible, intentional, or omitted\nNo modern objects may appear unless specifically governed\nAccuracy review should focus on what is visible enough to become a lasting visual fact.\n17. Lighting and Atmosphere\nLighting should support the emotional tone and preserve print clarity.\nAvoid:\nExcessive darkness\nCrushed shadows\nBlown highlights\nUnmotivated glow\nConflicting light sources\nCinematic effects that make the paper unusable as a printed surface\nAtmosphere should deepen the world without obscuring focal information.\n18. Daybook Contract\nDaybook must be able to retrieve or derive:\nComponent Type\nCollection, World, Series, and Volume\nScene title\nEmotional tone\nFocal subject\nSupporting motifs\nSeason and time of day\nAsset Intent\nNarrative Density\nReuse Level\nCanon Dependency\nPrompt version and linked Prompt Modules\nStyle Guide version\nMaster dimensions and DPI\nOutput formats\nFilename and revision\nApproval status\nAsset-family members\nCompanion-asset relationships\nCanon and production-pipeline relationships\nDaybook must not publish a Hero Paper with unresolved Canon Defining status, incomplete metadata, or missing required derivatives.\n19. Filename Standard\n{WORLD}_{VOLUME}_HP_{SEQ}_Master.png\n{WORLD}_{VOLUME}_HP_{SEQ}_Kit.png\n{WORLD}_{VOLUME}_HP_{SEQ}_Print.pdf\n{WORLD}_{VOLUME}_HP_{SEQ}_Preview.jpg\n{WORLD}_{VOLUME}_HP_{SEQ}_Thumb.jpg\nExample:\nWC_V01_HP_001_Master.png\n20. Quality Assurance Checklist\nCreative Review\nVisual Review\nProduction Review\nGovernance Review\n21. Definition of Done\nA Hero Paper is complete only when:\nThe flagship master is approved\nNarrative identity and emotional tone are clear\nRequired companion direction is documented\nAll required derivatives are generated\nPrint QA is passed\nPrompt, Style Guide, Canon, Visual Asset, and Pipeline relationships are complete where applicable\nFilename and metadata validate successfully\nDaybook can publish the asset without manual correction or an unresolved creative decision\n22. Version and Decision Control\nAll changes to this specification require:\nA new version number\nA decision summary\nA reason for change\nAn impact assessment\nApproval by the designated authority\nThis specification inherits the governance structure established by CS-001 while adding Hero Paper requirements for narrative identity, focal hierarchy, companion-asset direction, and canon-sensitive scene composition.\n\n[3AE04EB4-BF02-81AD-A281-D353BD9A704E]\nPurpose\nProvides the stable visual DNA for Wychcombe across canon references, environments, characters, artifacts, and product artwork.\nPrompt Assembly Position\nPlace this module first, before subject and composition instructions.\nContinuity Rules\nAtmosphere is restrained, not melodramatic.\nHistorical detail must feel inhabited and specific.\nMystery is communicated through evidence, omission, and arrangement.\nAvoid visual spectacle that overwhelms narrative meaning.\nAge should appear through use, repair, fading, patina, and custody rather than indiscriminate damage.\n\n[3AE04EB4-BF02-81AF-A302-C9A5EF68876E]\nPurpose\nDefines the recurring visual system for curated evidence layouts associated with the Wychcombe Journal Collection.\nRequired Behaviors\nObjects must imply relationships.\nAt least one focal object should anchor the composition.\nSupporting items should reinforce a clue, person, place, or time period.\nSurfaces should show realistic handling and age.\nNegative space should remain available for readability and product use.\nRecommended Uses\nVolume mood boards\nEphemera collections\nArtifact presentation\nProduct covers and marketing crops\nCanon-reference still lifes\n\n[3AE04EB4-BF02-81B0-AD10-EAD1DADD761B]\nMaterial Standard\nMaterials should remain visually distinct. Paper should not resemble leather; tarnished brass should not resemble painted gold; faded ink should remain legible where the story requires it.\nAging Standard\nAge is evidence. Each mark should suggest use, storage, repair, ownership, or time rather than serve as indiscriminate decoration.\nProduct Application\nFor printable ephemera, maintain sufficient edge clarity and tonal separation for cutting, layering, and home printing.\n\n[3AE04EB4-BF02-8131-849E-CAA5C7E908F9]\nLighting Intent\nWychcombe lighting should feel observed rather than staged.\nPreferred Conditions\nOvercast daylight through old glass\nSide light across paper and wood grain\nLate-afternoon warmth kept muted\nSmall practical lamps within otherwise natural scenes\nSoft shadow transitions with readable detail\nContinuity Rule\nLighting may create uncertainty, but it must not conceal essential canonical features of a subject.\n\n[3AE04EB4-BF02-81A2-9123-C82C280737AC]\nPurpose\nProvides the mandatory exclusion layer for Wychcombe image generation.\nGovernance Rules\nDo not remove a constraint merely to improve visual drama.\nAdd temporary record-specific exclusions in the asset prompt.\nRevise this module only when a formal visual-style decision changes.\nDo not allow an image model to answer unresolved canon questions through invented visual details.\nPrompt Assembly Position\nPlace this module last.\n\n[SHARED PROMPT]\nasset_role: foundation layer and primary wide-scene carrier for the Volume I Hero sequence materials: aged oak or walnut table; heavy cotton rag and laid papers with subtle deckled edges; leather and cloth bindings; brass tools; cotton or silk ribbon; graphite and iron-gall ink; faded map pigments visual_language: period-believable archival research scene within the Wychcombe world, rendered as an expansive working table rather than a decorative antique still life palette: aged walnut and oak browns, antique ivory, softened sepia, faded map pigments, muted botanical greens, restrained brass warmth, and low-saturation archival neutrals visual_hierarchy: Primary: partially unfolded estate map or garden plan paired with one open journal · Secondary: folded correspondence, ledger or catalog book, and botanical plate · Tertiary: brass dividers, spectacles, ribbon-tied packet, paper weights, bound journals, margin annotations, and indexing marks composition_rule: preserve clear focal hierarchy around the map and open journal, broad connective spacing across the long table, and stretches of table wood, map margin, and blank paper for crop tolerance narrative_tone: expanding understanding, careful comparison, connected histories, quiet responsibility, and the first glimpse of a larger system print_rule: 3600 x 3600 px; 12 x 12 in; 300 DPI minimum; sRGB IEC61966-2.1 master; PNG master plus printable PDF derivative; preserve 0.25 in focal safety area and crop-safe negative space; no hairlines below 0.5 pt; visible text must be approved and intentionally legible or omitted text_rule: visible names, dates, places, map labels, and annotations must be approved, intentionally legible, or omitted; no pseudo-text canon_rule: support approved Wychcombe canon without fixing unapproved geography or introducing new institutions, events, ownership facts, or chronology\n\n[FRONT PROMPT]\nprimary_focal_area: partially unfolded estate map or garden plan paired with one open journal secondary_narrative_cluster: folded correspondence, a ledger or catalog book, and a botanical plate arranged to imply active comparison across sources supporting_objects: brass dividers or compass; reading spectacles; ribbon-tied packet; paper weights; bound journals from different periods; margin annotations; indexing marks view_angle: directly overhead or gently elevated wide table view background_surface: long aged oak or walnut library table with visible but restrained grain and usable quiet areas lighting: natural library daylight or restrained warm non-electrical lamp light, with believable direction and no theatrical effects composition: broad connective research scene rather than an intimate desk still life; preserve clear focal hierarchy around the map and journal, with stretches of table wood, map margin, and blank paper for crop tolerance output_type: 12 x 12 inch square Hero Paper at 300 DPI with crop-safe composition and print-ready derivatives\n\n[NEGATIVE PROMPT]\nexclude modern printed maps, contemporary typography, office clips, ballpoint pens, plastic rulers, electrical lamps, modern bindings, generic fantasy props, malformed books or tools, cluttered composition, dramatic mystery lighting, heavy darkness, magical effects, unapproved names, dates, places, institutions, events, ownership facts, fixed geography, pseudo-text, repeated objects, hard digital texture, and obvious AI artifacts\n\n[CANON POLICY]\nCanon Dependency: Supports Canon\nCanon Record: Living Memory (Accepted)\n\n[PRINT AND OUTPUT REQUIREMENTS]\nReview Criteria: Reads as a working research table rather than a staged library still life; map, journal, and correspondence details remain period-appropriate; clear focal hierarchy and crop tolerance; no modern bindings, printed typography, electrical lamps, ballpoint pens, office supplies, or generic fantasy props; distinct from the Curator's Desk while feeling part of the same world; no obvious AI artifacts.","prompt_hash":"45848369fb3ff31a3c6c93ed50dfbe72c4a4fb0cd5dd0c0037cc8354e6f51ac6","compiled_prompt_status":"Compiled","visual_asset_notion_id":null,"asset_id":"WS