cter across paper, wood, brass, glass, ink, botanicals, textiles, terracotta, twine, wax, and related period-appropriate materials. Render Volume I materials as tactile, aged through plausible use, and integrated into the illustrated archival environment. Materials should show restrained variation, handling, age, and natural imperfection rather than artificial distressing. Paper: fine visible fiber and subtle tooth antique ivory through foxed cream restrained foxing, edge wear, folds, handling darkening, faint stains, and ink absorption where appropriate. Paper must remain usable and believable Wood: walnut and other period-appropriate woods with visible illustrated grain, softened edges, matte surfaces, handling wear, and restrained age variation. Brass: weathered brass with soft patina, warm restrained highlights, handling variation, and age appropriate to frequently used tools and fittings. Glass: old greenhouse, bottle, lens, or cabinet glass with softened transparency, subtle clouding, slight waviness, condensation when contextually appropriate, and restrained reflections. Ink and graphite: iron-gall or period-plausible ink with natural variation, slight fading, absorption into paper, fine linework, and occasional handling evidence. Graphite should remain soft, matte, and visibly drawn. Botanicals: pressed specimens, cuttings, seed heads, ferns, herbs, and botanical studies should show believable fibers, veins, flattening, drying, curling, and natural discoloration. Terracotta, twine, ribbon, linen, wax, and related materials: preserve recognizable material character through restrained texture, wear, pigment variation, fibers, impressions, and handling. Materials should feel accumulated through work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, and ordinary use. Tactile illustrated materials with restrained use-based aging: fine-fiber antique paper, worn matte walnut, softly patinated brass, slightly clouded period glass, absorbed and faded ink, soft graphite, naturally dried botanicals, weathered terracotta, fibrous twine and linen, and handled wax. Preserve believable material differences. Apply to Volume I visual components containing illustrated physical materials, surfaces, botanicals, archival objects, or paper-based elements. Material treatment should scale appropriately to the component rather than requiring every listed material to appear. Compatible with None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module governs the visual behavior of materials and aging rather than canon facts. Inject in the Style System section after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific content. The Style Lock determines the rendering medium Material World determines how physical materials appear within that medium. Component Specs and Production Specs determine which materials and objects are actually required. Materials are visually distinct and immediately recognizable: paper reads as fibrous paper, wood as wood, brass as aged metal, glass as glass, and botanicals as preserved or living plant material. Aging appears plausible and use-based, with restrained foxing, handling wear, softened edges, patina, fading, fibers, folds, and natural discoloration where appropriate. age and wear appear accumulated through handling, storage, botanical study, correspondence, and archival work. The overall material palette feels cohesive, tactile, muted, and period-plausible while preserving meaningful differences between individual materials. Uniform grunge, heavy staining, burned edges, excessive tearing, artificial aging, or distressed effects applied indiscriminately across the asset. Glossy, plastic, chrome-like, synthetic, or digitally polished surfaces inconsistent with the Volume I material language. Materials lose their physical identity, such as brass appearing plastic, wood appearing digitally smooth, paper resembling flat beige texture, or botanicals appearing artificial. Pair with Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for lighting and environmental mood, and with Negative Space or Technical Constraint modules where applicable. Material World governs how specified materials appear Component Specs and Production Specs control required materials, objects, composition, format, and production behavior. Canon and Production Spec requirements take precedence when they establish a specific object's material or condition. When instructions conflict, preserve this order: Canon and explicit Production Spec facts → Component production requirements → Illustrated Archival Style Lock → Material World → Atmospheric refinements. PROMPT MODULE: Period & Digital Exclusions — Volume I Type: Technical Constraint Injection point: negative_prompt Use across Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Volume I visual components. The result must remain materially plausible, illustrated, restrained, and consistent with the governed Volume I visual language. Apply to all Volume I visual components governed by the illustrated archival language. This module suppresses incompatible visual treatments and anachronisms rather than supplying canon content. Inject into the negative_prompt after the positive Style System instructions have established the intended illustrated archival treatment. Apply alongside Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I as its exclusion guardrail. Maintain these exclusions throughout generation, while allowing explicit canon, Production Spec, and Component Spec requirements to take precedence where they intentionally require an otherwise excluded element. The image remains convincingly within the illustrated Victorian archival language, with period-plausible objects, materials, typography, lighting, and surface treatment. Aging and historical character feel restrained and materially plausible rather than relying on generic vintage effects. Any visible text, labels, marks, or institutional identifiers appear intentional and supported by the Production Spec or linked canon rather than invented decorative pseudo-history. Modern or anachronistic objects or materials such as plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, contemporary packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic office supplies, or modern signage unless explicitly required. Neon or synthetic color, chrome-like reflections, artificial glow, dramatic fog, god rays, extreme cinematic contrast, or other theatrical effects. Generic antique-shop clutter, faux-vintage craft styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, steampunk imagery, excessive grunge, burned edges, or indiscriminate distressing. Pseudo-text, invented historical branding, unsupported institutional marks, fake labels, or other visual information that could be mistaken for canon. Pair directly with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I. The Style Lock establishes the required positive rendering language this module suppresses incompatible alternatives. When an otherwise excluded element is intentionally required, the explicit requirement takes precedence. This module controls exclusions only. PROMPT MODULE: Quiet Composition — Volume I Type: Negative Space Apply to Volume I visual Production Specs where composition must preserve visual quiet, usable negative space, restrained density, and clear hierarchy. Use across Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers, with the exact amount and location of quiet space determined by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. Preserve quiet visual space as an intentional part of the composition rather than treating empty areas as unfinished. Establish a clear hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate, limited supporting clusters, and sufficient breathing room between visual elements. Quiet space may take the form of open paper, restrained wood grain, low-density linen or material texture, pale margins, softly illuminated glass, uncluttered writing areas, or other contextually appropriate surfaces. Maintain visual balance rather than symmetry for its own sake. Allow areas of lower detail to support more active areas and preserve the intended function of the component. When the Production Spec defines a writing-space percentage or usability requirement, treat that requirement as mandatory. The quiet area must remain genuinely usable rather than merely lighter in color while still crowded with detail. Preserve intentional visual quiet and usable negative space. One clear focal area where appropriate, restrained supporting clusters, low-to-moderate density, and genuine breathing room. Respect writing-space, cut, trim, binding, and safe-area requirements. Quiet space should feel naturally integrated into the illustrated environment. Apply wherever Volume I assets need restrained visual density, usable negative space, clear hierarchy, and protection against overfilling. The exact amount and placement of quiet space must follow the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. This module governs composition and visual breathing room only. nject in the Style System after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and Material World — Volume I, and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific composition/content instructions. Treat Production Spec writing-space, usability, trim, cut, and safe-area requirements as mandatory. The composition has a clear visual hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate and restrained supporting elements. Meaningful areas of genuine visual quiet remain open and usable rather than being filled with decoration, text, botanicals, objects, or heavy texture. Active and quiet areas feel intentionally balanced, allowing the viewer’s eye to rest and move naturally through the composition. Any writing-space percentage or functional area required by the Production Spec remains visibly