od, brass as aged metal, glass as glass, and botanicals as preserved or living plant material. Aging appears plausible and use-based, with restrained foxing, handling wear, softened edges, patina, fading, fibers, folds, and natural discoloration where appropriate. age and wear appear accumulated through handling, storage, botanical study, correspondence, and archival work. The overall material palette feels cohesive, tactile, muted, and period-plausible while preserving meaningful differences between individual materials. Uniform grunge, heavy staining, burned edges, excessive tearing, artificial aging, or distressed effects applied indiscriminately across the asset. Glossy, plastic, chrome-like, synthetic, or digitally polished surfaces inconsistent with the Volume I material language. Materials lose their physical identity, such as brass appearing plastic, wood appearing digitally smooth, paper resembling flat beige texture, or botanicals appearing artificial. Pair with Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for lighting and environmental mood, and with Negative Space or Technical Constraint modules where applicable. Material World governs how specified materials appear Component Specs and Production Specs control required materials, objects, composition, format, and production behavior. Canon and Production Spec requirements take precedence when they establish a specific object's material or condition. When instructions conflict, preserve this order: Canon and explicit Production Spec facts → Component production requirements → Illustrated Archival Style Lock → Material World → Atmospheric refinements. PROMPT MODULE: Period & Digital Exclusions — Volume I Type: Technical Constraint Injection point: negative_prompt Use across Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Volume I visual components. The result must remain materially plausible, illustrated, restrained, and consistent with the governed Volume I visual language. Apply to all Volume I visual components governed by the illustrated archival language. This module suppresses incompatible visual treatments and anachronisms rather than supplying canon content. Inject into the negative_prompt after the positive Style System instructions have established the intended illustrated archival treatment. Apply alongside Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I as its exclusion guardrail. Maintain these exclusions throughout generation, while allowing explicit canon, Production Spec, and Component Spec requirements to take precedence where they intentionally require an otherwise excluded element. The image remains convincingly within the illustrated Victorian archival language, with period-plausible objects, materials, typography, lighting, and surface treatment. Aging and historical character feel restrained and materially plausible rather than relying on generic vintage effects. Any visible text, labels, marks, or institutional identifiers appear intentional and supported by the Production Spec or linked canon rather than invented decorative pseudo-history. Modern or anachronistic objects or materials such as plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, contemporary packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic office supplies, or modern signage unless explicitly required. Neon or synthetic color, chrome-like reflections, artificial glow, dramatic fog, god rays, extreme cinematic contrast, or other theatrical effects. Generic antique-shop clutter, faux-vintage craft styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, steampunk imagery, excessive grunge, burned edges, or indiscriminate distressing. Pseudo-text, invented historical branding, unsupported institutional marks, fake labels, or other visual information that could be mistaken for canon. Pair directly with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I. The Style Lock establishes the required positive rendering language this module suppresses incompatible alternatives. When an otherwise excluded element is intentionally required, the explicit requirement takes precedence. This module controls exclusions only. PROMPT MODULE: Quiet Composition — Volume I Type: Negative Space Apply to Volume I visual Production Specs where composition must preserve visual quiet, usable negative space, restrained density, and clear hierarchy. Use across Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers, with the exact amount and location of quiet space determined by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. Preserve quiet visual space as an intentional part of the composition rather than treating empty areas as unfinished. Establish a clear hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate, limited supporting clusters, and sufficient breathing room between visual elements. Quiet space may take the form of open paper, restrained wood grain, low-density linen or material texture, pale margins, softly illuminated glass, uncluttered writing areas, or other contextually appropriate surfaces. Maintain visual balance rather than symmetry for its own sake. Allow areas of lower detail to support more active areas and preserve the intended function of the component. When the Production Spec defines a writing-space percentage or usability requirement, treat that requirement as mandatory. The quiet area must remain genuinely usable rather than merely lighter in color while still crowded with detail. Preserve intentional visual quiet and usable negative space. One clear focal area where appropriate, restrained supporting clusters, low-to-moderate density, and genuine breathing room. Respect writing-space, cut, trim, binding, and safe-area requirements. Quiet space should feel naturally integrated into the illustrated environment. Apply wherever Volume I assets need restrained visual density, usable negative space, clear hierarchy, and protection against overfilling. The exact amount and placement of quiet space must follow the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. This module governs composition and visual breathing room only. nject in the Style System after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and Material World — Volume I, and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific composition/content instructions. Treat Production Spec writing-space, usability, trim, cut, and safe-area requirements as mandatory. The composition has a clear visual hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate and restrained supporting elements. Meaningful areas of genuine visual quiet remain open and usable rather than being filled with decoration, text, botanicals, objects, or heavy texture. Active and quiet areas feel intentionally balanced, allowing the viewer’s eye to rest and move naturally through the composition. Any writing-space percentage or functional area required by the Production Spec remains visibly usable and substantially unobstructed. Negative space feels integrated into the illustrated environment through paper, subtle material texture, restrained surface detail, or other contextually appropriate treatment rather than appearing artificially blank. The composition fills nearly every available area with objects, botanicals, text, marks, aging, patterns, or decorative detail. Required writing or functional space is visually occupied, heavily textured, interrupted, or reduced below the Production Spec requirement. Too many competing focal areas, clusters, or high-detail elements create visual noise or unclear hierarchy. Negative space appears as an unnatural empty hole, digitally erased region, hard-edged blank area, or disconnected background. Pair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I for rendering consistency, Material World — Volume I for believable low-density surfaces and textures, and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for environmental mood. Quiet Composition governs visual density, hierarchy, breathing room, and usability. Component Specs and Production Specs take precedence for explicit writing-space percentages, safe zones, trim areas, orientation, layout requirements, and required content. Quiet Composition should help satisfy those requirements rather than replace them. Instead, simplify secondary decoration, reduce density, consolidate clusters, or reposition nonessential elements. When instructions conflict, preserve this order: required canon/content → production and component requirements → required usability/negative space → style and material refinements → optional decorative detail.\nWychcombe is a Victorian estate given over to the quiet work of preservation — a place where botanical specimens are pressed and catalogued, where archives accumulate in climate-controlled stillness, and where scholarship proceeds at the pace of careful hands. The estate comprises a main house of honeyed stone, a sprawling greenhouse complex with ironwork gone soft with verdigris, outbuildings converted to workrooms and storage, and grounds that blur the line between curated garden and managed wilderness. Gravel paths, the creak of wooden drawers, the scratch of nib on paper. Time moves differently here: seasons matter more than hours, and the work of a single afternoon might continue research begun three generations prior. Visually, Wychcombe exists in a palette of **aged naturals** — ivory paper darkened to cream, green in every muted variation from moss to fern, the warm brown of walnut cases and sepia ink, brass worn to a soft glow. Color is restrained when it appears (a terracotta pot, a burgundy wax seal), it carries weight. This is a world of **things that endure** and the people who tend them — where meaning accumulates through proximity, repetition, and care.\n| Paper Fine fiber, subtle tooth, restrained foxing, use-based wear | Wood Visible grain, soft edge wear, matte finish | Brass Soft patina, warm highlights | Glass Clouding, age, condensation, slight waviness | Botanicals Realistic fibers, veins, flattening, and discoloration |\n\n[NEGATIVE AND WRITING SPACE]\nPreserve 0% usable visual space.\n\n[CANON CONSTRAINTS]\nCanon dependency: Supports Canon Canon constraint source only do not render its title: The Curators (accepted) The Curators are a Wychcombe institution or role-group responsible for preserving, interpreting, arranging, or controlling access to records and objects. Withholding\n\n[TECHNICAL AND OUTPUT REQUIREMENTS]\nOrientation: square\nFront/Back Style: single-sided\n\n[NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS / NEGATIVE PROMPT]\nphotograph, photography, photorealistic, hyperrealistic, DSLR still life, photographic lighting, photographic depth of field, physically based rendering, 3D render, cinematic rendering, glossy digital rendering, commercial product photography, museum still-life photography, razor-sharp photographic textures, ** harsh spotlights, magical glow, god rays, fog, extreme chiaroscuro, cinematic contrast, blue digital shadows, geometric sans, rounded fonts, any typeface that reads as modern in primary roles, Sentences structured and complex but never opaque, ### Words & Phrases to Avoid, fantasy plants, malformed tools, heavy sepia, generic antique-shop clutter, random grunge, any visual treatment that breaks the illustrated Victorian archival system, ### Negative Constraints (Do Not), Its job is to provide a strong coordinated surface for journaling and layering without carrying a major narrative scene, It supports the collection’s visual language without functioning as a primary narrative scene, Preserve sufficient visual quiet so other journal elements can be layered over the paper without excessive competition, composition that reads as a Hero Paper, illustrated scene, poster, single large standalone artwork unless explicitly required, fill every available area with motifs, objects, texture, marks, Large motifs should not dominate the page unless intentionally specified, The design must tolerate common cropping from any edge without destroying its visual logic, placing all meaningful visual interest in a single crop-sensitive location, Essential motifs should not depend upon seeing the entire sheet, assume seamless repetition unless explicitly required, obvious mechanical duplication that makes illustrated elements appear digitally stamped, uniform grunge, excessive staining, burned edges, artificial noise, distress applied equally across the entire sheet, require the user to preserve the entire sheet for the design to make sense, The paper should coordinate with more narrative components without competing with them for attention, invent names, dates, correspondence, locations, botanical identifications, quotations, signatures, document contents, historical claims, other factual-looking text, When text-like texture is desired without governed wording, use non-readable handwriting traces, faded partial marks, abstract ink forms, blank structures, other non-semantic treatments, Decorative use of canon material must not accidentally imply new facts or duplicate unique narrative evidence, effects dependent upon screen luminosity, extreme contrast, details too fine to reproduce reliably in print, This Component Spec does not define the individual paper’s motif, collection-specific palette, narrative theme, botanical subject, recurring objects, readable wording, canon content, decorative density, A Production Specification may refine Decorative Paper for a particular asset but should not silently redefine the fundamental production behavior of the component, theatrical gothic atmosphere, fantasy, horror, luxury-stationery polish, cinematic lighting, glossy digital surfaces, modern materials, This module reinforces the approved Volume I visual atmosphere and collection identity but should not introduce, define, alter canon facts, characters, places, events, discoveries, More specific approved modules and Production Specification requirements take precedence where they add detail without contradicting the governing Style Guide, Lighting is soft and diffused, suggesting greenhouse daylight, library daylight, restrained period lamplight without dramatic effects, This module establishes the atmospheric baseline and should not introduce canon facts or determine component-specific content, More specific component modules may refine composition, botanicals, typography, functional requirements without contradicting the Style Guide, Preserve plausible material identity without allowing realism to override the medium, glossy digital art, cinematic concept art, the output fails this module, use for non-visual outputs or components intentionally governed by a different rendering style, It must not introduce, alter, interpret, override canon, Later modules may refine atmosphere, materials, composition, subject matter but must not override this rendering requirement, object appears pasted in, photographed, rendered in a conflicting medium, This module reinforces its illustrated rendering requirement and should not introduce independent subject matter, canon, production specifications, This module governs material appearance, aging, physical character, not component layout, subject matter, uniformly grungy, burned, heavily damaged, artificially distressed, glossy furniture finishes or exaggerated rustic damage, mirror polish, chrome-like reflections, bright metallic CGI effects, plastic-looking foliage, unnaturally perfect specimens, glossy surfaces, chrome reflections, plastic foliage, synthetic textures, excessive distressing, generic vintage effects, It must not introduce specific objects, people, inscriptions, historical claims that are not supplied by the Production Spec or linked canon, allow material instructions to override canon, print requirements, component-specific constraints, Surfaces show natural variation without excessive distressing, it does not determine which objects or materials must be present, Material World may enrich that material visually but must not change its identity, provenance, age, meaning, canon significance, it does not create new canon facts, historical events, A cracked binding, erased notation, moved object, faded label, repeated repair, pressed specimen, empty drawer, mismatched date, unusual placement may carry narrative weight without announcing its importance, clues that appear solely because the story requires them, Preserve ambiguity when canon does not justify certainty, resolve uncertainty by inventing facts, This module must never invent a discovery, historical fact, named person, date, location, relationship, document content, ownership claim, object provenance, invent discoveries or factual canon to create atmosphere, purely abstract decorative assets may not require it, It may also accompany None when the asset needs Wychcombe’s general evidence logic without referencing specific facts, This module must never supply missing canon, This module governs how those facts are expressed as evidence and narrative context, not what the facts are, The asset distinguishes between established information and suggestion, leaving uncertainty intact where canon does not provide an answer, A restrained number of meaningful details rewards close observation without turning every object into a clue, It does not control rendering style, palette, material appearance, technical production requirements, component format, allow Atmospheric Grounding or visual styling modules to turn neutral objects into implied clues merely for mood, Conversely, when approved canon establishes meaningful evidence, visual modules should support its presentation without obscuring or sensationalizing it, visual elements and rendering treatments that break the illustrated Victorian archival system, glossy 3D materials, game-rendering aesthetics, polished digital illustration that reads as synthetic rather than tactile and archival, modern or anachronistic materials and objects including plastics, ballpoint pens, contemporary electrical devices, modern packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic ribbon, modern office supplies, contemporary signage, digital screens, modern typography, other clearly post-period visual language unless explicitly required by the Production Spec, clean digital white, neon color, bright synthetic saturation, mirror-polished metal, hard digital gradients, artificial glow, fog effects, theatrical lighting, any treatment that turns quiet archival imagery into spectacle, use generic antique-shop clutter, luxury stationery styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, horror aesthetics, steampunk machinery, faux-vintage craft-store decoration, uniform distressing, arbitrary “old-fashioned” embellishment, generate pseudo-text, modern labels, unsupported logos, invented institutional marks, fake historical branding, decorative text that appears factual but lacks an approved source, The goal is not merely to appear old, modern objects, plastics, electrical devices, synthetic materials, theatrical glow, pseudo-text, steampunk, unsupported historical branding, attach only when a Production Spec explicitly requires a different visual period or rendering system, It must never exclude an object, material, inscription, detail explicitly required by accepted canon or the Production Spec, The asset contains no obvious modern, digital, synthetic, anachronistic visual elements unless explicitly required by the Production Spec, Digital generation artifacts are visually suppressed: no glossy CGI surfaces, cinematic effects, synthetic-looking textures, Pair with Material World — Volume I so permitted materials retain plausible age, wear, physical character without drifting into glossy, generic vintage treatments, Pair with Quiet Composition — Volume I and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I without allowing atmospheric effects to introduce cinematic lighting, glow, theatrical contrast, This module must not suppress an element explicitly required by accepted canon, the Production Spec, the Component Spec, It must not invent replacement objects, historical facts, text, branding, canon details, filling every available area with decoration, archival marks, Decorative elements should not migrate into functional writing, cutting, trim, binding, safe areas unless explicitly permitted, Negative space should feel naturally integrated into the visual world, not digitally erased, artificially blank, disconnected from the rest of the composition, fill empty areas with decorative clutter, remove, reinterpret, override canon content, Component-specific composition rules may refine where quiet space appears, but should not eliminate required negative space, Decorative elements intrude into required trim, cut, safe, writing, usability zones without explicit instruction, It does not determine required subjects, exact component dimensions, Required narrative or canon elements must not be removed merely to create more negative space, PASS / FAIL — Does the finished piece read first as a decorative journal paper rather than a Hero Paper, desk scene, ephemera sheet, collection of separate objects?, PASS / FAIL — Does the paper unmistakably belong to Volume I and The Curator’s Desk visual family while maintaining its own quieter, background-oriented function?, PASS / FAIL — Are botanical studies integrated into the paper surface with natural variation in scale, placement, completeness, density rather than forming an obvious mechanical repeat?, PASS / FAIL — Does the composition avoid a dominant central focal scene while still maintaining intentional visual rhythm and areas of greater and lesser detail?, PASS / FAIL — Is approximately 15% of the sheet clearly usable quiet space, with enough additional low-detail area for layering, journal use?, PASS / FAIL — Do paper, ink, graphite, botanical material, other surfaces feel tactile, illustrated, restrained, consistent with the linked Volume I material language?, PASS / FAIL — Do archival marks, catalog structures, specimen traces, annotation elements function as subtle evidence rather than decorative clutter?, PASS / FAIL — Is aging believable and use-based rather than uniform grunge, heavy staining, artificial vintage effects?, synthetic surfaces, modern graphic-design conventions, commercial scrapbook styling?, PASS / FAIL — Is the design free of pseudo-text, prominent gibberish handwriting, unsupported dates, invented documents, unsupported historical claims, other accidental canon?, PASS / FAIL — Are botanical subjects plausible within the established Volume I vocabulary rather than fantasy plants, generic cottagecore florals, decorative bouquets?, PASS / FAIL — Does the finished sheet coordinate naturally with the Curator’s Desk Hero Paper and Ephemera Sheet without copying their compositions?, PASS / FAIL — Does the paper remain visually useful at full 12 × 12 scale and when cropped into smaller journal sections?, PASS / FAIL — Does the final result feel accumulated through observation, preservation, use rather than manufactured as a modern faux-Victorian pattern?, create a central desk scene, assembled collage, obvious grid, modern repeating wallpaper motif, collection of isolated stickers, depict a complete desk scene, room interior, greenhouse scene, open journal as the primary subject, still-life tableau, central narrative vignette","prompt_hash":null,"compiled_prompt_status":"Validation Failed","visual_asset_notion_id":null,"asset_id":null,"asset_version":null,"provider":null,"model_name":null,"model_version":null,"generation_settings":null,"seed":null,"provider_request_id":null,"cost_usd":null,"drive_file_id":null,"drive_folder_id":null,"drive_url":null,"daybook_asset_id":null,"errors":[{"code":"UNAUTHORIZED_READABLE_TEXT_REQUEST","field":"negative_prompt","message":"The canonical negative prompt contains an affirmative or inverted request for unsupported readable content: \"print requirements\".","governing_rule":"Production Specification: Curator’s Desk Botanical Study — Decorative Paper","recommended_action":"Remove the inverted readable-text instruction or authorize its exact wording from an approved source."}],"warnings":[],"failed_stage":null,"error_code":null,"resolved_source_ids":{"world":"wychcombe","volume":"78c13c7f-69bd-4ce3-8a9d-388b2992b527","world_id":"wychcombe","collection":"0d4bfa84-5411-4bfd-a7b6-25fc82d44458","world_name":"Wychcombe","style_guide":"d4fab007-9604-4cec-a100-e63f479b0a12","volume_name":"Volume I: The Curator’s Desk.","canon_records":["2bd24482-d2c0-4ef9-a3a5-bc5fcc853672"],"component_spec":"9246407c-7719-46d5-a65e-0783453be58e","prompt_modules":["e6daf9d9-a32c-457c-ba65-d818fc8de145","a58efef6-17ef-4a5e-b904-e0c7b3982017","ba44d968-3b1e-42b1-bf0c-b60897bb7b91","18327fa6-60f9-46a4-893e-34e4272beb9f","8677a201-ed38-46e3-8e93-bac017585c5c","db1cc97e-363a-4c82-88cd-441d5cf48739"],"collection_name":"Victorian Garden Journals","production_spec":"0c51adac-c7cd-4416-a69c-923feaf058c8","volume_notion_id":"78c13c7f-69bd-4ce3-8a9d-388b2992b527","collection_notion_id":"0d4bfa84-5411-4bfd-a7b6-25fc82d44458"},"retry_count":0,"initiated_by":"7e27c46a-7464-491a-9eb0-f23f52cc117b","started_at":"2026-08-31T13:14:59.671Z","completed_at":"2026-08-31T13:14:59.777Z","notion_retries":null,"compiled_sections":[{"key":"world_and_collection_context","label":"World And Collection Context","source":"World record + Curatorial Evidence Language — Wychcombe","content":"World: Wychcombe\nVolume: Volume I: The Curator’s Desk.\nPROMPT MODULE: Curatorial Evidence Language — Wychcombe\nType: Canon Anchor\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Usage\nApply to Wychcombe Production Specs and generated assets where narrative meaning is communicated through objects, documents, botanical material, correspondence, archives, places, traces of use, omissions, sequence, provenance, or physical evidence. Use this module to make discoveries and storytelling feel native to Wychcombe rather than artificially dramatic. This module governs how evidence communicates meaning; it does not create new canon facts, discoveries, people, dates, places, objects, or historical events.\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nIn Wychcombe, narrative meaning should emerge through evidence, relationship, sequence, place, provenance, material condition, and interpretation rather than arbitrary revelation or decorative mystery.\nTreat objects, documents, botanicals, buildings, correspondence, tools, labels, records, traces of handling, repairs, wear, storage, placement, absence, and repetition as potential evidence. Their significance should come from how they relate to people, places, time, and other evidence rather than from theatrical presentation.\nFavor the curator’s method: observe first, infer carefully, preserve uncertainty where evidence is incomplete, and distinguish what is known from what is suggested. A cracked binding, erased notation, moved object, faded label, repeated repair, pressed specimen, empty drawer, mismatched date, or unusual placement may carry narrative weight without announcing its importance.\nDiscoveries should feel recoverable because evidence existed before the viewer encountered it. Avoid clues that appear solely because the story requires them. Evidence should have plausible provenance, physical context, and a reason for surviving.\nOrdinary work may produce meaningful evidence. Gardening, cataloguing, correspondence, printing, storage, repairs, bookkeeping, collecting, preservation, and other repeated activities can leave traces that later acquire significance.\nPreserve ambiguity when canon does not justify certainty. Records may be incomplete, memories partial, labels mistaken, chronology approximate, and interpretations disputed. Do not resolve uncertainty by inventing facts.\nVisual assets should imply narrative through carefully chosen material relationships rather than excessive symbolic decoration. A small number of meaningful details is preferable to a scene overloaded with obvious “clues.”\nThis module must never invent a discovery, historical fact, named person, date, location, relationship, document content, ownership claim, or object provenance. Those must come from the Production Spec or approved canon.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nWychcombe reveals meaning through evidence, provenance, material condition, sequence, place, omission, and careful interpretation. Let objects and records suggest history through plausible traces of use and relationship. Favor subtle recoverable evidence over theatrical clues. Preserve uncertainty when canon is incomplete. Never invent discoveries or factual canon to create atmosphere.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Wychcombe visual or narrative components where objects, documents, botanicals, places, archival material, or physical traces contribute to storytelling. Apply only where evidence or narrative context is relevant; purely abstract decorative assets may not require it.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nBest suited to Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining assets. It may also accompany None when the asset needs Wychcombe’s general evidence logic without referencing specific facts. This module must never supply missing canon. The stronger the canon dependency, the more strictly evidence must trace back to approved records.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nInject early in World & Collection Context after foundational Wychcombe world rules and before Style System modules, component-specific content, and Production Spec scene instructions. Canon Records and explicit Production Spec facts remain authoritative. This module governs how those facts are expressed as evidence and narrative context, not what the facts are.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nNarrative interest emerges from plausible objects, records, materials, placement, wear, omission, or relationships rather than obvious theatrical clues.\nEvidence appears to have existed naturally within the world before the viewer encountered it and has believable provenance or physical context.\nThe asset distinguishes between established information and suggestion, leaving uncertainty intact where canon does not provide an answer.\nA restrained number of meaningful details rewards close observation without turning every object into a clue.\nOrdinary work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, cataloguing, or material history contributes naturally to the sense of story.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nInvented names, dates, discoveries, secret messages, ownership claims, relationships, historical events, document contents, or other unsupported canon appear merely to make the asset interesting.\nObvious mystery clichés dominate the scene, such as conveniently exposed secret letters, dramatic coded messages, glowing clues, conspicuous hidden compartments, or excessive symbolic objects.\nEvery object is treated as significant, creating clue clutter rather than a believable lived environment.\nEvidence lacks provenance, context, physical plausibility, or a reason for being present and preserved.\nAmbiguous or incomplete canon is converted into definitive claims instead of remaining uncertain, disputed, approximate, or unresolved.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with Wychcombe World & Collection Context, approved Canon Records, and Production Specs that contain narrative or evidentiary content. Pair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock, Material World, Quiet Composition, Atmospheric Grounding, and Period & Digital Exclusions when producing Volume I visual assets.\nThis module governs narrative evidence behavior only. It does not control rendering style, palette, material appearance, composition, technical production requirements, or component format.\nCanon Records and explicit Production Spec facts always take precedence. If this module suggests that an object could carry evidentiary meaning but canon defines it as ordinary or unrelated, preserve the canon interpretation.\nDo not allow Atmospheric Grounding or visual styling modules to turn neutral objects into implied clues merely for mood. Conversely, when approved canon establishes meaningful evidence, visual modules should support its presentation without obscuring or sensationalizing it."},{"key":"visual_palette","label":"Visual Palette","source":"World Bible","content":"Wychcombe is a Victorian estate given over to the quiet work of preservation — a place where botanical specimens are pressed and catalogued, where archives accumulate in climate-controlled stillness, and where scholarship proceeds at the pace of careful hands. The estate comprises a main house of honeyed stone, a sprawling greenhouse complex with ironwork gone soft with verdigris, outbuildings converted to workrooms and storage, and grounds that blur the line between curated garden and managed wilderness.\n\nThe atmosphere is one of **studied hush** — not silence, but the particular quiet of concentration. Gravel paths, the creak of wooden drawers, the scratch of nib on paper. Time moves differently here: seasons matter more than hours, and the work of a single afternoon might continue research begun three generations prior.\n\nVisually, Wychcombe exists in a palette of **aged naturals** — ivory paper darkened to cream, green in every muted variation from moss to fern, the warm brown of walnut cases and sepia ink, brass worn to a soft glow. Color is restrained; when it appears (a terracotta pot, a burgundy wax seal), it carries weight.\n\nThis is a world of **things that endure** and the people who tend them — where meaning accumulates through proximity, repetition, and care."},{"key":"prose_voice","label":"Prose Voice","source":"World Bible","content":"For Wychcombe, your prose voice should feel like **curated documentation** — authoritative but not clinical, careful but not cold. A few principles:\n\n**Measured precision over flourish.** Wychcombe is a place of preservation and scholarship. Sentences should feel considered, as if each word earns its place. Avoid purple prose; let material details carry emotional weight.\n\n**The curator's eye.** Describe objects, spaces, and people the way an archivist would — noting condition, provenance, wear patterns, what's present and what's conspicuously absent. \"The ledger's spine had cracked at the same page for decades\" tells us more than adjectives.\n\n**Restraint as atmosphere.** Wychcombe's mood lives in what's held back. Grief is a pressed flower tucked between pages. Tension is a key that fits no known lock. Let silence, omission, and the weight of accumulated years do narrative work.\n\n**Grounded sensory language.** Lean into your material palette — the smell of foxed paper, the cold of brass in morning light, condensation on greenhouse glass. These textures anchor the world more than abstract description.\n\n**Time as layered presence.** Write as if the past is always partially visible beneath the present — in habits, in repeated gestures, in objects that outlast their owners."},{"key":"atmospheric_notes","label":"Atmospheric Notes","source":"World Bible","content":"- Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight\n- Diffused morning or overcast light\n- Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate"},{"key":"material_world","label":"Material World","source":"World Bible","content":"| Paper | Fine fiber, subtle tooth, restrained foxing, use-based wear |\n| Wood | Visible grain, soft edge wear, matte finish |\n| Brass | Soft patina, warm highlights, no mirror polish |\n| Glass | Clouding, age, condensation, slight waviness |\n| Botanicals | Realistic fibers, veins, flattening, and discoloration |"},{"key":"style_system","label":"Style System","source":"Style Guide: Volume I Visual Language: The Curator’s Desk","content":"STYLE GUIDE: Victorian Garden Journals\nType: Full Suite\nScope: A complete style guide covering visual language, typography, tone of voice, and production constraints for this world. All production work references this document.\n\n## Visual Language\n\n### Colour Palette\nFamily\tPrimary Use\n--Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream\tPaper base, margins, writing areas\n--Moss / Olive / Fern\tBotanical accents and quiet patterning\n--Walnut / Charcoal / Sepia\tWood, linework, typography, structural depth\n--Weathered Brass\tTools, keys, hardware, restrained highlights\n--Terracotta / Muted Burgundy\tRare warm accents, pots, wax seals\n\n### Illustration Style\nIllustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals\n- Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight\n- Diffused morning or overcast light\n- Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate\n\n**Avoid** harsh spotlights, magical glow, god rays, fog, extreme chiaroscuro, cinematic contrast, and blue digital shadows.\n\n**Composition**\n\n1. One primary focal area\n2. One or two secondary narrative clusters\n3. Supporting depth materials\n4. Quiet zones for usability\n\nNegative space is required. Preserve margins, open wood grain, quiet linen, glass light, and writable zones.\n\nKeep critical elements inside a 0.25-inch visual safety zone for 12 × 12 papers.\n\n### Texture & Materials\nWatercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite\nAged rag paper integration, visible paper texture\nMuted natural pigments, low-to-moderate saturation\nSoftened edges, restrained engraved detail\nIllustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals\nSoft archival daylight or restrained period lamplight\nIntentional, reviewed labels and marks only\nPeriod-appropriate natural and archival materials\n\n## Typography & Tone\n\n### Typeface Direction\nPrimary: Cormorant Garamond / Playfair Display — old-style serif. Annotations: condensed or small-caps variant. Numerals: old-style figures. Display: Playfair Display SC, tracked. Secondary (UI/captions): Source Sans Pro. No geometric sans, rounded fonts, or any typeface that reads as modern in primary roles.\n\n### Type Hierarchy\nH1 Display: Playfair Display SC, 48pt+, tracked +200, Ink Navy. H2: Cormorant Garamond italic, 24pt. H3: Source Sans Pro SemiBold, 16pt. Body: Cormorant Garamond 11–12pt, 1.5× leading. Caption: Source Sans Pro 8pt. Micro: 7pt, tracked +100.\n\n### Prose Voice\nVictorian naturalist-scholar — learned, precise, quietly passionate. Sentences structured and complex but never opaque. Observation celebrated; enthusiasm restrained. Author has decades of field experience and knows the names of things. First person sparingly; third person for description; second person only as invitation.\nRegister: Personal / intimate\n\n### Reference Phrases\n\"A remarkable specimen, noteworthy for its…\" | \"The collector will observe that…\" | \"For those who find themselves drawn to…\" | \"There is much here that rewards patience.\" | \"Drawn from the archive of…\"\n\n### Words & Phrases to Avoid\navoid fantasy plants, malformed tools, heavy sepia, generic antique-shop clutter, random grunge, and any visual treatment that breaks the illustrated Victorian archival system.\n\n## Constraints\n\n### Negative Constraints (Do Not)\nPhotography, hyperreal 3D, glossy digital rendering\nClean digital white, polished synthetic surfaces\nCommercial saturation, bright synthetic color\nRazor-sharp photographic depth of field\nDSLR still life, cinematic rendering, game-concept-art appearance\nHigh-contrast commercial lighting, theatrical glow\nPseudo-text, barcodes, contemporary labels\nModern plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, synthetic ribbon\n\n### Production Rules\nAll artwork at 300 DPI for print, 144 DPI digital. CMYK for print (ISO Coated v2), sRGB for digital. Bleed: 3mm. Safe zone: 5mm. All fonts embedded and outlined before handoff. Paper simulation reduced for digital-only SKUs. All artwork approved in greyscale. All copy through tone review before publication.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I\nType: Atmospheric Grounding\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nCreate the scene within a quiet Victorian botanical and archival atmosphere shaped by scholarly intimacy, inherited responsibility, botanical care, gentle discovery, and methodical observation. The environment should feel accumulated through genuine use rather than manufactured as a decorative vintage setting. Favor aged rag paper, foxed parchment, worn walnut or oak, weathered brass, iron-gall ink, graphite, linen, natural twine, terracotta, old greenhouse glass, pressed botanicals, and restrained traces of soil or moss where appropriate. Use muted natural pigments and low-to-moderate saturation. Lighting should feel like soft greenhouse or muted library daylight, diffused morning or overcast light, with restrained period lamplight only when appropriate. Preserve quiet visual space and a sense of thoughtful observation. Avoid theatrical gothic atmosphere, fantasy, horror, luxury-stationery polish, generic antique-shop clutter, heavy sepia, random grunge, cinematic lighting, magical glow, glossy digital surfaces, and modern materials.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nQuiet Victorian botanical-archival atmosphere; scholarly intimacy and gentle discovery; accumulated rather than manufactured; muted natural pigments; aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, pressed botanicals and period materials; soft greenhouse or library daylight; restrained, tactile, observational, and non-theatrical.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera, Pocket, Envelope, Tag, Label, Hidden Discovery, Packaging and Marketing assets. Use wherever the Volume I visual atmosphere should remain consistent across the collection.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nSupports Canon. This module reinforces the approved Volume I visual atmosphere and collection identity but should not introduce, define, or alter canon facts, characters, places, events, objects, or discoveries.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nInject early in shared_prompt, after World & Collection Context and before component-specific composition, object, botanical, typography, or production instructions. More specific approved modules and Production Specification requirements take precedence where they add detail without contradicting the governing Style Guide.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nThe image feels quiet, tactile, and accumulated through use rather than staged or commercially styled.\nMaterials read distinctly and plausibly, such as aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, glass, terracotta, linen, or pressed botanicals when present.\nColor remains muted and natural, with cream, botanical greens, walnut, charcoal, sepia, and restrained warm accents rather than bright or synthetic color.\nLighting is soft and diffused, suggesting greenhouse daylight, library daylight, or restrained period lamplight without dramatic effects.\nThe overall atmosphere supports scholarly observation, botanical care, and gentle discovery while leaving sufficient visual quiet for the component's intended function.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nThe image appears photographic, hyperreal, glossy, digitally polished, or commercially staged rather than illustrated and tactile.\nLighting becomes cinematic, theatrical, magical, excessively dark, high-contrast, or dominated by glow or fog effects.\nColors become highly saturated, jewel-toned, synthetically green, heavily sepia, or otherwise inconsistent with the muted natural palette.\nThe scene becomes generic antique-shop clutter, excessive vintage decoration, gothic fantasy, horror, cottagecore spectacle, or luxury stationery.\nAging becomes uniform grunge or excessive distressing rather than plausible evidence of handling, use, and material age.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with World & Collection Context modules for Wychcombe and Volume I, component-specific composition modules, approved object or botanical modules, typography modules, and production/output modules. This module establishes the atmospheric baseline and should not introduce canon facts or determine component-specific content. The governing Style Guide remains authoritative for visual language. World and canon modules remain authoritative for factual content. More specific component modules may refine composition, objects, botanicals, typography, and functional requirements without contradicting the Style Guide. Production Specification requirements take precedence for the individual asset's dimensions, function, required content, and output constraints. If another module introduces conflicting atmosphere or visual treatment, resolve the conflict in favor of the governing Style Guide rather than blending incompatible styles.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I\nType: Style Reference\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Usage\nApply to all Volume I visual Production Specs that must follow the illustrated archival visual language. Attach when generating Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other illustrated Volume I assets. Use this module to prevent visual drift toward photography, hyperrealism, 3D rendering, cinematic imagery, or glossy digital artwork. This module governs rendering style only; component-specific composition and production behavior remain controlled by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec.\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nRender all applicable Volume I visual assets as unmistakably hand-illustrated Victorian botanical and archival artwork. The result must read as an illustration rather than a photograph, photographed still life, hyperreal 3D render, or cinematic digital scene.\nUse watercolor and gouache washes, fine ink linework, restrained graphite construction, subtle engraving-inspired hatching, visible aged rag-paper texture, softened illustrated edges, and muted natural pigments. Objects, botanicals, paper, wood, brass, glass, ink, textiles, labels, and other materials should be represented through illustrated marks, pigment, linework, and tactile paper integration rather than photographic surface realism.\nPreserve plausible material identity without allowing realism to override the medium. Wood grain should appear illustrated, brass should read through warm pigment and restrained highlights, glass through softened transparency and linework, botanicals through botanical drawing and painted form, and paper through visible fiber, tooth, foxing, and controlled aging.\nIf any substantial part of the image reads as photography, DSLR still life, hyperreal rendering, physically based rendering, glossy digital art, cinematic concept art, or commercial product photography, the output fails this module.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nHand-illustrated Victorian botanical-archival style only; watercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite, restrained engraving detail, visible aged paper texture, softened illustrated edges, muted natural pigments; plausible materials rendered through illustration rather than photographic realism; reject photography, hyperreal 3D, DSLR still life, cinematic rendering, glossy digital art, and commercial product styling.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply to all Volume I visual assets intended to use the illustrated archival language. Do not use for non-visual outputs or components intentionally governed by a different rendering style.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nCompatible with all canon dependency levels: None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module controls rendering style rather than canon content. It must not introduce, alter, interpret, or override canon.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nHigh priority. Inject early in the Style System section, after governing world and collection context and the linked Style Guide, but before Atmospheric Grounding, Material World, component-specific instructions, and Production Spec visual content. Treat the illustrated-not-photographic requirement as a mandatory style lock. Later modules may refine atmosphere, materials, composition, and subject matter but must not override this rendering requirement.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nThe entire asset immediately reads as a hand-illustrated Victorian botanical or archival work rather than a photograph.\nWatercolor or gouache washes, fine ink linework, graphite construction, or restrained engraving-style marks are visibly present and coherent across the asset.\nPaper, wood, brass, glass, botanicals, textiles, and other materials remain recognizable while clearly rendered through illustration rather than photographic surface realism.\nEdges, shadows, highlights, and textures have a softened illustrated quality with muted natural pigments and visible paper integration.\nAll major elements share the same illustrated visual language. No object appears pasted in, photographed, hyperreal, or rendered in a conflicting medium.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nAny major element reads as photography, a photographed still life, DSLR imagery, or photorealistic product photography.\nHyperrealistic 3D rendering, physically based materials, cinematic rendering, glossy digital surfaces, or artificial photographic depth of field are visible.\nObjects use realistic reflections, lighting, shadows, or surface textures that make them appear photographed rather than illustrated.\nThe asset mixes incompatible rendering treatments, such as illustrated botanicals beside photorealistic books, brass, paper, furniture, or tools.\nThe overall result resembles commercial vintage styling, game concept art, digital collage, or an antique-shop photograph instead of a unified illustrated archival work.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with Atmospheric Grounding for mood and lighting, Material World for material behavior, Negative Space modules for composition and usability, and Technical Constraint modules for production requirements and exclusions.\nThe linked Style Guide remains the governing visual-language reference. This module reinforces its illustrated rendering requirement and should not introduce independent subject matter, canon, composition, or production specifications.\nComponent Specs control component-specific format and production behavior. Production Specs control the individual asset's purpose, required content, composition, and canon references.\nIf another module requests photographic realism, hyperrealistic rendering, glossy digital treatment, cinematic rendering, or another incompatible medium, this Style Lock takes precedence for Volume I assets unless the governing Style Guide explicitly authorizes an exception.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Material World — Volume I\nType: Material World\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Usage\nApply to Volume I visual Production Specs where physical materials contribute to the illustrated archival world. Use this module to maintain consistent material character across paper, wood, brass, glass, ink, botanicals, textiles, terracotta, twine, wax, and related period-appropriate materials. This module governs material appearance, aging, texture, and physical character, not component layout, subject matter, or canon content.\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nRender Volume I materials as tactile, aged through plausible use, and integrated into the illustrated archival environment. Materials should show restrained variation, handling, age, and natural imperfection rather than artificial distressing.\nPaper: fine visible fiber and subtle tooth; antique ivory through foxed cream; restrained foxing, edge wear, folds, handling darkening, faint stains, and ink absorption where appropriate. Paper must remain usable and believable, never uniformly grungy, burned, heavily damaged, or artificially distressed.\nWood: walnut and other period-appropriate woods with visible illustrated grain, softened edges, matte surfaces, handling wear, and restrained age variation. Avoid glossy furniture finishes or exaggerated rustic damage.\nBrass: weathered brass with soft patina, warm restrained highlights, handling variation, and age appropriate to frequently used tools and fittings. Avoid mirror polish, chrome-like reflections, or bright metallic CGI effects.\nGlass: old greenhouse, bottle, lens, or cabinet glass with softened transparency, subtle clouding, slight waviness, condensation when contextually appropriate, and restrained reflections.\nInk and graphite: iron-gall or period-plausible ink with natural variation, slight fading, absorption into paper, fine linework, and occasional handling evidence. Graphite should remain soft, matte, and visibly drawn.\nBotanicals: pressed specimens, cuttings, seed heads, ferns, herbs, and botanical studies should show believable fibers, veins, flattening, drying, curling, and natural discoloration. Avoid plastic-looking foliage, fantasy plants, or unnaturally perfect specimens.\nTerracotta, twine, ribbon, linen, wax, and related materials: preserve recognizable material character through restrained texture, wear, pigment variation, fibers, impressions, and handling.\nMaterials should feel accumulated through work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, and ordinary use. Aging must communicate history through specific physical evidence rather than generic “vintage” decoration.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nTactile illustrated materials with restrained use-based aging: fine-fiber antique paper, worn matte walnut, softly patinated brass, slightly clouded period glass, absorbed and faded ink, soft graphite, naturally dried botanicals, weathered terracotta, fibrous twine and linen, and handled wax. Preserve believable material differences. Avoid uniform grunge, burned edges, glossy surfaces, chrome reflections, plastic foliage, synthetic textures, excessive distressing, and generic vintage effects.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply to Volume I visual components containing illustrated physical materials, surfaces, botanicals, archival objects, or paper-based elements. Material treatment should scale appropriately to the component rather than requiring every listed material to appear.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nCompatible with None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module governs the visual behavior of materials and aging rather than canon facts. It must not introduce specific objects, locations, dates, people, inscriptions, or historical claims that are not supplied by the Production Spec or linked canon.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nInject in the Style System section after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific content. The Style Lock determines the rendering medium; Material World determines how physical materials appear within that medium. Component Specs and Production Specs determine which materials and objects are actually required. Do not allow material instructions to override canon, composition, print requirements, or component-specific constraints.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nMaterials are visually distinct and immediately recognizable: paper reads as fibrous paper, wood as wood, brass as aged metal, glass as glass, and botanicals as preserved or living plant material.\nAging appears plausible and use-based, with restrained foxing, handling wear, softened edges, patina, fading, fibers, folds, and natural discoloration where appropriate.\nMaterial textures remain consistent with the illustrated archival style rather than becoming photographic or hyperrealistic.\nSurfaces show natural variation without excessive distressing; age and wear appear accumulated through handling, storage, botanical study, correspondence, and archival work.\nThe overall material palette feels cohesive, tactile, muted, and period-plausible while preserving meaningful differences between individual materials.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nUniform grunge, heavy staining, burned edges, excessive tearing, artificial aging, or distressed effects applied indiscriminately across the asset.\nGlossy, plastic, chrome-like, synthetic, or digitally polished surfaces inconsistent with the Volume I material language.\nMaterials lose their physical identity, such as brass appearing plastic, wood appearing digitally smooth, paper resembling flat beige texture, or botanicals appearing artificial.\nPhotorealistic textures, reflections, surface detail, or material rendering break the illustrated archival treatment.\nAging becomes decorative rather than plausible, making the asset resemble generic “vintage” craft imagery instead of objects with specific histories of use and preservation.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I, which governs rendering medium and takes precedence over any instruction that would make materials photographic or hyperrealistic. Pair with Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for lighting and environmental mood, and with Negative Space or Technical Constraint modules where applicable.\nMaterial World governs how specified materials appear; it does not determine which objects or materials must be present. Component Specs and Production Specs control required materials, objects, composition, format, and production behavior.\nCanon and Production Spec requirements take precedence when they establish a specific object's material or condition. Material World may enrich that material visually but must not change its identity, provenance, age, meaning, or canon significance.\nWhen instructions conflict, preserve this order: Canon and explicit Production Spec facts → Component production requirements → Illustrated Archival Style Lock → Material World → Atmospheric refinements.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Period & Digital Exclusions — Volume I\nType: Technical Constraint\nInjection point: negative_prompt\n\n## Usage\nApply to all Volume I visual Production Specs to prevent anachronistic, photographic, hyperreal, synthetic, digitally polished, or otherwise incompatible visual treatments. Use across Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Volume I visual components.\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nExclude visual elements and rendering treatments that break the illustrated Victorian archival system.\nDo not use photography, photographic still-life styling, DSLR aesthetics, photographic depth of field, hyperrealistic rendering, physically based rendering, glossy 3D materials, cinematic concept art, commercial product photography, game-rendering aesthetics, or polished digital illustration that reads as synthetic rather than tactile and archival.\nExclude modern or anachronistic materials and objects including plastics, ballpoint pens, contemporary electrical devices, modern packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic ribbon, modern office supplies, contemporary signage, digital screens, modern typography, and other clearly post-period visual language unless explicitly required by the Production Spec.\nAvoid clean digital white, neon color, bright synthetic saturation, chrome-like reflections, mirror-polished metal, hard digital gradients, blue digital shadows, artificial glow, god rays, fog effects, theatrical lighting, extreme chiaroscuro, cinematic contrast, or any treatment that turns quiet archival imagery into spectacle.\nDo not use generic antique-shop clutter, luxury stationery styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, horror aesthetics, steampunk machinery, faux-vintage craft-store decoration, random grunge, burned edges, uniform distressing, or arbitrary “old-fashioned” embellishment.\nDo not generate pseudo-text, modern labels, unsupported logos, invented institutional marks, fake historical branding, or decorative text that appears factual but lacks an approved source.\nThe goal is not merely to appear old. The result must remain materially plausible, illustrated, restrained, and consistent with the governed Volume I visual language.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nExclude photography, photorealism, DSLR still life, hyperreal 3D, cinematic rendering, glossy digital surfaces, modern objects, plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic materials, modern typography, neon color, chrome reflections, theatrical glow, extreme contrast, pseudo-text, generic antique-shop clutter, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, steampunk, random grunge, burned edges, and unsupported historical branding.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply to all Volume I visual components governed by the illustrated archival language. Do not attach only when a Production Spec explicitly requires a different visual period or rendering system.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nCompatible with None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module suppresses incompatible visual treatments and anachronisms rather than supplying canon content. It must never exclude an object, material, inscription, or detail explicitly required by accepted canon or the Production Spec.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nHigh priority. Inject into the negative_prompt after the positive Style System instructions have established the intended illustrated archival treatment. Apply alongside Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I as its exclusion guardrail. Maintain these exclusions throughout generation, while allowing explicit canon, Production Spec, and Component Spec requirements to take precedence where they intentionally require an otherwise excluded element.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nThe asset contains no obvious modern, digital, synthetic, photographic, or anachronistic visual elements unless explicitly required by the Production Spec.\nThe image remains convincingly within the illustrated Victorian archival language, with period-plausible objects, materials, typography, lighting, and surface treatment.\nDigital generation artifacts are visually suppressed: no glossy CGI surfaces, photographic depth of field, artificial glow, cinematic effects, or synthetic-looking textures.\nAging and historical character feel restrained and materially plausible rather than relying on generic vintage effects.\nAny visible text, labels, marks, or institutional identifiers appear intentional and supported by the Production Spec or linked canon rather than invented decorative pseudo-history.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nPhotography, photorealism, DSLR still-life treatment, hyperreal 3D rendering, cinematic lighting, glossy CGI materials, photographic depth of field, or other clearly digital rendering behavior.\nModern or anachronistic objects or materials such as plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, contemporary packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic office supplies, or modern signage unless explicitly required.\nNeon or synthetic color, chrome-like reflections, artificial glow, dramatic fog, god rays, extreme cinematic contrast, or other theatrical effects.\nGeneric antique-shop clutter, faux-vintage craft styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, steampunk imagery, excessive grunge, burned edges, or indiscriminate distressing.\nPseudo-text, invented historical branding, unsupported institutional marks, fake labels, or other visual information that could be mistaken for canon.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair directly with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I. The Style Lock establishes the required positive rendering language; this module suppresses incompatible alternatives.\nPair with Material World — Volume I so permitted materials retain plausible age, wear, and physical character without drifting into glossy, synthetic, or generic vintage treatments. Pair with Quiet Composition — Volume I and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I without allowing atmospheric effects to introduce cinematic lighting, fog, glow, or theatrical contrast.\nThis module must not suppress an element explicitly required by accepted canon, the Production Spec, or the Component Spec. When an otherwise excluded element is intentionally required, the explicit requirement takes precedence.\nThis module controls exclusions only. It must not invent replacement objects, historical facts, text, branding, or canon details.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Quiet Composition — Volume I\nType: Negative Space\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Usage\nApply to Volume I visual Production Specs where composition must preserve visual quiet, usable negative space, restrained density, and clear hierarchy. Use across Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers, with the exact amount and location of quiet space determined by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec.\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nPreserve quiet visual space as an intentional part of the composition rather than treating empty areas as unfinished.\nEstablish a clear hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate, limited supporting clusters, and sufficient breathing room between visual elements. Avoid filling every available area with decoration, objects, botanicals, text, texture, aging, or archival marks.\nQuiet space may take the form of open paper, restrained wood grain, low-density linen or material texture, pale margins, softly illuminated glass, uncluttered writing areas, or other contextually appropriate surfaces.\nMaintain visual balance rather than symmetry for its own sake. Allow areas of lower detail to support more active areas and preserve the intended function of the component.\nWhen the Production Spec defines a writing-space percentage or usability requirement, treat that requirement as mandatory. The quiet area must remain genuinely usable rather than merely lighter in color while still crowded with detail.\nDecorative elements should not migrate into functional writing, cutting, trim, binding, or safe areas unless explicitly permitted.\nNegative space should feel naturally integrated into the visual world, not digitally erased, artificially blank, or disconnected from the rest of the composition.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nPreserve intentional visual quiet and usable negative space. One clear focal area where appropriate, restrained supporting clusters, low-to-moderate density, and genuine breathing room. Do not fill empty areas with decorative clutter. Respect writing-space, cut, trim, binding, and safe-area requirements. Quiet space should feel naturally integrated into the illustrated environment.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply wherever Volume I assets need restrained visual density, usable negative space, clear hierarchy, and protection against overfilling. The exact amount and placement of quiet space must follow the linked Component Spec and Production Spec.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nCompatible with None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module governs composition and visual breathing room only. It must not introduce, remove, reinterpret, or override canon content.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nnject in the Style System after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and Material World — Volume I, and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific composition/content instructions. Treat Production Spec writing-space, usability, trim, cut, and safe-area requirements as mandatory. Component-specific composition rules may refine where quiet space appears, but should not eliminate required negative space.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nThe composition has a clear visual hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate and restrained supporting elements.\nMeaningful areas of genuine visual quiet remain open and usable rather than being filled with decoration, text, botanicals, objects, or heavy texture.\nActive and quiet areas feel intentionally balanced, allowing the viewer’s eye to rest and move naturally through the composition.\nAny writing-space percentage or functional area required by the Production Spec remains visibly usable and substantially unobstructed.\nNegative space feels integrated into the illustrated environment through paper, subtle material texture, restrained surface detail, or other contextually appropriate treatment rather than appearing artificially blank.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nThe composition fills nearly every available area with objects, botanicals, text, marks, aging, patterns, or decorative detail.\nRequired writing or functional space is visually occupied, heavily textured, interrupted, or reduced below the Production Spec requirement.\nToo many competing focal areas, clusters, or high-detail elements create visual noise or unclear hierarchy.\nNegative space appears as an unnatural empty hole, digitally erased region, hard-edged blank area, or disconnected background.\nDecorative elements intrude into required trim, cut, binding, safe, writing, or usability zones without explicit instruction.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I for rendering consistency, Material World — Volume I for believable low-density surfaces and textures, and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for environmental mood.\nQuiet Composition governs visual density, hierarchy, breathing room, and usability. It does not determine required subjects, objects, canon content, or exact component dimensions.\nComponent Specs and Production Specs take precedence for explicit writing-space percentages, safe zones, trim areas, orientation, layout requirements, and required content. Quiet Composition should help satisfy those requirements rather than replace them.\nRequired narrative or canon elements must not be removed merely to create more negative space. Instead, simplify secondary decoration, reduce density, consolidate clusters, or reposition nonessential elements.\nWhen instructions conflict, preserve this order: required canon/content → production and component requirements → required usability/negative space → style and material refinements → optional decorative detail."},{"key":"component_requirements","label":"Component Requirements","source":"Component Specification: Decorative Paper — Core Component Specification","content":"Next up: Decorative Paper. This should sit between Hero Paper and a simple repeating background. Its job is to provide a strong coordinated surface for journaling and layering without carrying a major narrative scene.\nName\nDecorative Paper — Core Component Specification\nComponent Type\nDecorative Paper\nSpecification Details\nPURPOSE\nDefines the reusable production requirements for Decorative Paper components. Decorative Paper provides a coordinated full-page visual surface for journaling, layering, matting, collage, and related paper-craft uses. It supports the collection’s visual language without functioning as a primary narrative scene. Collection-specific style, palette, motifs, canon, objects, typography, and atmosphere are inherited from the linked Style Guide, Canon Records, Prompt Modules, and individual Production Specification.\nFORMAT & COMPOSITION\n• Design as a complete full-page composition suitable for use at finished print size.\n• The page should remain visually useful when partially covered, cropped, torn, folded, or layered beneath other journal components.\n• Favor distributed visual rhythm over a dominant central focal point.\n• Decorative interest may concentrate toward edges, corners, bands, or selected areas when defined by the Production Specification.\n• Preserve sufficient visual quiet so other journal elements can be layered over the paper without excessive competition.\n• Avoid composition that reads as a Hero Paper, illustrated scene, poster, or single large standalone artwork unless explicitly required.\nVISUAL DENSITY\n• Maintain a controlled balance between decorated and quiet areas.\n• Do not fill every available area with motifs, objects, texture, or marks.\n• Large motifs should not dominate the page unless intentionally specified.\n• Fine details must remain visually coherent at final print size.\n• Decorative density should support practical journal use.\nCROPPING & LAYERING\n• The design must tolerate common cropping from any edge without destroying its visual logic.\n• Avoid placing all meaningful visual interest in a single crop-sensitive location.\n• Essential motifs should not depend upon seeing the entire sheet.\n• Cropped fragments should continue to feel intentional when used as mats, pockets, strips, torn edges, or layered backgrounds.\nPATTERN & REPEAT BEHAVIOR\n• Decorative Paper may use scattered motifs, directional compositions, borders, botanical arrangements, archival marks, surface treatments, restrained patterns, or other structures defined by the Production Specification.\n• Do not assume seamless repetition unless explicitly required.\n• Repeated motifs should vary naturally unless a formal repeating pattern is intentionally specified.\n• Avoid obvious mechanical duplication that makes illustrated elements appear digitally stamped.\nMATERIAL & SURFACE\n• Surface treatment should reproduce convincingly as printed material at final physical size.\n• Paper grain, foxing, fading, stains, fibers, ink, botanical impressions, handwriting traces, or other material effects should remain restrained and intentional.\n• Aging must suggest use, storage, handling, or material history rather than generic digital distress.\n• Avoid uniform grunge, excessive staining, burned edges, artificial noise, or distress applied equally across the entire sheet.\nUSABILITY\n• Decorative Paper must remain functional for writing, layering, collage, pockets, mounting photographs or cards, and other uses defined by the Production Specification.\n• Maintain usable areas of lower visual density.\n• Do not require the user to preserve the entire sheet for the design to make sense.\n• The paper should coordinate with more narrative components without competing with them for attention.\nTEXT & CANON\n• Readable text is closed-world content and must be explicitly supplied or supported by approved governed sources.\n• Do not invent names, dates, correspondence, locations, botanical identifications, quotations, signatures, document contents, historical claims, or other factual-looking text.\n• When text-like texture is desired without governed wording, use non-readable handwriting traces, faded partial marks, abstract ink forms, blank structures, or other non-semantic treatments.\n• Decorative use of canon material must not accidentally imply new facts or duplicate unique narrative evidence.\nBLEED & EDGE SAFETY\n• Background color, texture, pattern, and non-critical decorative imagery may extend through bleed.\n• Designs intended to reach the finished edge must continue naturally through the production bleed area.\n• Keep critical readable or canon-dependent content inside the safe area.\n• Natural cropping of non-critical decorative motifs at sheet edges is permitted where visually intentional.\nPRINT & OUTPUT\n• Final dimensions, resolution, DPI, color mode, bleed, safe zones, front/back behavior, and export requirements inherit from the linked Print Target.\n• Artwork must retain useful texture, linework, and tonal separation at final physical size.\n• Avoid effects dependent upon screen luminosity, extreme contrast, photographic depth of field, or details too fine to reproduce reliably in print.\nGOVERNANCE\nThis Component Spec does not define the individual paper’s motif, collection-specific palette, narrative theme, botanical subject, recurring objects, readable wording, canon content, or decorative density.\nThose requirements belong to the appropriate governed records and individual Production Specification.\nWhen requirements conflict, apply specificity in this order:\n1. Print Target technical requirements\n2. Individual Production Specification\n3. Approved canon requirements\n4. Linked Style Guide\n5. This Component Spec\nA Production Specification may refine Decorative Paper for a particular asset but should not silently redefine the fundamental production behavior of the component."},{"key":"shared_prompt","label":"Shared Prompt","source":"Prompt Payload","content":"Create a coordinated Volume I decorative paper belonging to Wychcombe and The Curator’s Desk component set. Treat the entire sheet as a designed paper surface rather than a depicted room, desk scene, or collection of separate ephemera."},{"key":"front_prompt","label":"Front Prompt","source":"Prompt Payload","content":"Produce one complete square decorative paper design."},{"key":"negative_prompt","label":"Negative Prompt","source":"Prompt Payload","content":"Do not depict a complete desk scene, room interior, greenhouse scene, open journal as the primary subject, still-life tableau, or central narrative vignette."},{"key":"canon_policy","label":"Canon Policy","source":"Canon Records: The Curators","content":"Canon Dependency: Supports Canon\nCanon Record: The Curators (accepted)\nNarrative Details: The Curators are a Wychcombe institution or role-group responsible for preserving, interpreting, arranging, or controlling access to records and objects.\nEmotional Register: Withholding"},{"key":"print_and_output_requirements","label":"Print And Output Requirements","source":"Production Specification","content":"Orientation: square\nFront/Back Style: single-sided\nWriting Space: 0%\nReview Criteria: PASS / FAIL — Does the finished piece read first as a decorative journal paper rather than a Hero Paper, desk scene, ephemera sheet, or collection of separate objects?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the paper unmistakably belong to Volume I and The Curator’s Desk visual family while maintaining its own quieter, background-oriented function?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Are botanical studies integrated into the paper surface with natural variation in scale, placement, completeness, and density rather than forming an obvious mechanical repeat?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the composition avoid a dominant central focal scene while still maintaining intentional visual rhythm and areas of greater and lesser detail?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Is approximately 15% of the sheet clearly usable quiet space, with enough additional low-detail area for layering, writing, cutting, and journal use?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Do paper, ink, graphite, botanical material, aging, and other surfaces feel tactile, illustrated, restrained, and consistent with the linked Volume I material language?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Do archival marks, catalog structures, specimen traces, or annotation elements function as subtle evidence rather than decorative clutter?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Is aging believable and use-based rather than uniform grunge, heavy staining, burned edges, excessive distressing, or artificial vintage effects?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the artwork avoid photography, photorealism, glossy digital rendering, cinematic lighting, synthetic surfaces, modern graphic-design conventions, and commercial scrapbook styling?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Is the design free of pseudo-text, prominent gibberish handwriting, unsupported dates, invented documents, unsupported historical claims, characters, events, discoveries, or other accidental canon?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Are botanical subjects plausible within the established Volume I vocabulary rather than fantasy plants, generic cottagecore florals, or decorative bouquets?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the finished sheet coordinate naturally with the Curator’s Desk Hero Paper and Ephemera Sheet without copying their compositions?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the paper remain visually useful at full 12 × 12 scale and when cropped into smaller journal sections?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the final result feel accumulated through observation, preservation, and use rather than manufactured as a modern faux-Victorian pattern?"},{"key":"world_rules","label":"World Rules","source":"World Bible","content":"Discoveries in Wychcombe emerge through evidence, relationship, sequence, place, and interpretation rather than arbitrary revelation.\nThe Wychcombe timeline is a layered chronology that distinguishes dated fact, approximate sequence, remembered history, disputed accounts, and narrative reveal order.\nHidden Discoveries tracks concealed material, layered clues, recoverable evidence, and revelations that become visible through sequence, context, or physical interaction.\nIn Wychcombe, memory lives through people, places, objects, habits, documents, omissions, and repeated acts of preservation."}],"generated_filename":null,"notion_upload_id":null},{"id":"85e2836d-2de9-4d3a-8e85-b05f7e662b1d","production_spec_id":"0c51adac-c7cd-4416-a69c-923feaf058c8","operation":"validate_and_compile","status":"validation_failed","dry_run":false,"payload_version":"PP-2.0","compiled_prompt":"[MANDATORY RENDERING STYLE]\nThe governing Style Guide rendering medium is mandatory and overrides lower-priority rendering directions. STYLE GUIDE: Victorian Garden Journals Type: Full Suite Scope: A complete style guide covering visual language, typography, tone of voice, and production constraints for this world. All production work references this document. ## Visual Language ### Colour Palette Family Primary Use Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream Paper base, margins, writing areas Moss / Olive / Fern Botanical accents and quiet patterning Walnut / Charcoal / Sepia Wood, linework, typography, structural depth Weathered Brass Tools, keys, hardware, restrained highlights Terracotta / Muted Burgundy Rare warm accents, pots, wax seals ### Illustration Style Illustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight Diffused morning or overcast light Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate Composition** One primary focal area One or two secondary narrative clusters Supporting depth materials Quiet zones for usability Negative space is required. Preserve margins, open wood grain, quiet linen, glass light, and writable zones. Keep critical elements inside a 0.25-inch visual safety zone for 12 × 12 papers. ### Texture & Materials Watercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite Aged rag paper integration, visible paper texture Muted natural pigments, low-to-moderate saturation Softened edges, restrained engraved detail Soft archival daylight or restrained period lamplight Intentional, reviewed labels and marks only Period-appropriate natural and archival materials ## Typography & Tone ### Typeface Direction Primary: Cormorant Garamond / Playfair Display — old-style serif. Annotations: condensed or small-caps variant. Numerals: old-style figures. Display: Playfair Display SC, tracked. Secondary (UI/captions): Source Sans Pro. ### Type Hierarchy H1 Display: Playfair Display SC, 48pt+, tracked +200, Ink Navy. H2: Cormorant Garamond italic, 24pt. H3: Source Sans Pro SemiBold, 16pt. Body: Cormorant Garamond 11–12pt, 1.5× leading. Caption: Source Sans Pro 8pt. Micro: 7pt, tracked +100. ### Prose Voice Victorian naturalist-scholar — learned, precise, quietly passionate. Observation celebrated enthusiasm restrained. Author has decades of field experience and knows the names of things. First person sparingly third person for description second person only as invitation. Register: Personal / intimate ### Reference Phrases Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. ## Constraints Clean digital white, polished synthetic surfaces Commercial saturation, bright synthetic color High-contrast commercial lighting, theatrical glow Pseudo-text, barcodes, contemporary labels Modern plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, synthetic ribbon ### Production Rules All artwork at 300 DPI for print, 144 DPI digital. CMYK for print (ISO Coated v2), sRGB for digital. Bleed: 3mm. Safe zone: 5mm. All fonts embedded and outlined before handoff. Paper simulation reduced for digital-only SKUs. All artwork approved in greyscale. All copy through tone review before publication. PROMPT MODULE: Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I Type: Atmospheric Grounding Injection point: shared_prompt ## Prompt Content ### Primary Create the scene within a quiet Victorian botanical and archival atmosphere shaped by scholarly intimacy, inherited responsibility, botanical care, gentle discovery, and methodical observation. The environment should feel accumulated through genuine use rather than manufactured as a decorative vintage setting. Favor aged rag paper, foxed parchment, worn walnut or oak, weathered brass, iron-gall ink, graphite, linen, natural twine, terracotta, old greenhouse glass, pressed botanicals, and restrained traces of soil or moss where appropriate. Use muted natural pigments and low-to-moderate saturation. Lighting should feel like soft greenhouse or muted library daylight, diffused morning or overcast light, with restrained period lamplight only when appropriate. Preserve quiet visual space and a sense of thoughtful observation. ### Alternative / Condensed Quiet Victorian botanical-archival atmosphere scholarly intimacy and gentle discovery accumulated rather than manufactured muted natural pigments aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, pressed botanicals and period materials soft greenhouse or library daylight restrained, tactile, observational, and non-theatrical. ## Targeting ### Applicable Component Types Hero Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera, Pocket, Envelope, Tag, Label, Hidden Discovery, Packaging and Marketing assets. Use wherever the Volume I visual atmosphere should remain consistent across the collection. ### Canon Dependency Hint Supports Canon. ### Priority / Injection Order Inject early in shared_prompt, after World & Collection Context and before component-specific composition, object, botanical, typography, or production instructions. ## Quality & Interactions ### Good Output Indicators The image feels quiet, tactile, and accumulated through use rather than staged or commercially styled. Materials read distinctly and plausibly, such as aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, glass, terracotta, linen, or pressed botanicals when present. Color remains muted and natural, with cream, botanical greens, walnut, charcoal, sepia, and restrained warm accents rather than bright or synthetic color. The overall atmosphere supports scholarly observation, botanical care, and gentle discovery while leaving sufficient visual quiet for the component's intended function. ### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present) Lighting becomes cinematic, theatrical, magical, excessively dark, high-contrast, or dominated by glow or fog effects. Colors become highly saturated, jewel-toned, synthetically green, heavily sepia, or otherwise inconsistent with the muted natural palette. The scene becomes generic antique-shop clutter, excessive vintage decoration, gothic fantasy, horror, cottagecore spectacle, or luxury stationery. Aging becomes uniform grunge or excessive distressing rather than plausible evidence of handling, use, and material age. ### Module Interactions Pair with World & Collection Context modules for Wychcombe and Volume I, component-specific composition modules, approved object or botanical modules, typography modules, and production/output modules. The governing Style Guide remains authoritative for visual language. World and canon modules remain authoritative for factual content. Production Specification requirements take precedence for the individual asset's dimensions, function, required content, and output constraints. If another module introduces conflicting atmosphere or visual treatment, resolve the conflict in favor of the governing Style Guide rather than blending incompatible styles. PROMPT MODULE: Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I Type: Style Reference ## Usage Apply to all Volume I visual Production Specs that must follow the illustrated archival visual language. Attach when generating Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other illustrated Volume I assets. This module governs rendering style only component-specific composition and production behavior remain controlled by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. Render all applicable Volume I visual assets as unmistakably hand-illustrated Victorian botanical and archival artwork. Use watercolor and gouache washes, fine ink linework, restrained graphite construction, subtle engraving-inspired hatching, visible aged rag-paper texture, softened illustrated edges, and muted natural pigments. Wood grain should appear illustrated, brass should read through warm pigment and restrained highlights, glass through softened transparency and linework, botanicals through botanical drawing and painted form, and paper through visible fiber, tooth, foxing, and controlled aging. Hand-illustrated Victorian botanical-archival style only watercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite, restrained engraving detail, visible aged paper texture, softened illustrated edges, muted natural pigments Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply to all Volume I visual assets intended to use the illustrated archival language. Compatible with all canon dependency levels: None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module controls rendering style rather than canon content. High priority. Inject early in the Style System section, after governing world and collection context and the linked Style Guide, but before Atmospheric Grounding, Material World, component-specific instructions, and Production Spec visual content. Watercolor or gouache washes, fine ink linework, graphite construction, or restrained engraving-style marks are visibly present and coherent across the asset. Edges, shadows, highlights, and textures have a softened illustrated quality with muted natural pigments and visible paper integration. All major elements share the same illustrated visual language. Objects use realistic reflections, lighting, shadows, or surface textures that make them appear photographed rather than illustrated. Pair with Atmospheric Grounding for mood and lighting, Material World for material behavior, Negative Space modules for composition and usability, and Technical Constraint modules for production requirements and exclusions. The linked Style Guide remains the governing visual-language reference. Component Specs control component-specific format and production behavior. Production Specs control the individual asset's purpose, required content, composition, and canon references. PROMPT MODULE: Material World — Volume I Type: Material World Apply to Volume I visual Production Specs where physical materials contribute to the illustrated archival world. Use this module to maintain consistent material character across paper, wood, brass, glass, ink, botanicals, textiles, terracotta, twine, wax, and related period-appropriate materials. Render Volume I materials as tactile, aged through plausible use, and integrated into the illustrated archival environment. Materials should show restrained variation, handling, age, and natural imperfection rather than artificial distressing. Paper: fine visible fiber and subtle tooth antique ivory through foxed cream restrained foxing, edge wear, folds, handling darkening, faint stains, and ink absorption where appropriate. Paper must remain usable and believable Wood: walnut and other period-appropriate woods with visible illustrated grain, softened edges, matte surfaces, handling wear, and restrained age variation. Brass: weathered brass with soft patina, warm restrained highlights, handling variation, and age appropriate to frequently used tools and fittings. Glass: old greenhouse, bottle, lens, or cabinet glass with softened transparency, subtle clouding, slight waviness, condensation when contextually appropriate, and restrained reflections. Ink and graphite: iron-gall or period-plausible ink with natural variation, slight fading, absorption into paper, fine linework, and occasional handling evidence. Graphite should remain soft, matte, and visibly drawn. Botanicals: pressed specimens, cuttings, seed heads, ferns, herbs, and botanical studies should show believable fibers, veins, flattening, drying, curling, and natural discoloration. Terracotta, twine, ribbon, linen, wax, and related materials: preserve recognizable material character through restrained texture, wear, pigment variation, fibers, impressions, and handling. Materials should feel accumulated through work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, and ordinary use. Tactile illustrated materials with restrained use-based aging: fine-fiber antique paper, worn matte walnut, softly patinated brass, slightly clouded period glass, absorbed and faded ink, soft graphite, naturally dried botanicals, weathered terracotta, fibrous twine and linen, and handled wax. Preserve believable material differences. Apply to Volume I visual components containing illustrated physical materials, surfaces, botanicals, archival objects, or paper-based elements. Material treatment should scale appropriately to the component rather than requiring every listed material to appear. Compatible with None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module governs the visual behavior of materials and aging rather than canon facts. Inject in the Style System section after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific content. The Style Lock determines the rendering medium Material World determines how physical materials appear within that medium. Component Specs and Production Specs determine which materials and objects are actually required. Materials are visually distinct and immediately recognizable: paper reads as fibrous paper, wood as wood, brass as aged metal, glass as glass, and botanicals as preserved or living plant material. Aging appears plausible and use-based, with restrained foxing, handling wear, softened edges, patina, fading, fibers, folds, and natural discoloration where appropriate. age and wear appear accumulated through handling, storage, botanical study, correspondence, and archival work. The overall material palette feels cohesive, tactile, muted, and period-plausible while preserving meaningful differences between individual materials. Uniform grunge, heavy staining, burned edges, excessive tearing, artificial aging, or distressed effects applied indiscriminately across the asset. Glossy, plastic, chrome-like, synthetic, or digitally polished surfaces inconsistent with the Volume I material language. Materials lose their physical identity, such as brass appearing plastic, wood appearing digitally smooth, paper resembling flat beige texture, or botanicals appearing artificial. Pair with Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for lighting and environmental mood, and with Negative Space or Technical Constraint modules where applicable. Material World governs how specified materials appear Component Specs and Production Specs control required materials, objects, composition, format, and production behavior. Canon and Production Spec requirements take precedence when they establish a specific object's material or condition. When instructions conflict, preserve this order: Canon and explicit Production Spec facts → Component production requirements → Illustrated Archival Style Lock → Material World → Atmospheric refinements. PROMPT MODULE: Period & Digital Exclusions — Volume I Type: Technical Constraint Injection point: negative_prompt Use across Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Volume I visual components. The result must remain materially plausible, illustrated, restrained, and consistent with the governed Volume I visual language. Apply to all Volume I visual components governed by the illustrated archival language. This module suppresses incompatible visual treatments and anachronisms rather than supplying canon content. Inject into the negative_prompt after the positive Style System instructions have established the intended illustrated archival treatment. Apply alongside Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I as its exclusion guardrail. Maintain these exclusions throughout generation, while allowing explicit canon, Production Spec, and Component Spec requirements to take precedence where they intentionally require an otherwise excluded element. The image remains convincingly within the illustrated Victorian archival language, with period-plausible objects, materials, typography, lighting, and surface treatment. Aging and historical character feel restrained and materially plausible rather than relying on generic vintage effects. Any visible text, labels, marks, or institutional identifiers appear intentional and supported by the Production Spec or linked canon rather than invented decorative pseudo-history. Modern or anachronistic objects or materials such as plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, contemporary packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic office supplies, or modern signage unless explicitly required. Neon or synthetic color, chrome-like reflections, artificial glow, dramatic fog, god rays, extreme cinematic contrast, or other theatrical effects. Generic antique-shop clutter, faux-vintage craft styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, steampunk imagery, excessive grunge, burned edges, or indiscriminate distressing. Pseudo-text, invented historical branding, unsupported institutional marks, fake labels, or other visual information that could be mistaken for canon. Pair directly with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I. The Style Lock establishes the required positive rendering language this module suppresses incompatible alternatives. When an otherwise excluded element is intentionally required, the explicit requirement takes precedence. This module controls exclusions only. PROMPT MODULE: Quiet Composition — Volume I Type: Negative Space Apply to Volume I visual Production Specs where composition must preserve visual quiet, usable negative space, restrained density, and clear hierarchy. Use across Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers, with the exact amount and location of quiet space determined by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. Preserve quiet visual space as an intentional part of the composition rather than treating empty areas as unfinished. Establish a clear hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate, limited supporting clusters, and sufficient breathing room between visual elements. Quiet space may take the form of open paper, restrained wood grain, low-density linen or material texture, pale margins, softly illuminated glass, uncluttered writing areas, or other contextually appropriate surfaces. Maintain visual balance rather than symmetry for its own sake. Allow areas of lower detail to support more active areas and preserve the intended function of the component. When the Production Spec defines a writing-space percentage or usability requirement, treat that requirement as mandatory. The quiet area must remain genuinely usable rather than merely lighter in color while still crowded with detail. Preserve intentional visual quiet and usable negative space. One clear focal area where appropriate, restrained supporting clusters, low-to-moderate density, and genuine breathing room. Respect writing-space, cut, trim, binding, and safe-area requirements. Quiet space should feel naturally integrated into the illustrated environment. Apply wherever Volume I assets need restrained visual density, usable negative space, clear hierarchy, and protection against overfilling. The exact amount and placement of quiet space must follow the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. This module governs composition and visual breathing room only. nject in the Style System after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and Material World — Volume I, and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific composition/content instructions. Treat Production Spec writing-space, usability, trim, cut, and safe-area requirements as mandatory. The composition has a clear visual hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate and restrained supporting elements. Meaningful areas of genuine visual quiet remain open and usable rather than being filled with decoration, text, botanicals, objects, or heavy texture. Active and quiet areas feel intentionally balanced, allowing the viewer’s eye to rest and move naturally through the composition. Any writing-space percentage or functional area required by the Production Spec remains visibly usable and substantially unobstructed. Negative space feels integrated into the illustrated environment through paper, subtle material texture, restrained surface detail, or other contextually appropriate treatment rather than appearing artificially blank. The composition fills nearly every available area with objects, botanicals, text, marks, aging, patterns, or decorative detail. Required writing or functional space is visually occupied, heavily textured, interrupted, or reduced below the Production Spec requirement. Too many competing focal areas, clusters, or high-detail elements create visual noise or unclear hierarchy. Negative space appears as an unnatural empty hole, digitally erased region, hard-edged blank area, or disconnected background. Pair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I for rendering consistency, Material World — Volume I for believable low-density surfaces and textures, and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for environmental mood. Quiet Composition governs visual density, hierarchy, breathing room, and usability. Component Specs and Production Specs take precedence for explicit writing-space percentages, safe zones, trim areas, orientation, layout requirements, and required content. Quiet Composition should help satisfy those requirements rather than replace them. Instead, simplify secondary decoration, reduce density, consolidate clusters, or reposition nonessential elements. When instructions conflict, preserve this order: required canon/content → production and component requirements → required usability/negative space → style and material refinements → optional decorative detail. This must read unmistakably as artwork in the required medium, never as a photograph or photographed still life. Subject realism, plausible materials, detail, and natural lighting describe content within that medium; they never replace the governing medium. If any part of the image reads as photography, DSLR still-life photography, hyperreal 3D, cinematic rendering, or glossy digital realism, the result fails the style requirement.\n\n[GOVERNED READABLE TEXT]\nReadable text is closed-world content. Do not invent, infer, complete, or improvise any readable wording. Render only exact wording explicitly authorized below by the Production Specification or an Accepted Canon Record. All unspecified text-bearing areas must be blank ruled fields, empty label areas, empty catalog fields, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Do not generate unsupported names, dates, addresses, botanical identifications, specimen numbers, correspondence prose, ledger entries, geographic or map labels, historical claims, signatures, initials, or institutional information. No readable strings are authorized; all text-bearing areas must remain blank or non-readable.\n\n[ASSET AND SCENE]\nAsset: Curator’s Desk Botanical Study — Decorative Paper (Decorative Paper)\nWorld: Wychcombe; Volume: Volume I: The Curator’s Desk.\nCreate a richly illustrated but restrained decorative paper that translates the Curator’s Desk visual language into a repeatable, background-oriented composition. The paper should feel derived from accumulated botanical study materials, specimen sketches, archival markings, and aged paper surfaces rather than from conventional modern scrapbook pattern design. The overall effect should be quiet, scholarly, botanical, tactile, and collected over time.\nRepresent the visual residue of botanical observation and archival work within the Curator’s world. Rather than depicting a specific event or complete scene, the paper should suggest fragments of study accumulated through repeated observation: botanical drawings, specimen traces, faint annotations, catalog marks, paper impressions, and other restrained evidence of curatorial practice. The paper supports the world through atmosphere and material evidence rather than introducing new narrative facts.\nCreate a coordinated Volume I decorative paper belonging to Wychcombe and The Curator’s Desk component set. Treat the entire sheet as a designed paper surface rather than a depicted room, desk scene, or collection of separate ephemera.\nPROMPT MODULE: Curatorial Evidence Language — Wychcombe Type: Canon Anchor Injection point: shared_prompt ## Usage Apply to Wychcombe Production Specs and generated assets where narrative meaning is communicated through objects, documents, botanical material, correspondence, archives, places, traces of use, omissions, sequence, provenance, or physical evidence. Use this module to make discoveries and storytelling feel native to Wychcombe rather than artificially dramatic. This module governs how evidence communicates meaning ## Prompt Content ### Primary In Wychcombe, narrative meaning should emerge through evidence, relationship, sequence, place, provenance, material condition, and interpretation rather than arbitrary revelation or decorative mystery. Treat objects, documents, botanicals, buildings, correspondence, tools, labels, records, traces of handling, repairs, wear, storage, placement, absence, and repetition as potential evidence. Their significance should come from how they relate to people, places, time, and other evidence rather than from theatrical presentation. Favor the curator’s method: observe first, infer carefully, preserve uncertainty where evidence is incomplete, and distinguish what is known from what is suggested. Discoveries should feel recoverable because evidence existed before the viewer encountered it. Evidence should have plausible provenance, physical context, and a reason for surviving. Ordinary work may produce meaningful evidence. Gardening, cataloguing, correspondence, printing, storage, repairs, bookkeeping, collecting, preservation, and other repeated activities can leave traces that later acquire significance. Records may be incomplete, memories partial, labels mistaken, chronology approximate, and interpretations disputed. Visual assets should imply narrative through carefully chosen material relationships rather than excessive symbolic decoration. Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Those must come from the Production Spec or approved canon. ### Alternative / Condensed Wychcombe reveals meaning through evidence, provenance, material condition, sequence, place, omission, and careful interpretation. Let objects and records suggest history through plausible traces of use and relationship. Favor subtle recoverable evidence over theatrical clues. Preserve uncertainty when canon is incomplete. ## Targeting ### Applicable Component Types Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Wychcombe visual or narrative components where objects, documents, botanicals, places, archival material, or physical traces contribute to storytelling. Apply only where evidence or narrative context is relevant ### Canon Dependency Hint Best suited to Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining assets. The stronger the canon dependency, the more strictly evidence must trace back to approved records. ### Priority / Injection Order Inject early in World & Collection Context after foundational Wychcombe world rules and before Style System modules, component-specific content, and Production Spec scene instructions. Canon Records and explicit Production Spec facts remain authoritative. ## Quality & Interactions ### Good Output Indicators Narrative interest emerges from plausible objects, records, materials, placement, wear, omission, or relationships rather than obvious theatrical clues. Evidence appears to have existed naturally within the world before the viewer encountered it and has believable provenance or physical context. Ordinary work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, cataloguing, or material history contributes naturally to the sense of story. ### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present) Invented names, dates, discoveries, secret messages, ownership claims, relationships, historical events, document contents, or other unsupported canon appear merely to make the asset interesting. Obvious mystery clichés dominate the scene, such as conveniently exposed secret letters, dramatic coded messages, glowing clues, conspicuous hidden compartments, or excessive symbolic objects. Every object is treated as significant, creating clue clutter rather than a believable lived environment. Evidence lacks provenance, context, physical plausibility, or a reason for being present and preserved. Ambiguous or incomplete canon is converted into definitive claims instead of remaining uncertain, disputed, approximate, or unresolved. ### Module Interactions Pair with Wychcombe World & Collection Context, approved Canon Records, and Production Specs that contain narrative or evidentiary content. Pair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock, Material World, Quiet Composition, Atmospheric Grounding, and Period & Digital Exclusions when producing Volume I visual assets. This module governs narrative evidence behavior only. Canon Records and explicit Production Spec facts always take precedence. If this module suggests that an object could carry evidentiary meaning but canon defines it as ordinary or unrelated, preserve the canon interpretation.\n\n[COMPOSITION]\nProduce one complete square decorative paper design.\n\n[REQUIRED OBJECTS AND CONTENT]\nUse an Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream paper ground with a restrained arrangement of period-appropriate botanical study imagery. Include: fine botanical line drawings or softly illustrated plant studies ferns, herbs, seed heads, leaves, or garden specimens appropriate to the established Volume I vocabulary subtle archival or cataloguing marks where appropriate faint graphite, sepia ink, or muted botanical pigment restrained foxing, handling wear, paper variation, and other material aging meaningful open areas between illustrated elements Botanical elements should vary naturally in scale, orientation, completeness, and density. Some may appear as complete studies while others may appear cropped, faded, partial, or ghosted into the paper surface. Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Otherwise use non-readable marks, numbering fragments, linework, or blank label structures rather than invented text. Orientation\nNext up: Decorative Paper. This should sit between Hero Paper and a simple repeating background. Name Decorative Paper — Core Component Specification Component Type Decorative Paper Specification Details PURPOSE Defines the reusable production requirements for Decorative Paper components. Decorative Paper provides a coordinated full-page visual surface for journaling, layering, matting, collage, and related paper-craft uses. Collection-specific style, palette, motifs, canon, objects, typography, and atmosphere are inherited from the linked Style Guide, Canon Records, Prompt Modules, and individual Production Specification. FORMAT & COMPOSITION Design as a complete full-page composition suitable for use at finished print size. The page should remain visually useful when partially covered, cropped, torn, folded, or layered beneath other journal components. Favor distributed visual rhythm over a dominant central focal point. Decorative interest may concentrate toward edges, corners, bands, or selected areas when defined by the Production Specification. VISUAL DENSITY Maintain a controlled balance between decorated and quiet areas. Fine details must remain visually coherent at final print size. Decorative density should support practical journal use. CROPPING & LAYERING Cropped fragments should continue to feel intentional when used as mats, pockets, strips, torn edges, or layered backgrounds. PATTERN & REPEAT BEHAVIOR Decorative Paper may use scattered motifs, directional compositions, borders, botanical arrangements, archival marks, surface treatments, restrained patterns, or other structures defined by the Production Specification. Repeated motifs should vary naturally unless a formal repeating pattern is intentionally specified. MATERIAL & SURFACE Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Paper grain, foxing, fading, stains, fibers, ink, botanical impressions, handwriting traces, or other material effects should remain restrained and intentional. Aging must suggest use, storage, handling, or material history rather than generic digital distress. USABILITY Maintain usable areas of lower visual density. TEXT & CANON BLEED & EDGE SAFETY Background color, texture, pattern, and non-critical decorative imagery may extend through bleed. Designs intended to reach the finished edge must continue naturally through the production bleed area. Keep critical readable or canon-dependent content inside the safe area. Natural cropping of non-critical decorative motifs at sheet edges is permitted where visually intentional. PRINT & OUTPUT Final dimensions, resolution, DPI, color mode, bleed, safe zones, front/back behavior, and export requirements inherit from the linked Print Target. Artwork must retain useful texture, linework, and tonal separation at final physical size. GOVERNANCE Those requirements belong to the appropriate governed records and individual Production Specification. When requirements conflict, apply specificity in this order: Individual Production Specification Approved canon requirements Linked Style Guide This Component Spec\n\n[ATMOSPHERE AND LIGHTING]\nA few principles: Measured precision over flourish.** Wychcombe is a place of preservation and scholarship. Sentences should feel considered, as if each word earns its place. let material details carry emotional weight. Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Grief is a pressed flower tucked between pages. Let silence, omission, and the weight of accumulated years do narrative work. Grounded sensory language.** Lean into your material palette — the smell of foxed paper, the cold of brass in morning light, condensation on greenhouse glass. These textures anchor the world more than abstract description. Time as layered presence.** Write as if the past is always partially visible beneath the present — in habits, in repeated gestures, in objects that outlast their owners.\nSoft greenhouse or muted library daylight Diffused morning or overcast light Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate\n\n[MATERIALS AND PALETTE]\nSTYLE GUIDE: Victorian Garden Journals Type: Full Suite Scope: A complete style guide covering visual language, typography, tone of voice, and production constraints for this world. All production work references this document. ## Visual Language ### Colour Palette Family Primary Use Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream Paper base, margins, writing areas Moss / Olive / Fern Botanical accents and quiet patterning Walnut / Charcoal / Sepia Wood, linework, typography, structural depth Weathered Brass Tools, keys, hardware, restrained highlights Terracotta / Muted Burgundy Rare warm accents, pots, wax seals ### Illustration Style Illustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight Diffused morning or overcast light Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate Composition** One primary focal area One or two secondary narrative clusters Supporting depth materials Quiet zones for usability Negative space is required. Preserve margins, open wood grain, quiet linen, glass light, and writable zones. Keep critical elements inside a 0.25-inch visual safety zone for 12 × 12 papers. ### Texture & Materials Watercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite Aged rag paper integration, visible paper texture Muted natural pigments, low-to-moderate saturation Softened edges, restrained engraved detail Soft archival daylight or restrained period lamplight Intentional, reviewed labels and marks only Period-appropriate natural and archival materials ## Typography & Tone ### Typeface Direction Primary: Cormorant Garamond / Playfair Display — old-style serif. Annotations: condensed or small-caps variant. Numerals: old-style figures. Display: Playfair Display SC, tracked. Secondary (UI/captions): Source Sans Pro. ### Type Hierarchy H1 Display: Playfair Display SC, 48pt+, tracked +200, Ink Navy. H2: Cormorant Garamond italic, 24pt. H3: Source Sans Pro SemiBold, 16pt. Body: Cormorant Garamond 11–12pt, 1.5× leading. Caption: Source Sans Pro 8pt. Micro: 7pt, tracked +100. ### Prose Voice Victorian naturalist-scholar — learned, precise, quietly passionate. Observation celebrated enthusiasm restrained. Author has decades of field experience and knows the names of things. First person sparingly third person for description second person only as invitation. Register: Personal / intimate ### Reference Phrases Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. ## Constraints Clean digital white, polished synthetic surfaces Commercial saturation, bright synthetic color High-contrast commercial lighting, theatrical glow Pseudo-text, barcodes, contemporary labels Modern plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, synthetic ribbon ### Production Rules All artwork at 300 DPI for print, 144 DPI digital. CMYK for print (ISO Coated v2), sRGB for digital. Bleed: 3mm. Safe zone: 5mm. All fonts embedded and outlined before handoff. Paper simulation reduced for digital-only SKUs. All artwork approved in greyscale. All copy through tone review before publication. PROMPT MODULE: Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I Type: Atmospheric Grounding Injection point: shared_prompt ## Prompt Content ### Primary Create the scene within a quiet Victorian botanical and archival atmosphere shaped by scholarly intimacy, inherited responsibility, botanical care, gentle discovery, and methodical observation. The environment should feel accumulated through genuine use rather than manufactured as a decorative vintage setting. Favor aged rag paper, foxed parchment, worn walnut or oak, weathered brass, iron-gall ink, graphite, linen, natural twine, terracotta, old greenhouse glass, pressed botanicals, and restrained traces of soil or moss where appropriate. Use muted natural pigments and low-to-moderate saturation. Lighting should feel like soft greenhouse or muted library daylight, diffused morning or overcast light, with restrained period lamplight only when appropriate. Preserve quiet visual space and a sense of thoughtful observation. ### Alternative / Condensed Quiet Victorian botanical-archival atmosphere scholarly intimacy and gentle discovery accumulated rather than manufactured muted natural pigments aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, pressed botanicals and period materials soft greenhouse or library daylight restrained, tactile, observational, and non-theatrical. ## Targeting ### Applicable Component Types Hero Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera, Pocket, Envelope, Tag, Label, Hidden Discovery, Packaging and Marketing assets. Use wherever the Volume I visual atmosphere should remain consistent across the collection. ### Canon Dependency Hint Supports Canon. ### Priority / Injection Order Inject early in shared_prompt, after World & Collection Context and before component-specific composition, object, botanical, typography, or production instructions. ## Quality & Interactions ### Good Output Indicators The image feels quiet, tactile, and accumulated through use rather than staged or commercially styled. Materials read distinctly and plausibly, such as aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, glass, terracotta, linen, or pressed botanicals when present. Color remains muted and natural, with cream, botanical greens, walnut, charcoal, sepia, and restrained warm accents rather than bright or synthetic color. The overall atmosphere supports scholarly observation, botanical care, and gentle discovery while leaving sufficient visual quiet for the component's intended function. ### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present) Lighting becomes cinematic, theatrical, magical, excessively dark, high-contrast, or dominated by glow or fog effects. Colors become highly saturated, jewel-toned, synthetically green, heavily sepia, or otherwise inconsistent with the muted natural palette. The scene becomes generic antique-shop clutter, excessive vintage decoration, gothic fantasy, horror, cottagecore spectacle, or luxury stationery. Aging becomes uniform grunge or excessive distressing rather than plausible evidence of handling, use, and material age. ### Module Interactions Pair with World & Collection Context modules for Wychcombe and Volume I, component-specific composition modules, approved object or botanical modules, typography modules, and production/output modules. The governing Style Guide remains authoritative for visual language. World and canon modules remain authoritative for factual content. Production Specification requirements take precedence for the individual asset's dimensions, function, required content, and output constraints. If another module introduces conflicting atmosphere or visual treatment, resolve the conflict in favor of the governing Style Guide rather than blending incompatible styles. PROMPT MODULE: Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I Type: Style Reference ## Usage Apply to all Volume I visual Production Specs that must follow the illustrated archival visual language. Attach when generating Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other illustrated Volume I assets. This module governs rendering style only component-specific composition and production behavior remain controlled by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. Render all applicable Volume I visual assets as unmistakably hand-illustrated Victorian botanical and archival artwork. Use watercolor and gouache washes, fine ink linework, restrained graphite construction, subtle engraving-inspired hatching, visible aged rag-paper texture, softened illustrated edges, and muted natural pigments. Wood grain should appear illustrated, brass should read through warm pigment and restrained highlights, glass through softened transparency and linework, botanicals through botanical drawing and painted form, and paper through visible fiber, tooth, foxing, and controlled aging. Hand-illustrated Victorian botanical-archival style only watercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite, restrained engraving detail, visible aged paper texture, softened illustrated edges, muted natural pigments Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply to all Volume I visual assets intended to use the illustrated archival language. Compatible with all canon dependency levels: None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module controls rendering style rather than canon content. High priority. Inject early in the Style System section, after governing world and collection context and the linked Style Guide, but before Atmospheric Grounding, Material World, component-specific instructions, and Production Spec visual content. Watercolor or gouache washes, fine ink linework, graphite construction, or restrained engraving-style marks are visibly present and coherent across the asset. Edges, shadows, highlights, and textures have a softened illustrated quality with muted natural pigments and visible paper integration. All major elements share the same illustrated visual language. Objects use realistic reflections, lighting, shadows, or surface textures that make them appear photographed rather than illustrated. Pair with Atmospheric Grounding for mood and lighting, Material World for material behavior, Negative Space modules for composition and usability, and Technical Constraint modules for production requirements and exclusions. The linked Style Guide remains the governing visual-language reference. Component Specs control component-specific format and production behavior. Production Specs control the individual asset's purpose, required content, composition, and canon references. PROMPT MODULE: Material World — Volume I Type: Material World Apply to Volume I visual Production Specs where physical materials contribute to the illustrated archival world. Use this module to maintain consistent material character across paper, wood, brass, glass, ink, botanicals, textiles, terracotta, twine, wax, and related period-appropriate materials. Render Volume I materials as tactile, aged through plausible use, and integrated into the illustrated archival environment. Materials should show restrained variation, handling, age, and natural imperfection rather than artificial distressing. Paper: fine visible fiber and subtle tooth antique ivory through foxed cream restrained foxing, edge wear, folds, handling darkening, faint stains, and ink absorption where appropriate. Paper must remain usable and believable Wood: walnut and other period-appropriate woods with visible illustrated grain, softened edges, matte surfaces, handling wear, and restrained age variation. Brass: weathered brass with soft patina, warm restrained highlights, handling variation, and age appropriate to frequently used tools and fittings. Glass: old greenhouse, bottle, lens, or cabinet glass with softened transparency, subtle clouding, slight waviness, condensation when contextually appropriate, and restrained reflections. Ink and graphite: iron-gall or period-plausible ink with natural variation, slight fading, absorption into paper, fine linework, and occasional handling evidence. Graphite should remain soft, matte, and visibly drawn. Botanicals: pressed specimens, cuttings, seed heads, ferns, herbs, and botanical studies should show believable fibers, veins, flattening, drying, curling, and natural discoloration. Terracotta, twine, ribbon, linen, wax, and related materials: preserve recognizable material character through restrained texture, wear, pigment variation, fibers, impressions, and handling. Materials should feel accumulated through work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, and ordinary use. Tactile illustrated materials with restrained use-based aging: fine-fiber antique paper, worn matte walnut, softly patinated brass, slightly clouded period glass, absorbed and faded ink, soft graphite, naturally dried botanicals, weathered terracotta, fibrous twine and linen, and handled wax. Preserve believable material differences. Apply to Volume I visual components containing illustrated physical materials, surfaces, botanicals, archival objects, or paper-based elements. Material treatment should scale appropriately to the component rather than requiring every listed material to appear. Compatible with None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module governs the visual behavior of materials and aging rather than canon facts. Inject in the Style System section after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific content. The Style Lock determines the rendering medium Material World determines how physical materials appear within that medium. Component Specs and Production Specs determine which materials and objects are actually required. Materials are visually distinct and immediately recognizable: paper reads as fibrous paper, wood as wood, brass as aged metal, glass as glass, and botanicals as preserved or living plant material. Aging appears plausible and use-based, with restrained foxing, handling wear, softened edges, patina, fading, fibers, folds, and natural discoloration where appropriate. age and wear appear accumulated through handling, storage, botanical study, correspondence, and archival work. The overall material palette feels cohesive, tactile, muted, and period-plausible while preserving meaningful differences between individual materials. Uniform grunge, heavy staining, burned edges, excessive tearing, artificial aging, or distressed effects applied indiscriminately across the asset. Glossy, plastic, chrome-like, synthetic, or digitally polished surfaces inconsistent with the Volume I material language. Materials lose their physical identity, such as brass appearing plastic, wood appearing digitally smooth, paper resembling flat beige texture, or botanicals appearing artificial. Pair with Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for lighting and environmental mood, and with Negative Space or Technical Constraint modules where applicable. Material World governs how specified materials appear Component Specs and Production Specs control required materials, objects, composition, format, and production behavior. Canon and Production Spec requirements take precedence when they establish a specific object's material or condition. When instructions conflict, preserve this order: Canon and explicit Production Spec facts → Component production requirements → Illustrated Archival Style Lock → Material World → Atmospheric refinements. PROMPT MODULE: Period & Digital Exclusions — Volume I Type: Technical Constraint Injection point: negative_prompt Use across Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Volume I visual components. The result must remain materially plausible, illustrated, restrained, and consistent with the governed Volume I visual language. Apply to all Volume I visual components governed by the illustrated archival language. This module suppresses incompatible visual treatments and anachronisms rather than supplying canon content. Inject into the negative_prompt after the positive Style System instructions have established the intended illustrated archival treatment. Apply alongside Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I as its exclusion guardrail. Maintain these exclusions throughout generation, while allowing explicit canon, Production Spec, and Component Spec requirements to take precedence where they intentionally require an otherwise excluded element. The image remains convincingly within the illustrated Victorian archival language, with period-plausible objects, materials, typography, lighting, and surface treatment. Aging and historical character feel restrained and materially plausible rather than relying on generic vintage effects. Any visible text, labels, marks, or institutional identifiers appear intentional and supported by the Production Spec or linked canon rather than invented decorative pseudo-history. Modern or anachronistic objects or materials such as plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, contemporary packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic office supplies, or modern signage unless explicitly required. Neon or synthetic color, chrome-like reflections, artificial glow, dramatic fog, god rays, extreme cinematic contrast, or other theatrical effects. Generic antique-shop clutter, faux-vintage craft styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, steampunk imagery, excessive grunge, burned edges, or indiscriminate distressing. Pseudo-text, invented historical branding, unsupported institutional marks, fake labels, or other visual information that could be mistaken for canon. Pair directly with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I. The Style Lock establishes the required positive rendering language this module suppresses incompatible alternatives. When an otherwise excluded element is intentionally required, the explicit requirement takes precedence. This module controls exclusions only. PROMPT MODULE: Quiet Composition — Volume I Type: Negative Space Apply to Volume I visual Production Specs where composition must preserve visual quiet, usable negative space, restrained density, and clear hierarchy. Use across Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers, with the exact amount and location of quiet space determined by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. Preserve quiet visual space as an intentional part of the composition rather than treating empty areas as unfinished. Establish a clear hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate, limited supporting clusters, and sufficient breathing room between visual elements. Quiet space may take the form of open paper, restrained wood grain, low-density linen or material texture, pale margins, softly illuminated glass, uncluttered writing areas, or other contextually appropriate surfaces. Maintain visual balance rather than symmetry for its own sake. Allow areas of lower detail to support more active areas and preserve the intended function of the component. When the Production Spec defines a writing-space percentage or usability requirement, treat that requirement as mandatory. The quiet area must remain genuinely usable rather than merely lighter in color while still crowded with detail. Preserve intentional visual quiet and usable negative space. One clear focal area where appropriate, restrained supporting clusters, low-to-moderate density, and genuine breathing room. Respect writing-space, cut, trim, binding, and safe-area requirements. Quiet space should feel naturally integrated into the illustrated environment. Apply wherever Volume I assets need restrained visual density, usable negative space, clear hierarchy, and protection against overfilling. The exact amount and placement of quiet space must follow the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. This module governs composition and visual breathing room only. nject in the Style System after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and Material World — Volume I, and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific composition/content instructions. Treat Production Spec writing-space, usability, trim, cut, and safe-area requirements as mandatory. The composition has a clear visual hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate and restrained supporting elements. Meaningful areas of genuine visual quiet remain open and usable rather than being filled with decoration, text, botanicals, objects, or heavy texture. Active and quiet areas feel intentionally balanced, allowing the viewer’s eye to rest and move naturally through the composition. Any writing-space percentage or functional area required by the Production Spec remains visibly usable and substantially unobstructed. Negative space feels integrated into the illustrated environment through paper, subtle material texture, restrained surface detail, or other contextually appropriate treatment rather than appearing artificially blank. The composition fills nearly every available area with objects, botanicals, text, marks, aging, patterns, or decorative detail. Required writing or functional space is visually occupied, heavily textured, interrupted, or reduced below the Production Spec requirement. Too many competing focal areas, clusters, or high-detail elements create visual noise or unclear hierarchy. Negative space appears as an unnatural empty hole, digitally erased region, hard-edged blank area, or disconnected background. Pair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I for rendering consistency, Material World — Volume I for believable low-density surfaces and textures, and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for environmental mood. Quiet Composition governs visual density, hierarchy, breathing room, and usability. Component Specs and Production Specs take precedence for explicit writing-space percentages, safe zones, trim areas, orientation, layout requirements, and required content. Quiet Composition should help satisfy those requirements rather than replace them. Instead, simplify secondary decoration, reduce density, consolidate clusters, or reposition nonessential elements. When instructions conflict, preserve this order: required canon/content → production and component requirements → required usability/negative space → style and material refinements → optional decorative detail.\nWychcombe is a Victorian estate given over to the quiet work of preservation — a place where botanical specimens are pressed and catalogued, where archives accumulate in climate-controlled stillness, and where scholarship proceeds at the pace of careful hands. The estate comprises a main house of honeyed stone, a sprawling greenhouse complex with ironwork gone soft with verdigris, outbuildings converted to workrooms and storage, and grounds that blur the line between curated garden and managed wilderness. Gravel paths, the creak of wooden drawers, the scratch of nib on paper. Time moves differently here: seasons matter more than hours, and the work of a single afternoon might continue research begun three generations prior. Visually, Wychcombe exists in a palette of **aged naturals** — ivory paper darkened to cream, green in every muted variation from moss to fern, the warm brown of walnut cases and sepia ink, brass worn to a soft glow. Color is restrained when it appears (a terracotta pot, a burgundy wax seal), it carries weight. This is a world of **things that endure** and the people who tend them — where meaning accumulates through proximity, repetition, and care.\n| Paper Fine fiber, subtle tooth, restrained foxing, use-based wear | Wood Visible grain, soft edge wear, matte finish | Brass Soft patina, warm highlights | Glass Clouding, age, condensation, slight waviness | Botanicals Realistic fibers, veins, flattening, and discoloration |\n\n[NEGATIVE AND WRITING SPACE]\nPreserve 0% usable visual space.\n\n[CANON CONSTRAINTS]\nCanon dependency: Supports Canon Canon constraint source only do not render its title: The Curators (accepted) The Curators are a Wychcombe institution or role-group responsible for preserving, interpreting, arranging, or controlling access to records and objects. Withholding\n\n[TECHNICAL AND OUTPUT REQUIREMENTS]\nOrientation: square\nFront/Back Style: single-sided\n\n[NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS / NEGATIVE PROMPT]\nphotograph, photography, photorealistic, hyperrealistic, DSLR still life, photographic lighting, photographic depth of field, physically based rendering, 3D render, cinematic rendering, glossy digital rendering, commercial product photography, museum still-life photography, razor-sharp photographic textures, ** harsh spotlights, magical glow, god rays, fog, extreme chiaroscuro, cinematic contrast, blue digital shadows, geometric sans, rounded fonts, any typeface that reads as modern in primary roles, Sentences structured and complex but never opaque, ### Words & Phrases to Avoid, fantasy plants, malformed tools, heavy sepia, generic antique-shop clutter, random grunge, any visual treatment that breaks the illustrated Victorian archival system, ### Negative Constraints (Do Not), Its job is to provide a strong coordinated surface for journaling and layering without carrying a major narrative scene, It supports the collection’s visual language without functioning as a primary narrative scene, Preserve sufficient visual quiet so other journal elements can be layered over the paper without excessive competition, composition that reads as a Hero Paper, illustrated scene, poster, single large standalone artwork unless explicitly required, fill every available area with motifs, objects, texture, marks, Large motifs should not dominate the page unless intentionally specified, The design must tolerate common cropping from any edge without destroying its visual logic, placing all meaningful visual interest in a single crop-sensitive location, Essential motifs should not depend upon seeing the entire sheet, assume seamless repetition unless explicitly required, obvious mechanical duplication that makes illustrated elements appear digitally stamped, uniform grunge, excessive staining, burned edges, artificial noise, distress applied equally across the entire sheet, require the user to preserve the entire sheet for the design to make sense, The paper should coordinate with more narrative components without competing with them for attention, invent names, dates, correspondence, locations, botanical identifications, quotations, signatures, document contents, historical claims, other factual-looking text, When text-like texture is desired without governed wording, use non-readable handwriting traces, faded partial marks, abstract ink forms, blank structures, other non-semantic treatments, Decorative use of canon material must not accidentally imply new facts or duplicate unique narrative evidence, effects dependent upon screen luminosity, extreme contrast, details too fine to reproduce reliably in print, This Component Spec does not define the individual paper’s motif, collection-specific palette, narrative theme, botanical subject, recurring objects, readable wording, canon content, decorative density, A Production Specification may refine Decorative Paper for a particular asset but should not silently redefine the fundamental production behavior of the component, theatrical gothic atmosphere, fantasy, horror, luxury-stationery polish, cinematic lighting, glossy digital surfaces, modern materials, This module reinforces the approved Volume I visual atmosphere and collection identity but should not introduce, define, alter canon facts, characters, places, events, discoveries, More specific approved modules and Production Specification requirements take precedence where they add detail without contradicting the governing Style Guide, Lighting is soft and diffused, suggesting greenhouse daylight, library daylight, restrained period lamplight without dramatic effects, This module establishes the atmospheric baseline and should not introduce canon facts or determine component-specific content, More specific component modules may refine composition, botanicals, typography, functional requirements without contradicting the Style Guide, Preserve plausible material identity without allowing realism to override the medium, glossy digital art, cinematic concept art, the output fails this module, use for non-visual outputs or components intentionally governed by a different rendering style, It must not introduce, alter, interpret, override canon, Later modules may refine atmosphere, materials, composition, subject matter but must not override this rendering requirement, object appears pasted in, photographed, rendered in a conflicting medium, This module reinforces its illustrated rendering requirement and should not introduce independent subject matter, canon, production specifications, This module governs material appearance, aging, physical character, not component layout, subject matter, uniformly grungy, burned, heavily damaged, artificially distressed, glossy furniture finishes or exaggerated rustic damage, mirror polish, chrome-like reflections, bright metallic CGI effects, plastic-looking foliage, unnaturally perfect specimens, glossy surfaces, chrome reflections, plastic foliage, synthetic textures, excessive distressing, generic vintage effects, It must not introduce specific objects, people, inscriptions, historical claims that are not supplied by the Production Spec or linked canon, allow material instructions to override canon, print requirements, component-specific constraints, Surfaces show natural variation without excessive distressing, it does not determine which objects or materials must be present, Material World may enrich that material visually but must not change its identity, provenance, age, meaning, canon significance, it does not create new canon facts, historical events, A cracked binding, erased notation, moved object, faded label, repeated repair, pressed specimen, empty drawer, mismatched date, unusual placement may carry narrative weight without announcing its importance, clues that appear solely because the story requires them, Preserve ambiguity when canon does not justify certainty, resolve uncertainty by inventing facts, This module must never invent a discovery, historical fact, named person, date, location, relationship, document content, ownership claim, object provenance, invent discoveries or factual canon to create atmosphere, purely abstract decorative assets may not require it, It may also accompany None when the asset needs Wychcombe’s general evidence logic without referencing specific facts, This module must never supply missing canon, This module governs how those facts are expressed as evidence and narrative context, not what the facts are, The asset distinguishes between established information and suggestion, leaving uncertainty intact where canon does not provide an answer, A restrained number of meaningful details rewards close observation without turning every object into a clue, It does not control rendering style, palette, material appearance, technical production requirements, component format, allow Atmospheric Grounding or visual styling modules to turn neutral objects into implied clues merely for mood, Conversely, when approved canon establishes meaningful evidence, visual modules should support its presentation without obscuring or sensationalizing it, visual elements and rendering treatments that break the illustrated Victorian archival system, glossy 3D materials, game-rendering aesthetics, polished digital illustration that reads as synthetic rather than tactile and archival, modern or anachronistic materials and objects including plastics, ballpoint pens, contemporary electrical devices, modern packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic ribbon, modern office supplies, contemporary signage, digital screens, modern typography, other clearly post-period visual language unless explicitly required by the Production Spec, clean digital white, neon color, bright synthetic saturation, mirror-polished metal, hard digital gradients, artificial glow, fog effects, theatrical lighting, any treatment that turns quiet archival imagery into spectacle, use generic antique-shop clutter, luxury stationery styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, horror aesthetics, steampunk machinery, faux-vintage craft-store decoration, uniform distressing, arbitrary “old-fashioned” embellishment, generate pseudo-text, modern labels, unsupported logos, invented institutional marks, fake historical branding, decorative text that appears factual but lacks an approved source, The goal is not merely to appear old, modern objects, plastics, electrical devices, synthetic materials, theatrical glow, pseudo-text, steampunk, unsupported historical branding, attach only when a Production Spec explicitly requires a different visual period or rendering system, It must never exclude an object, material, inscription, detail explicitly required by accepted canon or the Production Spec, The asset contains no obvious modern, digital, synthetic, anachronistic visual elements unless explicitly required by the Production Spec, Digital generation artifacts are visually suppressed: no glossy CGI surfaces, cinematic effects, synthetic-looking textures, Pair with Material World — Volume I so permitted materials retain plausible age, wear, physical character without drifting into glossy, generic vintage treatments, Pair with Quiet Composition — Volume I and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I without allowing atmospheric effects to introduce cinematic lighting, glow, theatrical contrast, This module must not suppress an element explicitly required by accepted canon, the Production Spec, the Component Spec, It must not invent replacement objects, historical facts, text, branding, canon details, filling every available area with decoration, archival marks, Decorative elements should not migrate into functional writing, cutting, trim, binding, safe areas unless explicitly permitted, Negative space should feel naturally integrated into the visual world, not digitally erased, artificially blank, disconnected from the rest of the composition, fill empty areas with decorative clutter, remove, reinterpret, override canon content, Component-specific composition rules may refine where quiet space appears, but should not eliminate required negative space, Decorative elements intrude into required trim, cut, safe, writing, usability zones without explicit instruction, It does not determine required subjects, exact component dimensions, Required narrative or canon elements must not be removed merely to create more negative space, PASS / FAIL — Does the finished piece read first as a decorative journal paper rather than a Hero Paper, desk scene, ephemera sheet, collection of separate objects?, PASS / FAIL — Does the paper unmistakably belong to Volume I and The Curator’s Desk visual family while maintaining its own quieter, background-oriented function?, PASS / FAIL — Are botanical studies integrated into the paper surface with natural variation in scale, placement, completeness, density rather than forming an obvious mechanical repeat?, PASS / FAIL — Does the composition avoid a dominant central focal scene while still maintaining intentional visual rhythm and areas of greater and lesser detail?, PASS / FAIL — Is approximately 15% of the sheet clearly usable quiet space, with enough additional low-detail area for layering, journal use?, PASS / FAIL — Do paper, ink, graphite, botanical material, other surfaces feel tactile, illustrated, restrained, consistent with the linked Volume I material language?, PASS / FAIL — Do archival marks, catalog structures, specimen traces, annotation elements function as subtle evidence rather than decorative clutter?, PASS / FAIL — Is aging believable and use-based rather than uniform grunge, heavy staining, artificial vintage effects?, synthetic surfaces, modern graphic-design conventions, commercial scrapbook styling?, PASS / FAIL — Is the design free of pseudo-text, prominent gibberish handwriting, unsupported dates, invented documents, unsupported historical claims, other accidental canon?, PASS / FAIL — Are botanical subjects plausible within the established Volume I vocabulary rather than fantasy plants, generic cottagecore florals, decorative bouquets?, PASS / FAIL — Does the finished sheet coordinate naturally with the Curator’s Desk Hero Paper and Ephemera Sheet without copying their compositions?, PASS / FAIL — Does the paper remain visually useful at full 12 × 12 scale and when cropped into smaller journal sections?, PASS / FAIL — Does the final result feel accumulated through observation, preservation, use rather than manufactured as a modern faux-Victorian pattern?, create a central desk scene, assembled collage, obvious grid, modern repeating wallpaper motif, collection of isolated stickers, depict a complete desk scene, room interior, greenhouse scene, open journal as the primary subject, still-life tableau, central narrative vignette","prompt_hash":null,"compiled_prompt_status":"Validation Failed","visual_asset_notion_id":null,"asset_id":null,"asset_version":null,"provider":null,"model_name":null,"model_version":null,"generation_settings":null,"seed":null,"provider_request_id":null,"cost_usd":null,"drive_file_id":null,"drive_folder_id":null,"drive_url":null,"daybook_asset_id":null,"errors":[{"code":"UNAUTHORIZED_READABLE_TEXT_REQUEST","field":"negative_prompt","message":"The canonical negative prompt contains an affirmative or inverted request for unsupported readable content: \"print requirements\".","governing_rule":"Production Specification: Curator’s Desk Botanical Study — Decorative Paper","recommended_action":"Remove the inverted readable-text instruction or authorize its exact wording from an approved source."}],"warnings":[],"failed_stage":null,"error_code":null,"resolved_source_ids":{"world":"wychcombe","volume":"78c13c7f-69bd-4ce3-8a9d-388b2992b527","world_id":"wychcombe","collection":"0d4bfa84-5411-4bfd-a7b6-25fc82d44458","world_name":"Wychcombe","style_guide":"d4fab007-9604-4cec-a100-e63f479b0a12","volume_name":"Volume I: The Curator’s Desk.","canon_records":["2bd24482-d2c0-4ef9-a3a5-bc5fcc853672"],"component_spec":"9246407c-7719-46d5-a65e-0783453be58e","prompt_modules":["e6daf9d9-a32c-457c-ba65-d818fc8de145","a58efef6-17ef-4a5e-b904-e0c7b3982017","ba44d968-3b1e-42b1-bf0c-b60897bb7b91","18327fa6-60f9-46a4-893e-34e4272beb9f","8677a201-ed38-46e3-8e93-bac017585c5c","db1cc97e-363a-4c82-88cd-441d5cf48739"],"collection_name":"Victorian Garden Journals","production_spec":"0c51adac-c7cd-4416-a69c-923feaf058c8","volume_notion_id":"78c13c7f-69bd-4ce3-8a9d-388b2992b527","collection_notion_id":"0d4bfa84-5411-4bfd-a7b6-25fc82d44458"},"retry_count":0,"initiated_by":"7e27c46a-7464-491a-9eb0-f23f52cc117b","started_at":"2026-08-31T13:15:08.014Z","completed_at":"2026-08-31T13:15:08.101Z","notion_retries":null,"compiled_sections":[{"key":"world_and_collection_context","label":"World And Collection Context","source":"World record + Curatorial Evidence Language — Wychcombe","content":"World: Wychcombe\nVolume: Volume I: The Curator’s Desk.\nPROMPT MODULE: Curatorial Evidence Language — Wychcombe\nType: Canon Anchor\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Usage\nApply to Wychcombe Production Specs and generated assets where narrative meaning is communicated through objects, documents, botanical material, correspondence, archives, places, traces of use, omissions, sequence, provenance, or physical evidence. Use this module to make discoveries and storytelling feel native to Wychcombe rather than artificially dramatic. This module governs how evidence communicates meaning; it does not create new canon facts, discoveries, people, dates, places, objects, or historical events.\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nIn Wychcombe, narrative meaning should emerge through evidence, relationship, sequence, place, provenance, material condition, and interpretation rather than arbitrary revelation or decorative mystery.\nTreat objects, documents, botanicals, buildings, correspondence, tools, labels, records, traces of handling, repairs, wear, storage, placement, absence, and repetition as potential evidence. Their significance should come from how they relate to people, places, time, and other evidence rather than from theatrical presentation.\nFavor the curator’s method: observe first, infer carefully, preserve uncertainty where evidence is incomplete, and distinguish what is known from what is suggested. A cracked binding, erased notation, moved object, faded label, repeated repair, pressed specimen, empty drawer, mismatched date, or unusual placement may carry narrative weight without announcing its importance.\nDiscoveries should feel recoverable because evidence existed before the viewer encountered it. Avoid clues that appear solely because the story requires them. Evidence should have plausible provenance, physical context, and a reason for surviving.\nOrdinary work may produce meaningful evidence. Gardening, cataloguing, correspondence, printing, storage, repairs, bookkeeping, collecting, preservation, and other repeated activities can leave traces that later acquire significance.\nPreserve ambiguity when canon does not justify certainty. Records may be incomplete, memories partial, labels mistaken, chronology approximate, and interpretations disputed. Do not resolve uncertainty by inventing facts.\nVisual assets should imply narrative through carefully chosen material relationships rather than excessive symbolic decoration. A small number of meaningful details is preferable to a scene overloaded with obvious “clues.”\nThis module must never invent a discovery, historical fact, named person, date, location, relationship, document content, ownership claim, or object provenance. Those must come from the Production Spec or approved canon.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nWychcombe reveals meaning through evidence, provenance, material condition, sequence, place, omission, and careful interpretation. Let objects and records suggest history through plausible traces of use and relationship. Favor subtle recoverable evidence over theatrical clues. Preserve uncertainty when canon is incomplete. Never invent discoveries or factual canon to create atmosphere.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Wychcombe visual or narrative components where objects, documents, botanicals, places, archival material, or physical traces contribute to storytelling. Apply only where evidence or narrative context is relevant; purely abstract decorative assets may not require it.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nBest suited to Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining assets. It may also accompany None when the asset needs Wychcombe’s general evidence logic without referencing specific facts. This module must never supply missing canon. The stronger the canon dependency, the more strictly evidence must trace back to approved records.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nInject early in World & Collection Context after foundational Wychcombe world rules and before Style System modules, component-specific content, and Production Spec scene instructions. Canon Records and explicit Production Spec facts remain authoritative. This module governs how those facts are expressed as evidence and narrative context, not what the facts are.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nNarrative interest emerges from plausible objects, records, materials, placement, wear, omission, or relationships rather than obvious theatrical clues.\nEvidence appears to have existed naturally within the world before the viewer encountered it and has believable provenance or physical context.\nThe asset distinguishes between established information and suggestion, leaving uncertainty intact where canon does not provide an answer.\nA restrained number of meaningful details rewards close observation without turning every object into a clue.\nOrdinary work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, cataloguing, or material history contributes naturally to the sense of story.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nInvented names, dates, discoveries, secret messages, ownership claims, relationships, historical events, document contents, or other unsupported canon appear merely to make the asset interesting.\nObvious mystery clichés dominate the scene, such as conveniently exposed secret letters, dramatic coded messages, glowing clues, conspicuous hidden compartments, or excessive symbolic objects.\nEvery object is treated as significant, creating clue clutter rather than a believable lived environment.\nEvidence lacks provenance, context, physical plausibility, or a reason for being present and preserved.\nAmbiguous or incomplete canon is converted into definitive claims instead of remaining uncertain, disputed, approximate, or unresolved.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with Wychcombe World & Collection Context, approved Canon Records, and Production Specs that contain narrative or evidentiary content. Pair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock, Material World, Quiet Composition, Atmospheric Grounding, and Period & Digital Exclusions when producing Volume I visual assets.\nThis module governs narrative evidence behavior only. It does not control rendering style, palette, material appearance, composition, technical production requirements, or component format.\nCanon Records and explicit Production Spec facts always take precedence. If this module suggests that an object could carry evidentiary meaning but canon defines it as ordinary or unrelated, preserve the canon interpretation.\nDo not allow Atmospheric Grounding or visual styling modules to turn neutral objects into implied clues merely for mood. Conversely, when approved canon establishes meaningful evidence, visual modules should support its presentation without obscuring or sensationalizing it."},{"key":"visual_palette","label":"Visual Palette","source":"World Bible","content":"Wychcombe is a Victorian estate given over to the quiet work of preservation — a place where botanical specimens are pressed and catalogued, where archives accumulate in climate-controlled stillness, and where scholarship proceeds at the pace of careful hands. The estate comprises a main house of honeyed stone, a sprawling greenhouse complex with ironwork gone soft with verdigris, outbuildings converted to workrooms and storage, and grounds that blur the line between curated garden and managed wilderness.\n\nThe atmosphere is one of **studied hush** — not silence, but the particular quiet of concentration. Gravel paths, the creak of wooden drawers, the scratch of nib on paper. Time moves differently here: seasons matter more than hours, and the work of a single afternoon might continue research begun three generations prior.\n\nVisually, Wychcombe exists in a palette of **aged naturals** — ivory paper darkened to cream, green in every muted variation from moss to fern, the warm brown of walnut cases and sepia ink, brass worn to a soft glow. Color is restrained; when it appears (a terracotta pot, a burgundy wax seal), it carries weight.\n\nThis is a world of **things that endure** and the people who tend them — where meaning accumulates through proximity, repetition, and care."},{"key":"prose_voice","label":"Prose Voice","source":"World Bible","content":"For Wychcombe, your prose voice should feel like **curated documentation** — authoritative but not clinical, careful but not cold. A few principles:\n\n**Measured precision over flourish.** Wychcombe is a place of preservation and scholarship. Sentences should feel considered, as if each word earns its place. Avoid purple prose; let material details carry emotional weight.\n\n**The curator's eye.** Describe objects, spaces, and people the way an archivist would — noting condition, provenance, wear patterns, what's present and what's conspicuously absent. \"The ledger's spine had cracked at the same page for decades\" tells us more than adjectives.\n\n**Restraint as atmosphere.** Wychcombe's mood lives in what's held back. Grief is a pressed flower tucked between pages. Tension is a key that fits no known lock. Let silence, omission, and the weight of accumulated years do narrative work.\n\n**Grounded sensory language.** Lean into your material palette — the smell of foxed paper, the cold of brass in morning light, condensation on greenhouse glass. These textures anchor the world more than abstract description.\n\n**Time as layered presence.** Write as if the past is always partially visible beneath the present — in habits, in repeated gestures, in objects that outlast their owners."},{"key":"atmospheric_notes","label":"Atmospheric Notes","source":"World Bible","content":"- Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight\n- Diffused morning or overcast light\n- Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate"},{"key":"material_world","label":"Material World","source":"World Bible","content":"| Paper | Fine fiber, subtle tooth, restrained foxing, use-based wear |\n| Wood | Visible grain, soft edge wear, matte finish |\n| Brass | Soft patina, warm highlights, no mirror polish |\n| Glass | Clouding, age, condensation, slight waviness |\n| Botanicals | Realistic fibers, veins, flattening, and discoloration |"},{"key":"style_system","label":"Style System","source":"Style Guide: Volume I Visual Language: The Curator’s Desk","content":"STYLE GUIDE: Victorian Garden Journals\nType: Full Suite\nScope: A complete style guide covering visual language, typography, tone of voice, and production constraints for this world. All production work references this document.\n\n## Visual Language\n\n### Colour Palette\nFamily\tPrimary Use\n--Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream\tPaper base, margins, writing areas\n--Moss / Olive / Fern\tBotanical accents and quiet patterning\n--Walnut / Charcoal / Sepia\tWood, linework, typography, structural depth\n--Weathered Brass\tTools, keys, hardware, restrained highlights\n--Terracotta / Muted Burgundy\tRare warm accents, pots, wax seals\n\n### Illustration Style\nIllustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals\n- Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight\n- Diffused morning or overcast light\n- Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate\n\n**Avoid** harsh spotlights, magical glow, god rays, fog, extreme chiaroscuro, cinematic contrast, and blue digital shadows.\n\n**Composition**\n\n1. One primary focal area\n2. One or two secondary narrative clusters\n3. Supporting depth materials\n4. Quiet zones for usability\n\nNegative space is required. Preserve margins, open wood grain, quiet linen, glass light, and writable zones.\n\nKeep critical elements inside a 0.25-inch visual safety zone for 12 × 12 papers.\n\n### Texture & Materials\nWatercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite\nAged rag paper integration, visible paper texture\nMuted natural pigments, low-to-moderate saturation\nSoftened edges, restrained engraved detail\nIllustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals\nSoft archival daylight or restrained period lamplight\nIntentional, reviewed labels and marks only\nPeriod-appropriate natural and archival materials\n\n## Typography & Tone\n\n### Typeface Direction\nPrimary: Cormorant Garamond / Playfair Display — old-style serif. Annotations: condensed or small-caps variant. Numerals: old-style figures. Display: Playfair Display SC, tracked. Secondary (UI/captions): Source Sans Pro. No geometric sans, rounded fonts, or any typeface that reads as modern in primary roles.\n\n### Type Hierarchy\nH1 Display: Playfair Display SC, 48pt+, tracked +200, Ink Navy. H2: Cormorant Garamond italic, 24pt. H3: Source Sans Pro SemiBold, 16pt. Body: Cormorant Garamond 11–12pt, 1.5× leading. Caption: Source Sans Pro 8pt. Micro: 7pt, tracked +100.\n\n### Prose Voice\nVictorian naturalist-scholar — learned, precise, quietly passionate. Sentences structured and complex but never opaque. Observation celebrated; enthusiasm restrained. Author has decades of field experience and knows the names of things. First person sparingly; third person for description; second person only as invitation.\nRegister: Personal / intimate\n\n### Reference Phrases\n\"A remarkable specimen, noteworthy for its…\" | \"The collector will observe that…\" | \"For those who find themselves drawn to…\" | \"There is much here that rewards patience.\" | \"Drawn from the archive of…\"\n\n### Words & Phrases to Avoid\navoid fantasy plants, malformed tools, heavy sepia, generic antique-shop clutter, random grunge, and any visual treatment that breaks the illustrated Victorian archival system.\n\n## Constraints\n\n### Negative Constraints (Do Not)\nPhotography, hyperreal 3D, glossy digital rendering\nClean digital white, polished synthetic surfaces\nCommercial saturation, bright synthetic color\nRazor-sharp photographic depth of field\nDSLR still life, cinematic rendering, game-concept-art appearance\nHigh-contrast commercial lighting, theatrical glow\nPseudo-text, barcodes, contemporary labels\nModern plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, synthetic ribbon\n\n### Production Rules\nAll artwork at 300 DPI for print, 144 DPI digital. CMYK for print (ISO Coated v2), sRGB for digital. Bleed: 3mm. Safe zone: 5mm. All fonts embedded and outlined before handoff. Paper simulation reduced for digital-only SKUs. All artwork approved in greyscale. All copy through tone review before publication.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I\nType: Atmospheric Grounding\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nCreate the scene within a quiet Victorian botanical and archival atmosphere shaped by scholarly intimacy, inherited responsibility, botanical care, gentle discovery, and methodical observation. The environment should feel accumulated through genuine use rather than manufactured as a decorative vintage setting. Favor aged rag paper, foxed parchment, worn walnut or oak, weathered brass, iron-gall ink, graphite, linen, natural twine, terracotta, old greenhouse glass, pressed botanicals, and restrained traces of soil or moss where appropriate. Use muted natural pigments and low-to-moderate saturation. Lighting should feel like soft greenhouse or muted library daylight, diffused morning or overcast light, with restrained period lamplight only when appropriate. Preserve quiet visual space and a sense of thoughtful observation. Avoid theatrical gothic atmosphere, fantasy, horror, luxury-stationery polish, generic antique-shop clutter, heavy sepia, random grunge, cinematic lighting, magical glow, glossy digital surfaces, and modern materials.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nQuiet Victorian botanical-archival atmosphere; scholarly intimacy and gentle discovery; accumulated rather than manufactured; muted natural pigments; aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, pressed botanicals and period materials; soft greenhouse or library daylight; restrained, tactile, observational, and non-theatrical.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera, Pocket, Envelope, Tag, Label, Hidden Discovery, Packaging and Marketing assets. Use wherever the Volume I visual atmosphere should remain consistent across the collection.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nSupports Canon. This module reinforces the approved Volume I visual atmosphere and collection identity but should not introduce, define, or alter canon facts, characters, places, events, objects, or discoveries.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nInject early in shared_prompt, after World & Collection Context and before component-specific composition, object, botanical, typography, or production instructions. More specific approved modules and Production Specification requirements take precedence where they add detail without contradicting the governing Style Guide.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nThe image feels quiet, tactile, and accumulated through use rather than staged or commercially styled.\nMaterials read distinctly and plausibly, such as aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, glass, terracotta, linen, or pressed botanicals when present.\nColor remains muted and natural, with cream, botanical greens, walnut, charcoal, sepia, and restrained warm accents rather than bright or synthetic color.\nLighting is soft and diffused, suggesting greenhouse daylight, library daylight, or restrained period lamplight without dramatic effects.\nThe overall atmosphere supports scholarly observation, botanical care, and gentle discovery while leaving sufficient visual quiet for the component's intended function.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nThe image appears photographic, hyperreal, glossy, digitally polished, or commercially staged rather than illustrated and tactile.\nLighting becomes cinematic, theatrical, magical, excessively dark, high-contrast, or dominated by glow or fog effects.\nColors become highly saturated, jewel-toned, synthetically green, heavily sepia, or otherwise inconsistent with the muted natural palette.\nThe scene becomes generic antique-shop clutter, excessive vintage decoration, gothic fantasy, horror, cottagecore spectacle, or luxury stationery.\nAging becomes uniform grunge or excessive distressing rather than plausible evidence of handling, use, and material age.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with World & Collection Context modules for Wychcombe and Volume I, component-specific composition modules, approved object or botanical modules, typography modules, and production/output modules. This module establishes the atmospheric baseline and should not introduce canon facts or determine component-specific content. The governing Style Guide remains authoritative for visual language. World and canon modules remain authoritative for factual content. More specific component modules may refine composition, objects, botanicals, typography, and functional requirements without contradicting the Style Guide. Production Specification requirements take precedence for the individual asset's dimensions, function, required content, and output constraints. If another module introduces conflicting atmosphere or visual treatment, resolve the conflict in favor of the governing Style Guide rather than blending incompatible styles.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I\nType: Style Reference\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Usage\nApply to all Volume I visual Production Specs that must follow the illustrated archival visual language. Attach when generating Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other illustrated Volume I assets. Use this module to prevent visual drift toward photography, hyperrealism, 3D rendering, cinematic imagery, or glossy digital artwork. This module governs rendering style only; component-specific composition and production behavior remain controlled by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec.\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nRender all applicable Volume I visual assets as unmistakably hand-illustrated Victorian botanical and archival artwork. The result must read as an illustration rather than a photograph, photographed still life, hyperreal 3D render, or cinematic digital scene.\nUse watercolor and gouache washes, fine ink linework, restrained graphite construction, subtle engraving-inspired hatching, visible aged rag-paper texture, softened illustrated edges, and muted natural pigments. Objects, botanicals, paper, wood, brass, glass, ink, textiles, labels, and other materials should be represented through illustrated marks, pigment, linework, and tactile paper integration rather than photographic surface realism.\nPreserve plausible material identity without allowing realism to override the medium. Wood grain should appear illustrated, brass should read through warm pigment and restrained highlights, glass through softened transparency and linework, botanicals through botanical drawing and painted form, and paper through visible fiber, tooth, foxing, and controlled aging.\nIf any substantial part of the image reads as photography, DSLR still life, hyperreal rendering, physically based rendering, glossy digital art, cinematic concept art, or commercial product photography, the output fails this module.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nHand-illustrated Victorian botanical-archival style only; watercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite, restrained engraving detail, visible aged paper texture, softened illustrated edges, muted natural pigments; plausible materials rendered through illustration rather than photographic realism; reject photography, hyperreal 3D, DSLR still life, cinematic rendering, glossy digital art, and commercial product styling.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply to all Volume I visual assets intended to use the illustrated archival language. Do not use for non-visual outputs or components intentionally governed by a different rendering style.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nCompatible with all canon dependency levels: None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module controls rendering style rather than canon content. It must not introduce, alter, interpret, or override canon.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nHigh priority. Inject early in the Style System section, after governing world and collection context and the linked Style Guide, but before Atmospheric Grounding, Material World, component-specific instructions, and Production Spec visual content. Treat the illustrated-not-photographic requirement as a mandatory style lock. Later modules may refine atmosphere, materials, composition, and subject matter but must not override this rendering requirement.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nThe entire asset immediately reads as a hand-illustrated Victorian botanical or archival work rather than a photograph.\nWatercolor or gouache washes, fine ink linework, graphite construction, or restrained engraving-style marks are visibly present and coherent across the asset.\nPaper, wood, brass, glass, botanicals, textiles, and other materials remain recognizable while clearly rendered through illustration rather than photographic surface realism.\nEdges, shadows, highlights, and textures have a softened illustrated quality with muted natural pigments and visible paper integration.\nAll major elements share the same illustrated visual language. No object appears pasted in, photographed, hyperreal, or rendered in a conflicting medium.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nAny major element reads as photography, a photographed still life, DSLR imagery, or photorealistic product photography.\nHyperrealistic 3D rendering, physically based materials, cinematic rendering, glossy digital surfaces, or artificial photographic depth of field are visible.\nObjects use realistic reflections, lighting, shadows, or surface textures that make them appear photographed rather than illustrated.\nThe asset mixes incompatible rendering treatments, such as illustrated botanicals beside photorealistic books, brass, paper, furniture, or tools.\nThe overall result resembles commercial vintage styling, game concept art, digital collage, or an antique-shop photograph instead of a unified illustrated archival work.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with Atmospheric Grounding for mood and lighting, Material World for material behavior, Negative Space modules for composition and usability, and Technical Constraint modules for production requirements and exclusions.\nThe linked Style Guide remains the governing visual-language reference. This module reinforces its illustrated rendering requirement and should not introduce independent subject matter, canon, composition, or production specifications.\nComponent Specs control component-specific format and production behavior. Production Specs control the individual asset's purpose, required content, composition, and canon references.\nIf another module requests photographic realism, hyperrealistic rendering, glossy digital treatment, cinematic rendering, or another incompatible medium, this Style Lock takes precedence for Volume I assets unless the governing Style Guide explicitly authorizes an exception.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Material World — Volume I\nType: Material World\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Usage\nApply to Volume I visual Production Specs where physical materials contribute to the illustrated archival world. Use this module to maintain consistent material character across paper, wood, brass, glass, ink, botanicals, textiles, terracotta, twine, wax, and related period-appropriate materials. This module governs material appearance, aging, texture, and physical character, not component layout, subject matter, or canon content.\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nRender Volume I materials as tactile, aged through plausible use, and integrated into the illustrated archival environment. Materials should show restrained variation, handling, age, and natural imperfection rather than artificial distressing.\nPaper: fine visible fiber and subtle tooth; antique ivory through foxed cream; restrained foxing, edge wear, folds, handling darkening, faint stains, and ink absorption where appropriate. Paper must remain usable and believable, never uniformly grungy, burned, heavily damaged, or artificially distressed.\nWood: walnut and other period-appropriate woods with visible illustrated grain, softened edges, matte surfaces, handling wear, and restrained age variation. Avoid glossy furniture finishes or exaggerated rustic damage.\nBrass: weathered brass with soft patina, warm restrained highlights, handling variation, and age appropriate to frequently used tools and fittings. Avoid mirror polish, chrome-like reflections, or bright metallic CGI effects.\nGlass: old greenhouse, bottle, lens, or cabinet glass with softened transparency, subtle clouding, slight waviness, condensation when contextually appropriate, and restrained reflections.\nInk and graphite: iron-gall or period-plausible ink with natural variation, slight fading, absorption into paper, fine linework, and occasional handling evidence. Graphite should remain soft, matte, and visibly drawn.\nBotanicals: pressed specimens, cuttings, seed heads, ferns, herbs, and botanical studies should show believable fibers, veins, flattening, drying, curling, and natural discoloration. Avoid plastic-looking foliage, fantasy plants, or unnaturally perfect specimens.\nTerracotta, twine, ribbon, linen, wax, and related materials: preserve recognizable material character through restrained texture, wear, pigment variation, fibers, impressions, and handling.\nMaterials should feel accumulated through work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, and ordinary use. Aging must communicate history through specific physical evidence rather than generic “vintage” decoration.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nTactile illustrated materials with restrained use-based aging: fine-fiber antique paper, worn matte walnut, softly patinated brass, slightly clouded period glass, absorbed and faded ink, soft graphite, naturally dried botanicals, weathered terracotta, fibrous twine and linen, and handled wax. Preserve believable material differences. Avoid uniform grunge, burned edges, glossy surfaces, chrome reflections, plastic foliage, synthetic textures, excessive distressing, and generic vintage effects.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply to Volume I visual components containing illustrated physical materials, surfaces, botanicals, archival objects, or paper-based elements. Material treatment should scale appropriately to the component rather than requiring every listed material to appear.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nCompatible with None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module governs the visual behavior of materials and aging rather than canon facts. It must not introduce specific objects, locations, dates, people, inscriptions, or historical claims that are not supplied by the Production Spec or linked canon.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nInject in the Style System section after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific content. The Style Lock determines the rendering medium; Material World determines how physical materials appear within that medium. Component Specs and Production Specs determine which materials and objects are actually required. Do not allow material instructions to override canon, composition, print requirements, or component-specific constraints.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nMaterials are visually distinct and immediately recognizable: paper reads as fibrous paper, wood as wood, brass as aged metal, glass as glass, and botanicals as preserved or living plant material.\nAging appears plausible and use-based, with restrained foxing, handling wear, softened edges, patina, fading, fibers, folds, and natural discoloration where appropriate.\nMaterial textures remain consistent with the illustrated archival style rather than becoming photographic or hyperrealistic.\nSurfaces show natural variation without excessive distressing; age and wear appear accumulated through handling, storage, botanical study, correspondence, and archival work.\nThe overall material palette feels cohesive, tactile, muted, and period-plausible while preserving meaningful differences between individual materials.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nUniform grunge, heavy staining, burned edges, excessive tearing, artificial aging, or distressed effects applied indiscriminately across the asset.\nGlossy, plastic, chrome-like, synthetic, or digitally polished surfaces inconsistent with the Volume I material language.\nMaterials lose their physical identity, such as brass appearing plastic, wood appearing digitally smooth, paper resembling flat beige texture, or botanicals appearing artificial.\nPhotorealistic textures, reflections, surface detail, or material rendering break the illustrated archival treatment.\nAging becomes decorative rather than plausible, making the asset resemble generic “vintage” craft imagery instead of objects with specific histories of use and preservation.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I, which governs rendering medium and takes precedence over any instruction that would make materials photographic or hyperrealistic. Pair with Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for lighting and environmental mood, and with Negative Space or Technical Constraint modules where applicable.\nMaterial World governs how specified materials appear; it does not determine which objects or materials must be present. Component Specs and Production Specs control required materials, objects, composition, format, and production behavior.\nCanon and Production Spec requirements take precedence when they establish a specific object's material or condition. Material World may enrich that material visually but must not change its identity, provenance, age, meaning, or canon significance.\nWhen instructions conflict, preserve this order: Canon and explicit Production Spec facts → Component production requirements → Illustrated Archival Style Lock → Material World → Atmospheric refinements.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Period & Digital Exclusions — Volume I\nType: Technical Constraint\nInjection point: negative_prompt\n\n## Usage\nApply to all Volume I visual Production Specs to prevent anachronistic, photographic, hyperreal, synthetic, digitally polished, or otherwise incompatible visual treatments. Use across Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Volume I visual components.\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nExclude visual elements and rendering treatments that break the illustrated Victorian archival system.\nDo not use photography, photographic still-life styling, DSLR aesthetics, photographic depth of field, hyperrealistic rendering, physically based rendering, glossy 3D materials, cinematic concept art, commercial product photography, game-rendering aesthetics, or polished digital illustration that reads as synthetic rather than tactile and archival.\nExclude modern or anachronistic materials and objects including plastics, ballpoint pens, contemporary electrical devices, modern packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic ribbon, modern office supplies, contemporary signage, digital screens, modern typography, and other clearly post-period visual language unless explicitly required by the Production Spec.\nAvoid clean digital white, neon color, bright synthetic saturation, chrome-like reflections, mirror-polished metal, hard digital gradients, blue digital shadows, artificial glow, god rays, fog effects, theatrical lighting, extreme chiaroscuro, cinematic contrast, or any treatment that turns quiet archival imagery into spectacle.\nDo not use generic antique-shop clutter, luxury stationery styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, horror aesthetics, steampunk machinery, faux-vintage craft-store decoration, random grunge, burned edges, uniform distressing, or arbitrary “old-fashioned” embellishment.\nDo not generate pseudo-text, modern labels, unsupported logos, invented institutional marks, fake historical branding, or decorative text that appears factual but lacks an approved source.\nThe goal is not merely to appear old. The result must remain materially plausible, illustrated, restrained, and consistent with the governed Volume I visual language.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nExclude photography, photorealism, DSLR still life, hyperreal 3D, cinematic rendering, glossy digital surfaces, modern objects, plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic materials, modern typography, neon color, chrome reflections, theatrical glow, extreme contrast, pseudo-text, generic antique-shop clutter, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, steampunk, random grunge, burned edges, and unsupported historical branding.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply to all Volume I visual components governed by the illustrated archival language. Do not attach only when a Production Spec explicitly requires a different visual period or rendering system.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nCompatible with None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module suppresses incompatible visual treatments and anachronisms rather than supplying canon content. It must never exclude an object, material, inscription, or detail explicitly required by accepted canon or the Production Spec.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nHigh priority. Inject into the negative_prompt after the positive Style System instructions have established the intended illustrated archival treatment. Apply alongside Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I as its exclusion guardrail. Maintain these exclusions throughout generation, while allowing explicit canon, Production Spec, and Component Spec requirements to take precedence where they intentionally require an otherwise excluded element.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nThe asset contains no obvious modern, digital, synthetic, photographic, or anachronistic visual elements unless explicitly required by the Production Spec.\nThe image remains convincingly within the illustrated Victorian archival language, with period-plausible objects, materials, typography, lighting, and surface treatment.\nDigital generation artifacts are visually suppressed: no glossy CGI surfaces, photographic depth of field, artificial glow, cinematic effects, or synthetic-looking textures.\nAging and historical character feel restrained and materially plausible rather than relying on generic vintage effects.\nAny visible text, labels, marks, or institutional identifiers appear intentional and supported by the Production Spec or linked canon rather than invented decorative pseudo-history.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nPhotography, photorealism, DSLR still-life treatment, hyperreal 3D rendering, cinematic lighting, glossy CGI materials, photographic depth of field, or other clearly digital rendering behavior.\nModern or anachronistic objects or materials such as plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, contemporary packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic office supplies, or modern signage unless explicitly required.\nNeon or synthetic color, chrome-like reflections, artificial glow, dramatic fog, god rays, extreme cinematic contrast, or other theatrical effects.\nGeneric antique-shop clutter, faux-vintage craft styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, steampunk imagery, excessive grunge, burned edges, or indiscriminate distressing.\nPseudo-text, invented historical branding, unsupported institutional marks, fake labels, or other visual information that could be mistaken for canon.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair directly with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I. The Style Lock establishes the required positive rendering language; this module suppresses incompatible alternatives.\nPair with Material World — Volume I so permitted materials retain plausible age, wear, and physical character without drifting into glossy, synthetic, or generic vintage treatments. Pair with Quiet Composition — Volume I and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I without allowing atmospheric effects to introduce cinematic lighting, fog, glow, or theatrical contrast.\nThis module must not suppress an element explicitly required by accepted canon, the Production Spec, or the Component Spec. When an otherwise excluded element is intentionally required, the explicit requirement takes precedence.\nThis module controls exclusions only. It must not invent replacement objects, historical facts, text, branding, or canon details.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Quiet Composition — Volume I\nType: Negative Space\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Usage\nApply to Volume I visual Production Specs where composition must preserve visual quiet, usable negative space, restrained density, and clear hierarchy. Use across Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers, with the exact amount and location of quiet space determined by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec.\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nPreserve quiet visual space as an intentional part of the composition rather than treating empty areas as unfinished.\nEstablish a clear hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate, limited supporting clusters, and sufficient breathing room between visual elements. Avoid filling every available area with decoration, objects, botanicals, text, texture, aging, or archival marks.\nQuiet space may take the form of open paper, restrained wood grain, low-density linen or material texture, pale margins, softly illuminated glass, uncluttered writing areas, or other contextually appropriate surfaces.\nMaintain visual balance rather than symmetry for its own sake. Allow areas of lower detail to support more active areas and preserve the intended function of the component.\nWhen the Production Spec defines a writing-space percentage or usability requirement, treat that requirement as mandatory. The quiet area must remain genuinely usable rather than merely lighter in color while still crowded with detail.\nDecorative elements should not migrate into functional writing, cutting, trim, binding, or safe areas unless explicitly permitted.\nNegative space should feel naturally integrated into the visual world, not digitally erased, artificially blank, or disconnected from the rest of the composition.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nPreserve intentional visual quiet and usable negative space. One clear focal area where appropriate, restrained supporting clusters, low-to-moderate density, and genuine breathing room. Do not fill empty areas with decorative clutter. Respect writing-space, cut, trim, binding, and safe-area requirements. Quiet space should feel naturally integrated into the illustrated environment.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply wherever Volume I assets need restrained visual density, usable negative space, clear hierarchy, and protection against overfilling. The exact amount and placement of quiet space must follow the linked Component Spec and Production Spec.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nCompatible with None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module governs composition and visual breathing room only. It must not introduce, remove, reinterpret, or override canon content.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nnject in the Style System after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and Material World — Volume I, and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific composition/content instructions. Treat Production Spec writing-space, usability, trim, cut, and safe-area requirements as mandatory. Component-specific composition rules may refine where quiet space appears, but should not eliminate required negative space.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nThe composition has a clear visual hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate and restrained supporting elements.\nMeaningful areas of genuine visual quiet remain open and usable rather than being filled with decoration, text, botanicals, objects, or heavy texture.\nActive and quiet areas feel intentionally balanced, allowing the viewer’s eye to rest and move naturally through the composition.\nAny writing-space percentage or functional area required by the Production Spec remains visibly usable and substantially unobstructed.\nNegative space feels integrated into the illustrated environment through paper, subtle material texture, restrained surface detail, or other contextually appropriate treatment rather than appearing artificially blank.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nThe composition fills nearly every available area with objects, botanicals, text, marks, aging, patterns, or decorative detail.\nRequired writing or functional space is visually occupied, heavily textured, interrupted, or reduced below the Production Spec requirement.\nToo many competing focal areas, clusters, or high-detail elements create visual noise or unclear hierarchy.\nNegative space appears as an unnatural empty hole, digitally erased region, hard-edged blank area, or disconnected background.\nDecorative elements intrude into required trim, cut, binding, safe, writing, or usability zones without explicit instruction.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I for rendering consistency, Material World — Volume I for believable low-density surfaces and textures, and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for environmental mood.\nQuiet Composition governs visual density, hierarchy, breathing room, and usability. It does not determine required subjects, objects, canon content, or exact component dimensions.\nComponent Specs and Production Specs take precedence for explicit writing-space percentages, safe zones, trim areas, orientation, layout requirements, and required content. Quiet Composition should help satisfy those requirements rather than replace them.\nRequired narrative or canon elements must not be removed merely to create more negative space. Instead, simplify secondary decoration, reduce density, consolidate clusters, or reposition nonessential elements.\nWhen instructions conflict, preserve this order: required canon/content → production and component requirements → required usability/negative space → style and material refinements → optional decorative detail."},{"key":"component_requirements","label":"Component Requirements","source":"Component Specification: Decorative Paper — Core Component Specification","content":"Next up: Decorative Paper. This should sit between Hero Paper and a simple repeating background. Its job is to provide a strong coordinated surface for journaling and layering without carrying a major narrative scene.\nName\nDecorative Paper — Core Component Specification\nComponent Type\nDecorative Paper\nSpecification Details\nPURPOSE\nDefines the reusable production requirements for Decorative Paper components. Decorative Paper provides a coordinated full-page visual surface for journaling, layering, matting, collage, and related paper-craft uses. It supports the collection’s visual language without functioning as a primary narrative scene. Collection-specific style, palette, motifs, canon, objects, typography, and atmosphere are inherited from the linked Style Guide, Canon Records, Prompt Modules, and individual Production Specification.\nFORMAT & COMPOSITION\n• Design as a complete full-page composition suitable for use at finished print size.\n• The page should remain visually useful when partially covered, cropped, torn, folded, or layered beneath other journal components.\n• Favor distributed visual rhythm over a dominant central focal point.\n• Decorative interest may concentrate toward edges, corners, bands, or selected areas when defined by the Production Specification.\n• Preserve sufficient visual quiet so other journal elements can be layered over the paper without excessive competition.\n• Avoid composition that reads as a Hero Paper, illustrated scene, poster, or single large standalone artwork unless explicitly required.\nVISUAL DENSITY\n• Maintain a controlled balance between decorated and quiet areas.\n• Do not fill every available area with motifs, objects, texture, or marks.\n• Large motifs should not dominate the page unless intentionally specified.\n• Fine details must remain visually coherent at final print size.\n• Decorative density should support practical journal use.\nCROPPING & LAYERING\n• The design must tolerate common cropping from any edge without destroying its visual logic.\n• Avoid placing all meaningful visual interest in a single crop-sensitive location.\n• Essential motifs should not depend upon seeing the entire sheet.\n• Cropped fragments should continue to feel intentional when used as mats, pockets, strips, torn edges, or layered backgrounds.\nPATTERN & REPEAT BEHAVIOR\n• Decorative Paper may use scattered motifs, directional compositions, borders, botanical arrangements, archival marks, surface treatments, restrained patterns, or other structures defined by the Production Specification.\n• Do not assume seamless repetition unless explicitly required.\n• Repeated motifs should vary naturally unless a formal repeating pattern is intentionally specified.\n• Avoid obvious mechanical duplication that makes illustrated elements appear digitally stamped.\nMATERIAL & SURFACE\n• Surface treatment should reproduce convincingly as printed material at final physical size.\n• Paper grain, foxing, fading, stains, fibers, ink, botanical impressions, handwriting traces, or other material effects should remain restrained and intentional.\n• Aging must suggest use, storage, handling, or material history rather than generic digital distress.\n• Avoid uniform grunge, excessive staining, burned edges, artificial noise, or distress applied equally across the entire sheet.\nUSABILITY\n• Decorative Paper must remain functional for writing, layering, collage, pockets, mounting photographs or cards, and other uses defined by the Production Specification.\n• Maintain usable areas of lower visual density.\n• Do not require the user to preserve the entire sheet for the design to make sense.\n• The paper should coordinate with more narrative components without competing with them for attention.\nTEXT & CANON\n• Readable text is closed-world content and must be explicitly supplied or supported by approved governed sources.\n• Do not invent names, dates, correspondence, locations, botanical identifications, quotations, signatures, document contents, historical claims, or other factual-looking text.\n• When text-like texture is desired without governed wording, use non-readable handwriting traces, faded partial marks, abstract ink forms, blank structures, or other non-semantic treatments.\n• Decorative use of canon material must not accidentally imply new facts or duplicate unique narrative evidence.\nBLEED & EDGE SAFETY\n• Background color, texture, pattern, and non-critical decorative imagery may extend through bleed.\n• Designs intended to reach the finished edge must continue naturally through the production bleed area.\n• Keep critical readable or canon-dependent content inside the safe area.\n• Natural cropping of non-critical decorative motifs at sheet edges is permitted where visually intentional.\nPRINT & OUTPUT\n• Final dimensions, resolution, DPI, color mode, bleed, safe zones, front/back behavior, and export requirements inherit from the linked Print Target.\n• Artwork must retain useful texture, linework, and tonal separation at final physical size.\n• Avoid effects dependent upon screen luminosity, extreme contrast, photographic depth of field, or details too fine to reproduce reliably in print.\nGOVERNANCE\nThis Component Spec does not define the individual paper’s motif, collection-specific palette, narrative theme, botanical subject, recurring objects, readable wording, canon content, or decorative density.\nThose requirements belong to the appropriate governed records and individual Production Specification.\nWhen requirements conflict, apply specificity in this order:\n1. Print Target technical requirements\n2. Individual Production Specification\n3. Approved canon requirements\n4. Linked Style Guide\n5. This Component Spec\nA Production Specification may refine Decorative Paper for a particular asset but should not silently redefine the fundamental production behavior of the component."},{"key":"shared_prompt","label":"Shared Prompt","source":"Prompt Payload","content":"Create a coordinated Volume I decorative paper belonging to Wychcombe and The Curator’s Desk component set. Treat the entire sheet as a designed paper surface rather than a depicted room, desk scene, or collection of separate ephemera."},{"key":"front_prompt","label":"Front Prompt","source":"Prompt Payload","content":"Produce one complete square decorative paper design."},{"key":"negative_prompt","label":"Negative Prompt","source":"Prompt Payload","content":"Do not depict a complete desk scene, room interior, greenhouse scene, open journal as the primary subject, still-life tableau, or central narrative vignette."},{"key":"canon_policy","label":"Canon Policy","source":"Canon Records: The Curators","content":"Canon Dependency: Supports Canon\nCanon Record: The Curators (accepted)\nNarrative Details: The Curators are a Wychcombe institution or role-group responsible for preserving, interpreting, arranging, or controlling access to records and objects.\nEmotional Register: Withholding"},{"key":"print_and_output_requirements","label":"Print And Output Requirements","source":"Production Specification","content":"Orientation: square\nFront/Back Style: single-sided\nWriting Space: 0%\nReview Criteria: PASS / FAIL — Does the finished piece read first as a decorative journal paper rather than a Hero Paper, desk scene, ephemera sheet, or collection of separate objects?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the paper unmistakably belong to Volume I and The Curator’s Desk visual family while maintaining its own quieter, background-oriented function?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Are botanical studies integrated into the paper surface with natural variation in scale, placement, completeness, and density rather than forming an obvious mechanical repeat?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the composition avoid a dominant central focal scene while still maintaining intentional visual rhythm and areas of greater and lesser detail?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Is approximately 15% of the sheet clearly usable quiet space, with enough additional low-detail area for layering, writing, cutting, and journal use?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Do paper, ink, graphite, botanical material, aging, and other surfaces feel tactile, illustrated, restrained, and consistent with the linked Volume I material language?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Do archival marks, catalog structures, specimen traces, or annotation elements function as subtle evidence rather than decorative clutter?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Is aging believable and use-based rather than uniform grunge, heavy staining, burned edges, excessive distressing, or artificial vintage effects?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the artwork avoid photography, photorealism, glossy digital rendering, cinematic lighting, synthetic surfaces, modern graphic-design conventions, and commercial scrapbook styling?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Is the design free of pseudo-text, prominent gibberish handwriting, unsupported dates, invented documents, unsupported historical claims, characters, events, discoveries, or other accidental canon?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Are botanical subjects plausible within the established Volume I vocabulary rather than fantasy plants, generic cottagecore florals, or decorative bouquets?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the finished sheet coordinate naturally with the Curator’s Desk Hero Paper and Ephemera Sheet without copying their compositions?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the paper remain visually useful at full 12 × 12 scale and when cropped into smaller journal sections?\n\nPASS / FAIL — Does the final result feel accumulated through observation, preservation, and use rather than manufactured as a modern faux-Victorian pattern?"},{"key":"world_rules","label":"World Rules","source":"World Bible","content":"Discoveries in Wychcombe emerge through evidence, relationship, sequence, place, and interpretation rather than arbitrary revelation.\nThe Wychcombe timeline is a layered chronology that distinguishes dated fact, approximate sequence, remembered history, disputed accounts, and narrative reveal order.\nHidden Discoveries tracks concealed material, layered clues, recoverable evidence, and revelations that become visible through sequence, context, or physical interaction.\nIn Wychcombe, memory lives through people, places, objects, habits, documents, omissions, and repeated acts of preservation."}],"generated_filename":null,"notion_upload_id":null},{"id":"572217e8-278b-419e-9894-f5467b9c987b","production_spec_id":"0c51adac-c7cd-4416-a69c-923feaf058c8","operation":"validate_and_compile","status":"validation_failed","dry_run":false,"payload_version":"PP-2.0","compiled_prompt":"[MANDATORY RENDERING STYLE]\nThe governing Style Guide rendering medium is mandatory and overrides lower-priority rendering directions. STYLE GUIDE: Victorian Garden Journals Type: Full Suite Scope: A complete style guide covering visual language, typography, tone of voice, and production constraints for this world. All production work references this document. ## Visual Language ### Colour Palette Family Primary Use Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream Paper base, margins, writing areas Moss / Olive / Fern Botanical accents and quiet patterning Walnut / Charcoal / Sepia Wood, linework, typography, structural depth Weathered Brass Tools, keys, hardware, restrained highlights Terracotta / Muted Burgundy Rare warm accents, pots, wax seals ### Illustration Style Illustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight Diffused morning or overcast light Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate Composition** One primary focal area One or two secondary narrative clusters Supporting depth materials Quiet zones for usability Negative space is required. Preserve margins, open wood grain, quiet linen, glass light, and writable zones. Keep critical elements inside a 0.25-inch visual safety zone for 12 × 12 papers. ### Texture & Materials Watercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite Aged rag paper integration, visible paper texture Muted natural pigments, low-to-moderate saturation Softened edges, restrained engraved detail Soft archival daylight or restrained period lamplight Intentional, reviewed labels and marks only Period-appropriate natural and archival materials ## Typography & Tone ### Typeface Direction Primary: Cormorant Garamond / Playfair Display — old-style serif. Annotations: condensed or small-caps variant. Numerals: old-style figures. Display: Playfair Display SC, tracked. Seco