red brass, iron-gall ink, graphite, linen, natural twine, terracotta, old greenhouse glass, pressed botanicals, and restrained traces of soil or moss where appropriate. Use muted natural pigments and low-to-moderate saturation. Lighting should feel like soft greenhouse or muted library daylight, diffused morning or overcast light, with restrained period lamplight only when appropriate. Preserve quiet visual space and a sense of thoughtful observation. ### Alternative / Condensed Quiet Victorian botanical-archival atmosphere scholarly intimacy and gentle discovery accumulated rather than manufactured muted natural pigments aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, pressed botanicals and period materials soft greenhouse or library daylight restrained, tactile, observational, and non-theatrical. ## Targeting ### Applicable Component Types Hero Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera, Pocket, Envelope, Tag, Label, Hidden Discovery, Packaging and Marketing assets. Use wherever the Volume I visual atmosphere should remain consistent across the collection. ### Canon Dependency Hint Supports Canon. ### Priority / Injection Order Inject early in shared_prompt, after World & Collection Context and before component-specific composition, object, botanical, typography, or production instructions. ## Quality & Interactions ### Good Output Indicators The image feels quiet, tactile, and accumulated through use rather than staged or commercially styled. Materials read distinctly and plausibly, such as aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, glass, terracotta, linen, or pressed botanicals when present. Color remains muted and natural, with cream, botanical greens, walnut, charcoal, sepia, and restrained warm accents rather than bright or synthetic color. The overall atmosphere supports scholarly observation, botanical care, and gentle discovery while leaving sufficient visual quiet for the component's intended function. ### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present) Lighting becomes cinematic, theatrical, magical, excessively dark, high-contrast, or dominated by glow or fog effects. Colors become highly saturated, jewel-toned, synthetically green, heavily sepia, or otherwise inconsistent with the muted natural palette. The scene becomes generic antique-shop clutter, excessive vintage decoration, gothic fantasy, horror, cottagecore spectacle, or luxury stationery. Aging becomes uniform grunge or excessive distressing rather than plausible evidence of handling, use, and material age. ### Module Interactions Pair with World & Collection Context modules for Wychcombe and Volume I, component-specific composition modules, approved object or botanical modules, typography modules, and production/output modules. The governing Style Guide remains authoritative for visual language. World and canon modules remain authoritative for factual content. Production Specification requirements take precedence for the individual asset's dimensions, function, required content, and output constraints. If another module introduces conflicting atmosphere or visual treatment, resolve the conflict in favor of the governing Style Guide rather than blending incompatible styles. PROMPT MODULE: Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I Type: Style Reference ## Usage Apply to all Volume I visual Production Specs that must follow the illustrated archival visual language. Attach when generating Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other illustrated Volume I assets. This module governs rendering style only component-specific composition and production behavior remain controlled by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. Render all applicable Volume I visual assets as unmistakably hand-illustrated Victorian botanical and archival artwork. Use watercolor and gouache washes, fine ink linework, restrained graphite construction, subtle engraving-inspired hatching, visible aged rag-pap