sical context, and a reason for surviving. Ordinary work may produce meaningful evidence. Gardening, cataloguing, correspondence, printing, storage, repairs, bookkeeping, collecting, preservation, and other repeated activities can leave traces that later acquire significance. Records may be incomplete, memories partial, labels mistaken, chronology approximate, and interpretations disputed. Visual assets should imply narrative through carefully chosen material relationships rather than excessive symbolic decoration. Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Those must come from the Production Spec or approved canon. ### Alternative / Condensed Wychcombe reveals meaning through evidence, provenance, material condition, sequence, place, omission, and careful interpretation. Let objects and records suggest history through plausible traces of use and relationship. Favor subtle recoverable evidence over theatrical clues. Preserve uncertainty when canon is incomplete. ## Targeting ### Applicable Component Types Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Wychcombe visual or narrative components where objects, documents, botanicals, places, archival material, or physical traces contribute to storytelling. Apply only where evidence or narrative context is relevant ### Canon Dependency Hint Best suited to Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining assets. The stronger the canon dependency, the more strictly evidence must trace back to approved records. ### Priority / Injection Order Inject early in World & Collection Context after foundational Wychcombe world rules and before Style System modules, component-specific content, and Production Spec scene instructions. Canon Records and explicit Production Spec facts remain authoritative. ## Quality & Interactions ### Good Output Indicators Narrative interest emerges from plausible objects, records, materials, placement, wear, omission, or relationships rather than obvious theatrical clues. Evidence appears to have existed naturally within the world before the viewer encountered it and has believable provenance or physical context. Ordinary work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, cataloguing, or material history contributes naturally to the sense of story. ### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present) Invented names, dates, discoveries, secret messages, ownership claims, relationships, historical events, document contents, or other unsupported canon appear merely to make the asset interesting. Obvious mystery clichés dominate the scene, such as conveniently exposed secret letters, dramatic coded messages, glowing clues, conspicuous hidden compartments, or excessive symbolic objects. Every object is treated as significant, creating clue clutter rather than a believable lived environment. Evidence lacks provenance, context, physical plausibility, or a reason for being present and preserved. Ambiguous or incomplete canon is converted into definitive claims instead of remaining uncertain, disputed, approximate, or unresolved. ### Module Interactions Pair with Wychcombe World & Collection Context, approved Canon Records, and Production Specs that contain narrative or evidentiary content. Pair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock, Material World, Quiet Composition, Atmospheric Grounding, and Period & Digital Exclusions when producing Volume I visual assets. This module governs narrative evidence behavior only. Canon Records and explicit Production Spec facts always take precedence. If this module suggests that an object could carry evidentiary meaning but canon defines it as ordinary or unrelated, preserve the canon interpretation.\n\n[COMPOSITION]\nProduce one complete square decorative paper design.\n\n[REQUIRED OBJECTS AND CONTENT]\nUse an Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream paper ground with a restrained arrangement of period-appropriate botanical study imagery. Include: fine botanical line drawings or softly illustrated plant studies ferns, herbs, seed heads, leaves, or garden specimens appropriate to the established Volume I vocabulary subtle archival or cataloguing marks where appropriate faint graphite, sepia ink, or muted botanical pigment restrained foxing, handling wear, paper variation, and other material aging meaningful open areas between illustrated elements Botanical elements should vary naturally in scale, orientation, completeness, and density. Some may appear as complete studies while others may appear cropped, faded, partial, or ghosted into the paper surface. Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Otherwise use non-readable marks, numbering fragments, linework, or blank label structures rather than invented text. Orientation\nNext up: Decorative Paper. This should sit between Hero Paper and a simple repeating background. Name Decorative Paper — Core Component Specification Component Type Decorative Paper Specification Details PURPOSE Defines the reusable production requirements for Decorative Paper components. Decorative Paper provides a coordinated full-page visual surface for journaling, layering, matting, collage, and related paper-craft uses. Collection-specific style, palette, motifs, canon, objects, typography, and atmosphere are inherited from the linked Style Guide, Canon Records, Prompt Modules, and individual Production Specification. FORMAT & COMPOSITION Design as a complete full-page composition suitable for use at finished print size. The page should remain visually useful when partially covered, cropped, torn, folded, or layered 