language. This module suppresses incompatible visual treatments and anachronisms rather than supplying canon content. Inject into the negative_prompt after the positive Style System instructions have established the intended illustrated archival treatment. Apply alongside Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I as its exclusion guardrail. Maintain these exclusions throughout generation, while allowing explicit canon, Production Spec, and Component Spec requirements to take precedence where they intentionally require an otherwise excluded element. The image remains convincingly within the illustrated Victorian archival language, with period-plausible objects, materials, typography, lighting, and surface treatment. Aging and historical character feel restrained and materially plausible rather than relying on generic vintage effects. Any visible text, labels, marks, or institutional identifiers appear intentional and supported by the Production Spec or linked canon rather than invented decorative pseudo-history. Modern or anachronistic objects or materials such as plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, contemporary packaging, barcodes, QR codes, synthetic office supplies, or modern signage unless explicitly required. Neon or synthetic color, chrome-like reflections, artificial glow, dramatic fog, god rays, extreme cinematic contrast, or other theatrical effects. Generic antique-shop clutter, faux-vintage craft styling, cottagecore spectacle, gothic fantasy, steampunk imagery, excessive grunge, burned edges, or indiscriminate distressing. Pseudo-text, invented historical branding, unsupported institutional marks, fake labels, or other visual information that could be mistaken for canon. Pair directly with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I. The Style Lock establishes the required positive rendering language this module suppresses incompatible alternatives. When an otherwise excluded element is intentionally required, the explicit requirement takes precedence. This module controls exclusions only. PROMPT MODULE: Quiet Composition — Volume I Type: Negative Space Apply to Volume I visual Production Specs where composition must preserve visual quiet, usable negative space, restrained density, and clear hierarchy. Use across Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers, with the exact amount and location of quiet space determined by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. Preserve quiet visual space as an intentional part of the composition rather than treating empty areas as unfinished. Establish a clear hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate, limited supporting clusters, and sufficient breathing room between visual elements. Quiet space may take the form of open paper, restrained wood grain, low-density linen or material texture, pale margins, softly illuminated glass, uncluttered writing areas, or other contextually appropriate surfaces. Maintain visual balance rather than symmetry for its own sake. Allow areas of lower detail to support more active areas and preserve the intended function of the component. When the Production Spec defines a writing-space percentage or usability requirement, treat that requirement as mandatory. The quiet area must remain genuinely usable rather than merely lighter in color while still crowded with detail. Preserve intentional visual quiet and usable negative space. One clear focal area where appropriate, restrained supporting clusters, low-to-moderate density, and genuine breathing room. Respect writing-space, cut, trim, binding, and safe-area requirements. Quiet space should feel naturally integrated into the illustrated environment. Apply wherever Volume I assets need restrained visual density, usable negative space, clear hierarchy, and protection against overfilling. The exact amount and placement of quiet space must follow the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. This module governs composition and visual breathing room only. nject in the Style System after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and Material World — Volume I, and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific composition/content instructions. Treat Production Spec writing-space, usability, trim, cut, and safe-area requirements as mandatory. The composition has a clear visual hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate and restrained supporting elements. Meaningful areas of genuine visual quiet remain open and usable rather than being filled with decoration, text, botanicals, objects, or heavy texture. Active and quiet areas feel intentionally balanced, allowing the viewer’s eye to rest and move naturally through the composition. Any writing-space percentage or functional area required by the Production Spec remains visibly usable and substantially unobstructed. Negative space feels integrated into the illustrated environment through paper, subtle material texture, restrained surface detail, or other contextually appropriate treatment rather than appearing artificially blank. The composition fills nearly every available area with objects, botanicals, text, marks, aging, patterns, or decorative detail. Required writing or functional space is visually occupied, heavily textured, interrupted, or reduced below the Production Spec requirement. Too many competing focal areas, clusters, or high-detail elements create visual noise or unclear hierarchy. Negative space appears as an unnatural empty hole, digitally erased region, hard-edged blank area, or disconnected background. Pair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I for rendering consistency, Material World — Volume I for believable low-density surfaces and textures, and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for environmental mood. Quiet Composition governs visual density, hierarchy, breathing room, and usability. Component Specs and Production Specs take precedence for explicit writing-space percentages, safe zones, trim areas, orientation, layout requirements, and required content. Quiet Composition should help satisfy those requirements rather than replace them. Instead, simplify secondary decoration, reduce density, consolidate clusters, or reposition nonessential elements. When instructions conflict, preserve this order: required canon/content → production and component requirements → required usability/negative space → style and material refinements → optional decorative detail. This must read unmistakably as artwork in the required medium, never as a photograph or photographed still life. Subject realism, plausible materials, detail, and natural lighting describe content within that medium; they never replace the governing medium. If any part of the image reads as photography, DSLR still-life photography, hyperreal 3D, cinematic rendering, or glossy digital realism, the result fails the style requirement.\n\n[GOVERNED READABLE TEXT]\nReadable text is closed-world content. Do not invent, infer, complete, or improvise any readable wording. Render only exact wording explicitly authorized below by the Production Specification or an Accepted Canon Record. All unspecified text-bearing areas must be blank ruled fields, empty label areas, empty catalog fields, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Do not generate unsupported names, dates, addresses, botanical identifications, specimen numbers, correspondence prose, ledger entries, geographic or map labels, historical claims, signatures, initials, or institutional information. No readable strings are authorized; all text-bearing areas must remain blank or non-readable.\n\n[ASSET AND SCENE]\nAsset: Curator’s Desk Botanical Study — Decorative Paper (Decorative Paper)\nWorld: Wychcombe; Volume: Volume I: The Curator’s Desk.\nCreate a richly illustrated but restrained decorative paper that translates the Curator’s Desk visual language into a repeatable, background-oriented composition. The paper should feel derived from accumulated botanical study materials, specimen sketches, archival markings, and aged paper surfaces rather than from conventional modern scrapbook pattern design. The overall effect should be quiet, scholarly, botanical, tactile, and collected over time.\nRepresent the visual residue of botanical observation and archival work within the Curator’s world. Rather than depicting a specific event or complete scene, the paper should suggest fragments of study accumulated through repeated observation: botanical drawings, specimen traces, faint annotations, catalog marks, paper impressions, and other restrained evidence of curatorial practice. The paper supports the world through atmosphere and material evidence rather than introducing new narrative facts.\nCreate a coordinated Volume I decorative paper belonging to Wychcombe and The Curator’s Desk component set. Treat the entire sheet as a designed paper surface rather than a depicted room, desk scene, or collection of separate ephemera.\nPROMPT MODULE: Curatorial Evidence Language — Wychcombe Type: Canon Anchor Injection point: shared_prompt ## Usage Apply to Wychcombe Production Specs and generated assets where narrative meaning is communicated through objects, documents, botanical material, correspondence, archives, places, traces of use, omissions, sequence, provenance, or physical evidence. Use this module to make discoveries and storytelling feel native to Wychcombe rather than artificially dramatic. This module governs how evidence communicates meaning ## Prompt Content ### Primary In Wychcombe, narrative meaning should emerge through evidence, relationship, sequence, place, provenance, material condition, and interpretation rather than arbitrary revelation or decorative mystery. Treat objects, documents, botanicals, buildings, correspondence, tools, labels, records, traces of handling, repairs, wear, storage, placement, absence, and repetition as potential evidence. Their significance should come from how they relate to people, places, time, and other evidence rather than from theatrical presentation. Favor the curator’s method: observe first, infer carefully, preserve uncertainty where evidence is incomplete, and distinguish what is known from what is suggested. Discoveries should feel recoverable because evidence existed before the viewer encountered it. Evidence should have plausible provenance, physical context, and a reason for surviving. Ordinary work may produce meaningful evidence. Gardening, cataloguing, correspondence, printing, storage, repairs, bookkeeping, collecting, preservation, and other repeated activities can leave traces that later acquire significance. Records may be incomplete, memories partial, labels mistaken, chronology approximate, and interpretations disputed. Visual assets should imply narrative through carefully chosen material relationships rather than excessive symbolic decoration. A small number of meaningful details is preferable to a scene overloaded with obvious “clues.” Those must come from the Production Spec or approved canon. ### Alternative / Condensed Wychcombe reveals meaning through evidence, provenance, material condition, sequence, place, omission, and careful interpretation. Let objects and records suggest history through plausible traces of use and relationship. Favor subtle recoverable evidence over theatrical clues. Preserve uncertainty when canon is incomplete. ## Targeting ### Applicable Component Types Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Wychcombe visual or narrative components where objects, documents, botanicals, places, archival material, or physical traces contribute to storytelling. Apply only where evidence or narrative context is relevant ### Canon Dependency Hint Best suited to Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining assets. The stronger the canon dependency, the more strictly evidence must trace back to approved records. ### Priority / Injection Order Inject early in World & Collection Context after foundational Wychcombe world rules and before Style System modules, component-specific content, and Production Spec scene instructions. Canon Records and explicit Production Spec facts remain authoritative. ## Quality & Interactions ### Good Output Indicators Narrative interest emerges from plausible objects, records, materials, placement, wear, omission, or relationships rather than obvious theatrical clues. Evidence appears to have existed naturally within the world before the viewer encountered it and has believable provenance or physical context. Ordinary work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, cataloguing, or material history contributes naturally to the sense of story. ### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present) Invented names, dates, discoveries, secret messages, ownership claims, relationships, historical events, document contents, or other unsupported canon appear merely to make the asset interesting. Obvious mystery clichés dominate the scene, such as conveniently exposed secret letters, dramatic coded messages, glowing clues, conspicuous hidden compartments, or excessive symbolic objects. Every object is treated as significant, creating clue clutter rather than a believable lived environment. Evidence lacks provenance, context, physical plausibility, or a reason for being present and preserved. Ambiguous or incomplete canon is converted into definitive claims instead of remaining uncertain, disputed, approximate, or unresolved. ### Module Interactions Pair with Wychcombe World & Collection Context, approved Canon Records, and Production Specs that contain narrative or evidentiary content. Pair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock, Material World, Quiet Composition, Atmospheric Grounding, and Period & Digital Exclusions when producing Volume I visual assets. This module governs narrative evidence behavior only. Canon Records and explicit Production Spec facts always take precedence. If this module suggests that an object could carry evidentiary meaning but canon defines it as ordinary or unrelated, preserve the canon interpretation.\n\n[COMPOSITION]\nProduce one complete square decorative paper design.\n\n[REQUIRED OBJECTS AND CONTENT]\nUse an Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream paper ground with a restrained arrangement of period-appropriate botanical study imagery. Include: fine botanical line drawings or softly illustrated plant studies ferns, herbs, seed heads, leaves, or garden specimens appropriate to the established Volume I vocabulary subtle archival or cataloguing marks where appropriate faint graphite, sepia ink, or muted botanical pigment restrained foxing, handling wear, paper variation, and other material aging meaningful open areas between illustrated elements Botanical elements should vary naturally in scale, orientation, completeness, and density. Some may appear as complete studies while others may appear cropped, faded, partial, or ghosted into the paper surface. Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Otherwise use non-readable marks, numbering fragments, linework, or blank label structures rather than invented text. Orientation\nNext up: Decorative Paper. This should sit between Hero Paper and a simple repeating background. Name Decorative Paper — Core Component Specification Component Type Decorative Paper Specification Details PURPOSE Defines the reusable production requirements for Decorative Paper components. Decorative Paper provides a coordinated full-page visual surface for journaling, layering, matting, collage, and related paper-craft uses. Collection-specific style, palette, motifs, canon, objects, typography, and atmosphere are inherited from the linked Style Guide, Canon Records, Prompt Modules, and individual Production Specification. FORMAT & COMPOSITION Design as a complete full-page composition suitable for use at finished print size. The page should remain visually useful when partially covered, cropped, torn, folded, or layered beneath other journal components. Favor distributed visual rhythm over a dominant central focal point. Decorative interest may concentrate toward edges, corners, bands, or selected areas when defined by the Production Specification. VISUAL DENSITY Maintain a controlled balance between decorated and quiet areas. Fine details must remain visually coherent at final print size. Decorative density should support practical journal use. CROPPING & LAYERING Cropped fragments should continue to feel intentional when used as mats, pockets, strips, torn edges, or layered backgrounds. PATTERN & REPEAT BEHAVIOR Decorative Paper may use scattered motifs, directional compositions, borders, botanical arrangements, archival marks, surface treatments, restrained patterns, or other structures defined by the Production Specification. Repeated motifs should vary naturally unless a formal repeating pattern is intentionally specified. MATERIAL & SURFACE Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Paper grain, foxing, fading, stains, fibers, ink, botanical impressions, handwriting traces, or other material effects should remain restrained and intentional. Aging must suggest use, storage, handling, or material history rather than generic digital distress. USABILITY Maintain usable areas of lower visual density. TEXT & CANON BLEED & EDGE SAFETY Background color, texture, pattern, and non-critical decorative imagery may extend through bleed. Designs intended to reach the finished edge must continue naturally through the production bleed area. Keep critical readable or canon-dependent content inside the safe area. Natural cropping of non-critical decorative motifs at sheet edges is permitted where visually intentional. PRINT & OUTPUT Final dimensions, resolution, DPI, color mode, bleed, safe zones, front/back behavior, and export requirements inherit from the linked Print Target. Artwork must retain useful texture, linework, and tonal separation at final physical size. GOVERNANCE Those requirements belong to the appropriate governed records and individual Production Specification. When requirements conflict, apply specificity in this order: Individual Production Specification Approved canon requirements Linked Style Guide This Component Spec\n\n[ATMOSPHERE AND LIGHTING]\nA few principles: Measured precision over flourish.** Wychcombe is a place of preservation and scholarship. Sentences should feel considered, as if each word earns its place. let material details carry emotional weight. The curator's eye.** Describe objects, spaces, and people the way an archivist would — noting condition, provenance, wear patterns, what's present and what's conspicuously absent. \"The ledger's spine had cracked at the same page for decades\" tells us more than adjectives. Restraint as atmosphere.** Wychcombe's mood lives in what's held back. Grief is a pressed flower tucked between pages. Let silence, omission, and the weight of accumulated years do narrative work. Grounded sensory language.** Lean into your material palette — the smell of foxed paper, the cold of brass in morning light, condensation on greenhouse glass. These textures anchor the world more than abstract description. Time as layered presence.** Write as if the past is always partially visible beneath the present — in habits, in repeated gestures, in objects that outlast their owners.\nSoft greenhouse or muted library daylight Diffused morning or overcast light Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate\n\n[MATERIALS AND PALETTE]\nSTYLE GUIDE: Victorian Garden Journals Type: Full Suite Scope: A complete style guide covering visual language, typography, tone of voice, and production constraints for this world. All production work references this document. ## Visual Language ### Colour Palette Family Primary Use Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream Paper base, margins, writing areas Moss / Olive / Fern Botanical accents and quiet patterning Walnut / Charcoal / Sepia Wood, linework, typography, structural depth Weathered Brass Tools, keys, hardware, restrained highlights Terracotta / Muted Burgundy Rare warm accents, pots, wax seals ### Illustration Style Illustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight Diffused morning or overcast light Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate Composition** One primary focal area One or two secondary narrative clusters Supporting depth materials Quiet zones for usability Negative space is required. Preserve margins, open wood grain, quiet linen, glass light, and writable zones. Keep critical elements inside a 0.25-inch visual safety zone for 12 × 12 papers. ### Texture & Materials Watercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite Aged rag paper integration, visible paper texture Muted natural pigments, low-to-moderate saturation Softened edges, restrained engraved detail Soft archival daylight or restrained period lamplight Intentional, reviewed labels and marks only Period-appropriate natural and archival materials ## Typography & Tone ### Typeface Direction Primary: Cormorant Garamond / Playfair Display — old-style serif. Annotations: condensed or small-caps variant. Numerals: old-style figures. Display: Playfair Display SC, tracked. Secondary (UI/captions): Source Sans Pro. ### Type Hierarchy H1 Display: Playfair Display SC, 48pt+, tracked +200, Ink Navy. H2: Cormorant Garamond italic, 24pt. H3: Source Sans Pro SemiBold, 16pt. Body: Cormorant Garamond 11–12pt, 1.5× leading. Caption: Source Sans Pro 8pt. Micro: 7pt, tracked +100. ### Prose Voice Victorian naturalist-scholar — learned, precise, quietly passionate. Observation celebrated enthusiasm restrained. Author has decades of field experience and knows the names of things. First person sparingly third person for description second person only as invitation. Register: Personal / intimate ### Reference Phrases \"A remarkable specimen, noteworthy for its…\" \"The collector will observe that…\" \"For those who find themselves drawn to…\" \"There is much here that rewards patience.\" \"Drawn from the archive of…\" ## Constraints Clean digital white, polished synthetic surfaces Commercial saturation, bright synthetic color High-contrast commercial lighting, theatrical glow Pseudo-text, barcodes, contemporary labels Modern plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, synthetic ribbon ### Production Rules All artwork at 300 DPI for print, 144 DPI digital. CMYK for print (ISO Coated v2), sRGB for digital. Bleed: 3mm. Safe zone: 5mm. All fonts embedded and outlined before handoff. Paper simulation reduced for digital-only SKUs. All artwork approved in greyscale. All copy through tone review before publication. PROMPT MODULE: Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I Type: Atmospheric Grounding Injection point: shared_prompt ## Prompt Content ### Primary Create the scene within a quiet Victorian botanical and archival atmosphere shaped by scholarly intimacy, inherited responsibility, botanical care, gentle discovery, and methodical observation. The environment should feel accumulated through genuine use rather than manufactured as a decorative vintage setting. Favor aged rag paper, foxed parchment, worn walnut or oak, weathered brass, iron-gall ink, graphite, linen, natural twine, terracotta, old greenhouse glass, pressed botanicals, and restrained traces of soil or moss where appropriate. Use muted natural pigments and low-to-moderate saturation. Lighting should feel like soft greenhouse or muted library daylight, diffused morning or overcast light, with restrained period lamplight only when appropriate. Preserve quiet visual space and a sense of thoughtful observation. ### Alternative / Condensed Quiet Victorian botanical-archival atmosphere scholarly intimacy and gentle discovery accumulated rather than manufactured muted natural pigments aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, pressed botanicals and period materials soft greenhouse or library daylight restrained, tactile, observational, and non-theatrical. ## Targeting ### Applicable Component Types Hero Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera, Pocket, Envelope, Tag, Label, Hidden Discovery, Packaging and Marketing assets. Use wherever the Volume I visual atmosphere should remain consistent across the collection. ### Canon Dependency Hint Supports Canon. ### Priority / Injection Order Inject early in shared_prompt, after World & Collection Context and before component-specific composition, object, botanical, typography, or production instructions. ## Quality & Interactions ### Good Output Indicators The image feels quiet, tactile, and accumulated through use rather than staged or commercially styled. Materials read distinctly and plausibly, such as aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, glass, terracotta, linen, or pressed botanicals when present. Color remains muted and natural, with cream, botanical greens, walnut, charcoal, sepia, and restrained warm accents rather than bright or synthetic color. The overall atmosphere supports scholarly observation, botanical care, and gentle discovery while leaving sufficient visual quiet for the component's intended function. ### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present) Lighting becomes cinematic, theatrical, magical, excessively dark, high-contrast, or dominated by glow or fog effects. Colors become highly saturated, jewel-toned, synthetically green, heavily sepia, or otherwise inconsistent with the muted natural palette. The scene becomes generic antique-shop clutter, excessive vintage decoration, gothic fantasy, horror, cottagecore spectacle, or luxury stationery. Aging becomes uniform grunge or excessive distressing rather than plausible evidence of handling, use, and material age. ### Module Interactions Pair with World & Collection Context modules for Wychcombe and Volume I, component-specific composition modules, approved object or botanical modules, typography modules, and production/output modules. The governing Style Guide remains authoritative for visual language. World and canon modules remain authoritative for factual content. Production Specification requirements take precedence for the individual asset's dimensions, function, required content, and output constraints. If another module introduces conflicting atmosphere or visual treatment, resolve the conflict in favor of the governing Style Guide rather than blending incompatible styles. PROMPT MODULE: Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I Type: Style Reference ## Usage Apply to all Volume I visual Production Specs that must follow the illustrated archival visual language. Attach when generating Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other illustrated Volume I assets. This module governs rendering style only component-specific composition and production behavior remain controlled by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. Render all applicable Volume I visual assets as unmistakably hand-illustrated Victorian botanical and archival artwork. Use watercolor and gouache washes, fine ink linework, restrained graphite construction, subtle engraving-inspired hatching, visible aged rag-paper texture, softened illustrated edges, and muted natural pigments. Wood grain should appear illustrated, brass should read through warm pigment and restrained highlights, glass through softened transparency and linework, botanicals through botanical drawing and painted for