ce rather than symmetry for its own sake. Allow areas of lower detail to support more active areas and preserve the intended function of the component. When the Production Spec defines a writing-space percentage or usability requirement, treat that requirement as mandatory. The quiet area must remain genuinely usable rather than merely lighter in color while still crowded with detail. Preserve intentional visual quiet and usable negative space. One clear focal area where appropriate, restrained supporting clusters, low-to-moderate density, and genuine breathing room. Respect writing-space, cut, trim, binding, and safe-area requirements. Quiet space should feel naturally integrated into the illustrated environment. Apply wherever Volume I assets need restrained visual density, usable negative space, clear hierarchy, and protection against overfilling. The exact amount and placement of quiet space must follow the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. This module governs composition and visual breathing room only. nject in the Style System after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and Material World — Volume I, and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific composition/content instructions. Treat Production Spec writing-space, usability, trim, cut, and safe-area requirements as mandatory. The composition has a clear visual hierarchy with one primary focal area where appropriate and restrained supporting elements. Meaningful areas of genuine visual quiet remain open and usable rather than being filled with decoration, text, botanicals, objects, or heavy texture. Active and quiet areas feel intentionally balanced, allowing the viewer’s eye to rest and move naturally through the composition. Any writing-space percentage or functional area required by the Production Spec remains visibly usable and substantially unobstructed. Negative space feels integrated into the illustrated environment through paper, subtle material texture, restrained surface detail, or other contextually appropriate treatment rather than appearing artificially blank. The composition fills nearly every available area with objects, botanicals, text, marks, aging, patterns, or decorative detail. Required writing or functional space is visually occupied, heavily textured, interrupted, or reduced below the Production Spec requirement. Too many competing focal areas, clusters, or high-detail elements create visual noise or unclear hierarchy. Negative space appears as an unnatural empty hole, digitally erased region, hard-edged blank area, or disconnected background. Pair with Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I for rendering consistency, Material World — Volume I for believable low-density surfaces and textures, and Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for environmental mood. Quiet Composition governs visual density, hierarchy, breathing room, and usability. Component Specs and Production Specs take precedence for explicit writing-space percentages, safe zones, trim areas, orientation, layout requirements, and required content. Quiet Composition should help satisfy those requirements rather than replace them. Instead, simplify secondary decoration, reduce density, consolidate clusters, or reposition nonessential elements. When instructions conflict, preserve this order: required canon/content → production and component requirements → required usability/negative space → style and material refinements → optional decorative detail. This must read unmistakably as artwork in the required medium, never as a photograph or photographed still life. Subject realism, plausible materials, detail, and natural lighting describe content within that medium; they never replace the governing medium. If any part of the image reads as photography, DSLR still-life photography, hyperreal 3D, cinematic rendering, or glossy digital realism, the result fails the style requirement.\n\n[GOVERNED READABLE TEXT]\nReadable text is closed-world content. Do not invent, infer,