l / intimate ### Reference Phrases Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. ## Constraints Clean digital white, polished synthetic surfaces Commercial saturation, bright synthetic color High-contrast commercial lighting, theatrical glow Pseudo-text, barcodes, contemporary labels Modern plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, synthetic ribbon ### Production Rules All artwork at 300 DPI for print, 144 DPI digital. CMYK for print (ISO Coated v2), sRGB for digital. Bleed: 3mm. Safe zone: 5mm. All fonts embedded and outlined before handoff. Paper simulation reduced for digital-only SKUs. All artwork approved in greyscale. All copy through tone review before publication. PROMPT MODULE: Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I Type: Atmospheric Grounding Injection point: shared_prompt ## Prompt Content ### Primary Create the scene within a quiet Victorian botanical and archival atmosphere shaped by scholarly intimacy, inherited responsibility, botanical care, gentle discovery, and methodical observation. The environment should feel accumulated through genuine use rather than manufactured as a decorative vintage setting. Favor aged rag paper, foxed parchment, worn walnut or oak, weathered brass, iron-gall ink, graphite, linen, natural twine, terracotta, old greenhouse glass, pressed botanicals, and restrained traces of soil or moss where appropriate. Use muted natural pigments and low-to-moderate saturation. Lighting should feel like soft greenhouse or muted library daylight, diffused morning or overcast light, with restrained period lamplight only when appropriate. Preserve quiet visual space and a sense of thoughtful observation. ### Alternative / Condensed Quiet Victorian botanical-archival atmosphere scholarly intimacy and gentle discovery accumulated rather than manufactured muted natural pigments aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, pressed botanicals and period materials soft greenhouse or library daylight restrained, tactile, observational, and non-theatrical. ## Targeting ### Applicable Component Types Hero Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera, Pocket, Envelope, Tag, Label, Hidden Discovery, Packaging and Marketing assets. Use wherever the Volume I visual atmosphere should remain consistent across the collection. ### Canon Dependency Hint Supports Canon. ### Priority / Injection Order Inject early in shared_prompt, after World & Collection Context and before component-specific composition, object, botanical, typography, or production instructions. ## Quality & Interactions ### Good Output Indicators The image feels quiet, tactile, and accumulated through use rather than staged or commercially styled. Materials read distinctly and plausibly, such as aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, glass, terracotta, linen, or pressed botanicals when present. Color remains muted and natural, with cream, botanical greens, walnut, charcoal, sepia, and restrained warm accents rather than bright or synthetic color. The overall atmosphere supports scholarly observation, botanical care, and gentle discovery while leaving sufficient visual quiet for the component's intended function. ### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present) Lighting becomes cinematic, theatrical, magical, excessively dark, high-contrast, or dominated by glow or fog effects. Colors become highly saturated, jewel-toned, synthetically green, heavily sepia, or otherwise inconsistent with the muted natural palette. The scene becomes generic antique-shop clutter, excessive vintage decoration, gothic fantasy, horror, cottagecore spectacle, or luxury stationery. Aging becomes uniform grunge or excessive distressing rather than plausible evidence of handling, use, and material age. ### Module Interactions Pair with World & Collection Context modules for Wychcombe and Volume I, component-specific composition modules, approved object or botanical modules, typography modules, and production/output modules. The governing Style Guide remains authoritative for visual language. World and canon modules remain authoritative for factual content. Production Specification requirements take precedence for the individual asset's dimensions, function, required content, and output constraints. If another module introduces conflicting atmosphere or visual treatment, resolve the conflict in favor of the governing Style Guide rather than blending incompatible styles.\nWychcombe is a Victorian estate given over to the quiet work of preservation — a place where botanical specimens are pressed and catalogued, where archives accumulate in climate-controlled stillness, and where scholarship proceeds at the pace of careful hands. The estate comprises a main house of honeyed stone, a sprawling greenhouse complex with ironwork gone soft with verdigris, outbuildings converted to workrooms and storage, and grounds that blur the line between curated garden and managed wilderness. Gravel paths, the creak of wooden drawers, the scratch of nib on paper. Time moves differently here: seasons matter more than hours, and the work of a single afternoon might continue research begun three generations prior. Visually, Wychcombe exists in a palette of **aged naturals** — ivory paper darkened to cream, green in every muted variation from moss to fern, the warm brown of walnut cases and sepia ink, brass worn to a soft glow. Color is restrained when it appears (a terracotta pot, a burgundy wax seal), it carries weight. This is a world of **things that endure** and the people who tend them — where meaning accumulates through proximity, repetition, and care.\n| Paper Fine fiber, subtle tooth, restrained foxing, use-based wear | Wood Visible grain, soft edge wear, matte finish | Brass Soft patina, warm highlights | Glass Clouding, age, condensation, slight waviness | Botanicals Realistic fibers, veins, flattening, and discoloration |\n\n[NEGATIVE AND WRITING SPACE]\nPreserve 20% usable visual space.\n\n[CANON CONSTRAINTS]\nCanon dependency: Supports Canon Canon constraint source only do not render its title: Stationery House (accepted) The Stationery House Founded in 1884. It is not merely a shop. It is an in-world publishing house, village institution, stationer, and keeper of memory. parchment, ink, moss, sage, brass, walnut, dusty blue, berry, botanical sketches, wax seals, library cards, pressed flowers, maps, and letterpress details. a village hanging-sign logo combined with a botanical publisher’s seal and monogram. Confidence Products should feel as though they come from inside Wychcombe rather than from a generic online store. Its visual language includes parchment, ink, moss, sage, brass, walnut, dusty blue, berry, botanical sketches, wax seals, library cards, pressed flowers, maps, and letterpress details do not render its title: The Curators (accepted) The Curators are a Wychcombe institution or role-group responsible for preserving, interpreting, arranging, or controlling access to records and objects. Withholding\n\n[TECHNICAL AND OUTPUT REQUIREMENTS]\nOrientation: portrait\nFront/Back Style: single-sided\nDoes the sheet contain exactly eight clearly separated, individually usable ephemera pieces? Does each piece remain visually complete and understandable when cut away from the sheet? Do the eight pieces provide meaningful variety in size, shape, orientation, paper treatment, function, and visual density while still belonging unmistakably to the same Volume I collection? Do the pieces feel accumulated through genuine botanical, archival, stationery, and curatorial work rather than designed as a matching commercial stationery or scrapbook set? Are the required piece types represented: botanical specimen label, pressed botanical specimen card, archival/catalog card, correspondence fragment, Stationery House label or publisher’s mark, botanical field-note card, archive/library identification tag, and evidence/document fragment? Is usable writing or annotation space preserved on appropriate pieces, totaling approximately 20% of the functional ephemera area? Does the Stationery House piece use only information supported by the linked accepted Canon Record? Preserve the requested physical text-bearing object or area. Render its content as blank ruled fields, empty label or catalog areas, restrained non-semantic handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, or other non-readable period-appropriate marks. Are all important text, botanical details, labels, marks, and narrative elements safely positioned for cutting and final reproduction? Does the final sheet remain visually strong and legible both at full-sheet view and when the individual pieces are viewed separately?\n\n[NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS / NEGATIVE PROMPT]\nphotograph, photography, photorealistic, hyperrealistic, DSLR still life, photographic lighting, photographic depth of field, physically based rendering, 3D render, cinematic rendering, glossy digital rendering, commercial product photography, museum still-life photography, razor-sharp photographic textures, ** harsh spotlights, magical glow, god rays, fog, extreme chiaroscuro, cinematic contrast, blue digital shadows, geometric sans, rounded fonts, any typeface that reads as modern in primary roles, Sentences structured and complex but never opaque, ### Words & Phrases to Avoid, fantasy plants, malformed tools, heavy sepia, generic antique-shop clutter, random grunge, any visual treatment that breaks the illustrated Victorian archival system, ### Negative Constraints (Do Not), Balance the overall sheet without requiring individual pieces to form a symmetrical grid, allow decorative elements, shadows, botanicals, text, surface treatments to unintentionally bridge separate pieces, ambiguous overlaps that make it unclear where one piece ends and another begins, Important text, labels, faces, symbols, evidence, narrative details must not sit directly against a cut edge, create hairline projections, excessively intricate silhouettes, fragile edge details that would be impractical to cut at final reproduction size, A piece should not depend on neighboring pieces for its visual meaning unless explicitly designed as a set, An Ephemera Sheet should provide meaningful variation without appearing to contain unrelated assets, filling unused space with arbitrary decorative objects merely to make the sheet appear fuller, generate pseudo-text as decorative filler, create registration-sensitive front/back dependencies unless explicitly required, This Component Spec does not define the number or narrative identity of individual ephemera pieces, collection-specific style, palette, characters, locations, historical facts, recurring objects, botanical subjects, correspondence content, other canon, A Production Specification may refine the Ephemera Sheet for a particular asset, but should not silently redefine the fundamental production behavior of the component, theatrical gothic atmosphere, fantasy, horror, luxury-stationery polish, cinematic lighting, glossy digital surfaces, modern materials, This module reinforces the approved Volume I visual atmosphere and collection identity but should not introduce, define, alter canon facts, places, events, objects, discoveries, More specific approved modules and Production Specification requirements take precedence where they add detail without contradicting the governing Style Guide, Lighting is soft and diffused, suggesting greenhouse daylight, library daylight, restrained period lamplight without dramatic effects, This module establishes the atmospheric baseline and should not introduce canon facts or determine component-specific content, More specific component modules may refine composition, typography, functional requirements without contradicting the Style Guide, Are there clear, practical cutting boundaries and sufficient separation between pieces, with no unintended decoration, textures bridging them?, Is readable text limited to intentional, supported content, with no pseudo-text or invented narrative documents?, invented readable narrative text, Small evidence/document fragment such as a clipped map, ledger, filing, archival-paper fragment that suggests practical research without introducing unsupported canon, connect separate pieces with continuous decoration, background imagery, pseudo-text, invented canon, modern objects, generic antique-shop decoration, filler elements added merely to occupy empty sheet space, render the sheet as one continuous scene or desk composition, merge individual pieces together, overlapping pieces that obscure cutting boundaries, continuous decoration crossing from one piece to another, arbitrary filler objects added merely to occupy empty space, eight-identical-card grid, modern sticker-sheet styling, scrapbook-kit styling, commercial craft-store aesthetic, matching decorative stationery-set appearance, unsupported names, dates, historical claims, correspondence, institutional information, readable invented documents, modern objects or materials, make every piece equally ornate or equally dense, unrelated decorative clutter, It is not merely a shop","prompt_hash":"2c6cb1948f675b4f75e023b1163350f401a166a46bbdadba2e15e30d320b5906","compiled_prompt_status":"Compiled","visual_asset_notion_id":null,"asset_id":"WS-WYC-V00-ESWYCEPH-MASTER","asset_version":"v001","provider":"replit_ai_integrations","model_name":"gpt-image-2","model_version":null,"generation_settings":{"size":"1280x1648","target":{"dpi":149,"size":"1280x1648","orientation":"portrait","printWidthIn":8.5,"requestedDpi":150,"printHeightIn":11},"quality":"medium","estimated_cost_usd":null},"seed":null,"provider_request_id":null,"cost_usd":null,"drive_file_id":null,"drive_folder_id":null,"drive_url":null,"daybook_asset_id":null,"errors":null,"warnings":[],"failed_stage":null,"error_code":null,"resolved_source_ids":{"world":"wychcombe","volume":"78c13c7f-69bd-4ce3-8a9d-388b2992b527","world_id":"wychcombe","collection":"0d4bfa84-5411-4bfd-a7b6-25fc82d44458","world_name":"Wychcombe","style_guide":"d4fab007-9604-4cec-a100-e63f479b0a12","volume_name":"Volume I: The Curator’s Desk.","canon_records":["d2d2e2f5-bf2c-4c4e-a07d-33694f98d97d","2bd24482-d2c0-4ef9-a3a5-bc5fcc853672"],"component_spec":"97403161-2bab-4b3e-ad06-322b139e4010","prompt_modules":["e6daf9d9-a32c-457c-ba65-d818fc8de145"],"collection_name":"Victorian Garden Journals","production_spec":"900b88f0-6d83-4b30-a5bc-8a8d55f7d050","volume_notion_id":"78c13c7f-69bd-4ce3-8a9d-388b2992b527","collection_notion_id":"0d4bfa84-5411-4bfd-a7b6-25fc82d44458"},"retry_count":0,"initiated_by":"7e27c46a-7464-491a-9eb0-f23f52cc117b","started_at":"2026-08-31T12:58:44.326Z","completed_at":"2026-08-31T12:59:32.860Z","notion_retries":null,"compiled_sections":[{"key":"world_and_collection_context","label":"World And Collection Context","source":"World record","content":"World: Wychcombe\nVolume: Volume I: The Curator’s Desk."},{"key":"visual_palette","label":"Visual Palette","source":"World Bible","content":"Wychcombe is a Victorian estate given over to the quiet work of preservation — a place where botanical specimens are pressed and catalogued, where archives accumulate in climate-controlled stillness, and where scholarship proceeds at the pace of careful hands. The estate comprises a main house of honeyed stone, a sprawling greenhouse complex with ironwork gone soft with verdigris, outbuildings converted to workrooms and storage, and grounds that blur the line between curated garden and managed wilderness.\n\nThe atmosphere is one of **studied hush** — not silence, but the particular quiet of concentration. Gravel paths, the creak of wooden drawers, the scratch of nib on paper. Time moves differently here: seasons matter more than hours, and the work of a single afternoon might continue research begun three generations prior.\n\nVisually, Wychcombe exists in a palette of **aged naturals** — ivory paper darkened to cream, green in every muted variation from moss to fern, the warm brown of walnut cases and sepia ink, brass worn to a soft glow. Color is restrained; when it appears (a terracotta pot, a burgundy wax seal), it carries weight.\n\nThis is a world of **things that endure** and the people who tend them — where meaning accumulates through proximity, repetition, and care."},{"key":"prose_voice","label":"Prose Voice","source":"World Bible","content":"For Wychcombe, your prose voice should feel like **curated documentation** — authoritative but not clinical, careful but not cold. A few principles:\n\n**Measured precision over flourish.** Wychcombe is a place of preservation and scholarship. Sentences should feel considered, as if each word earns its place. Avoid purple prose; let material details carry emotional weight.\n\n**The curator's eye.** Describe objects, spaces, and people the way an archivist would — noting condition, provenance, wear patterns, what's present and what's conspicuously absent. \"The ledger's spine had cracked at the same page for decades\" tells us more than adjectives.\n\n**Restraint as atmosphere.** Wychcombe's mood lives in what's held back. Grief is a pressed flower tucked between pages. Tension is a key that fits no known lock. Let silence, omission, and the weight of accumulated years do narrative work.\n\n**Grounded sensory language.** Lean into your material palette — the smell of foxed paper, the cold of brass in morning light, condensation on greenhouse glass. These textures anchor the world more than abstract description.\n\n**Time as layered presence.** Write as if the past is always partially visible beneath the present — in habits, in repeated gestures, in objects that outlast their owners."},{"key":"atmospheric_notes","label":"Atmospheric Notes","source":"World Bible","content":"- Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight\n- Diffused morning or overcast light\n- Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate"},{"key":"material_world","label":"Material World","source":"World Bible","content":"| Paper | Fine fiber, subtle tooth, restrained foxing, use-based wear |\n| Wood | Visible grain, soft edge wear, matte finish |\n| Brass | Soft patina, warm highlights, no mirror polish |\n| Glass | Clouding, age, condensation, slight waviness |\n| Botanicals | Realistic fibers, veins, flattening, and discoloration |"},{"key":"style_system","label":"Style System","source":"Style Guide: Volume I Visual Language: The Curator’s Desk","content":"STYLE GUIDE: Victorian Garden Journals\nType: Full Suite\nScope: A complete style guide covering visual language, typography, tone of voice, and production constraints for this world. All production work references this document.\n\n## Visual Language\n\n### Colour Palette\nFamily\tPrimary Use\n--Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream\tPaper base, margins, writing areas\n--Moss / Olive / Fern\tBotanical accents and quiet patterning\n--Walnut / Charcoal / Sepia\tWood, linework, typography, structural depth\n--Weathered Brass\tTools, keys, hardware, restrained highlights\n--Terracotta / Muted Burgundy\tRare warm accents, pots, wax seals\n\n### Illustration Style\nIllustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals\n- Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight\n- Diffused morning or overcast light\n- Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate\n\n**Avoid** harsh spotlights, magical glow, god rays, fog, extreme chiaroscuro, cinematic contrast, and blue digital shadows.\n\n**Composition**\n\n1. One primary focal area\n2. One or two secondary narrative clusters\n3. Supporting depth materials\n4. Quiet zones for usability\n\nNegative space is required. Preserve margins, open wood grain, quiet linen, glass light, and writable zones.\n\nKeep critical elements inside a 0.25-inch visual safety zone for 12 × 12 papers.\n\n### Texture & Materials\nWatercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite\nAged rag paper integration, visible paper texture\nMuted natural pigments, low-to-moderate saturation\nSoftened edges, restrained engraved detail\nIllustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals\nSoft archival daylight or restrained period lamplight\nIntentional, reviewed labels and marks only\nPeriod-appropriate natural and archival materials\n\n## Typography & Tone\n\n### Typeface Direction\nPrimary: Cormorant Garamond / Playfair Display — old-style serif. Annotations: condensed or small-caps variant. Numerals: old-style figures. Display: Playfair Display SC, tracked. Secondary (UI/captions): Source Sans Pro. No geometric sans, rounded fonts, or any typeface that reads as modern in primary roles.\n\n### Type Hierarchy\nH1 Display: Playfair Display SC, 48pt+, tracked +200, Ink Navy. H2: Cormorant Garamond italic, 24pt. H3: Source Sans Pro SemiBold, 16pt. Body: Cormorant Garamond 11–12pt, 1.5× leading. Caption: Source Sans Pro 8pt. Micro: 7pt, tracked +100.\n\n### Prose Voice\nVictorian naturalist-scholar — learned, precise, quietly passionate. Sentences structured and complex but never opaque. Observation celebrated; enthusiasm restrained. Author has decades of field experience and knows the names of things. First person sparingly; third person for description; second person only as invitation.\nRegister: Personal / intimate\n\n### Reference Phrases\n\"A remarkable specimen, noteworthy for its…\" | \"The collector will observe that…\" | \"For those who find themselves drawn to…\" | \"There is much here that rewards patience.\" | \"Drawn from the archive of…\"\n\n### Words & Phrases to Avoid\navoid fantasy plants, malformed tools, heavy sepia, generic antique-shop clutter, random grunge, and any visual treatment that breaks the illustrated Victorian archival system.\n\n## Constraints\n\n### Negative Constraints (Do Not)\nPhotography, hyperreal 3D, glossy digital rendering\nClean digital white, polished synthetic surfaces\nCommercial saturation, bright synthetic color\nRazor-sharp photographic depth of field\nDSLR still life, cinematic rendering, game-concept-art appearance\nHigh-contrast commercial lighting, theatrical glow\nPseudo-text, barcodes, contemporary labels\nModern plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, synthetic ribbon\n\n### Production Rules\nAll artwork at 300 DPI for print, 144 DPI digital. CMYK for print (ISO Coated v2), sRGB for digital. Bleed: 3mm. Safe zone: 5mm. All fonts embedded and outlined before handoff. Paper simulation reduced for digital-only SKUs. All artwork approved in greyscale. All copy through tone review before publication.\n\nPROMPT MODULE: Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I\nType: Atmospheric Grounding\nInjection point: shared_prompt\n\n## Prompt Content\n\n### Primary\nCreate the scene within a quiet Victorian botanical and archival atmosphere shaped by scholarly intimacy, inherited responsibility, botanical care, gentle discovery, and methodical observation. The environment should feel accumulated through genuine use rather than manufactured as a decorative vintage setting. Favor aged rag paper, foxed parchment, worn walnut or oak, weathered brass, iron-gall ink, graphite, linen, natural twine, terracotta, old greenhouse glass, pressed botanicals, and restrained traces of soil or moss where appropriate. Use muted natural pigments and low-to-moderate saturation. Lighting should feel like soft greenhouse or muted library daylight, diffused morning or overcast light, with restrained period lamplight only when appropriate. Preserve quiet visual space and a sense of thoughtful observation. Avoid theatrical gothic atmosphere, fantasy, horror, luxury-stationery polish, generic antique-shop clutter, heavy sepia, random grunge, cinematic lighting, magical glow, glossy digital surfaces, and modern materials.\n\n### Alternative / Condensed\nQuiet Victorian botanical-archival atmosphere; scholarly intimacy and gentle discovery; accumulated rather than manufactured; muted natural pigments; aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, pressed botanicals and period materials; soft greenhouse or library daylight; restrained, tactile, observational, and non-theatrical.\n\n## Targeting\n\n### Applicable Component Types\nHero Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera, Pocket, Envelope, Tag, Label, Hidden Discovery, Packaging and Marketing assets. Use wherever the Volume I visual atmosphere should remain consistent across the collection.\n\n### Canon Dependency Hint\nSupports Canon. This module reinforces the approved Volume I visual atmosphere and collection identity but should not introduce, define, or alter canon facts, characters, places, events, objects, or discoveries.\n\n### Priority / Injection Order\nInject early in shared_prompt, after World & Collection Context and before component-specific composition, object, botanical, typography, or production instructions. More specific approved modules and Production Specification requirements take precedence where they add detail without contradicting the governing Style Guide.\n\n## Quality & Interactions\n\n### Good Output Indicators\nThe image feels quiet, tactile, and accumulated through use rather than staged or commercially styled.\nMaterials read distinctly and plausibly, such as aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, glass, terracotta, linen, or pressed botanicals when present.\nColor remains muted and natural, with cream, botanical greens, walnut, charcoal, sepia, and restrained warm accents rather than bright or synthetic color.\nLighting is soft and diffused, suggesting greenhouse daylight, library daylight, or restrained period lamplight without dramatic effects.\nThe overall atmosphere supports scholarly observation, botanical care, and gentle discovery while leaving sufficient visual quiet for the component's intended function.\n\n### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present)\nThe image appears photographic, hyperreal, glossy, digitally polished, or commercially staged rather than illustrated and tactile.\nLighting becomes cinematic, theatrical, magical, excessively dark, high-contrast, or dominated by glow or fog effects.\nColors become highly saturated, jewel-toned, synthetically green, heavily sepia, or otherwise inconsistent with the muted natural palette.\nThe scene becomes generic antique-shop clutter, excessive vintage decoration, gothic fantasy, horror, cottagecore spectacle, or luxury stationery.\nAging becomes uniform grunge or excessive distressing rather than plausible evidence of handling, use, and material age.\n\n### Module Interactions\nPair with World & Collection Context modules for Wychcombe and Volume I, component-specific composition modules, approved object or botanical modules, typography modules, and production/output modules. This module establishes the atmospheric baseline and should not introduce canon facts or determine component-specific content. The governing Style Guide remains authoritative for visual language. World and canon modules remain authoritative for factual content. More specific component modules may refine composition, objects, botanicals, typography, and functional requirements without contradicting the Style Guide. Production Specification requirements take precedence for the individual asset's dimensions, function, required content, and output constraints. If another module introduces conflicting atmosphere or visual treatment, resolve the conflict in favor of the governing Style Guide rather than blending incompatible styles."},{"key":"component_requirements","label":"Component Requirements","source":"Component Specification: Ephemera Sheet — Core Component Specification","content":"Yes. This is a good second component because it tests whether the production system can handle multiple discrete pieces on one printable sheet, rather than another full-page composition.\nKeep Component type = Ephemera Sheet.\nName\nEphemera Sheet — Core Component Specification\nSpecification details\nPURPOSE\nDefines the reusable production requirements for Ephemera Sheet components. An Ephemera Sheet contains multiple individually usable paper elements arranged together for efficient printing and cutting. This specification governs sheet-level composition, separation, usability, and production behavior. Collection-specific visual language, narrative content, canon, objects, typography, and atmosphere are inherited from the linked Style Guide, Canon Records, Prompt Modules, and individual Production Specification.\nSHEET & COMPOSITION\n• Arrange multiple discrete ephemera pieces within a single production sheet.\n• Each piece must read as an intentional standalone object rather than part of one continuous scene.\n• Maintain clear visual separation between individual pieces.\n• Preserve sufficient space between pieces for clean cutting or extraction.\n• Balance the overall sheet without requiring individual pieces to form a symmetrical grid.\n• Pieces may vary in size, shape, orientation, and visual density when appropriate to their intended function.\n• Do not allow decorative elements, shadows, botanicals, text, or surface treatments to unintentionally bridge separate pieces.\nINDIVIDUAL PIECE INTEGRITY\n• Every ephemera piece must remain visually complete when removed from the sheet.\n• Critical content must remain safely inside the individual piece boundary.\n• Borders, frames, labels, seals, tabs, tickets, cards, tags, scraps, correspondence fragments, and similar forms must retain sufficient edge definition for practical cutting.\n• Avoid ambiguous overlaps that make it unclear where one piece ends and another begins.\n• Intentional layered or attached-object effects may be used only when the Production Specification explicitly calls for them.\nCUTTING & SAFE AREAS\n• Maintain practical cutting clearance around each discrete piece.\n• Important text, labels, faces, symbols, evidence, or narrative details must not sit directly against a cut edge.\n• Decorative backgrounds may extend to individual piece edges where appropriate.\n• Do not create hairline projections, excessively intricate silhouettes, or fragile edge details that would be impractical to cut at final reproduction size.\n• Sheet-edge bleed and safe-area requirements inherit from the linked Print Target.\nUSABILITY\n• Pieces should be practical for journaling, layering, pockets, collage, documentation, decoration, or other uses defined by the individual Production Specification.\n• Preserve writable or intentionally quiet areas where required.\n• Readable text must remain legible at final printed size.\n• Scale visual detail appropriately for the physical size of each piece.\n• A piece should not depend on neighboring pieces for its visual meaning unless explicitly designed as a set.\nVARIETY & COHESION\n• An Ephemera Sheet should provide meaningful variation without appearing to contain unrelated assets.\n• Variation may come from format, scale, material, function, typography, botanical treatment, evidence type, edge treatment, or visual density.\n• Cohesion should come from the governing Style Guide, Production Specification, and shared collection context rather than repeating identical decoration on every piece.\n• Avoid filling unused space with arbitrary decorative objects merely to make the sheet appear fuller.\nTEXT & NARRATIVE CONTENT\n• Readable text must be intentional and supported by the Production Specification or applicable canon.\n• Do not generate pseudo-text as decorative filler.\n• Generic production markings may be used only where governed and appropriate.\n• Canon-dependent names, dates, locations, correspondence, labels, seals, institutional marks, or historical claims must come from approved source records.\nFRONT / BACK\nFront/back treatment is defined by the individual Production Specification. If double-sided, front and reverse designs must account for practical registration and cutting. Do not create registration-sensitive front/back dependencies unless explicitly required.\nPRINT & OUTPUT\n• Final sheet dimensions, resolution, DPI, color mode, bleed, safe zones, and export requirements inherit from the linked Print Target.\n• Artwork must remain suitable for physical cutting and use at final reproduction size.\n• Fine linework and typography must remain reproducible at print scale.\n• Required individual piece dimensions should be defined by the Production Specification when size is functionally important.\nGOVERNANCE\nThis Component Spec does not define the number or narrative identity of individual ephemera pieces, collection-specific style, palette, characters, locations, historical facts, recurring objects, botanical subjects, correspondence content, or other canon.\nThose requirements must come from the appropriate governed records and the individual Production Specification.\nWhen requirements conflict, apply specificity in this order:\n1. Print Target technical requirements\n2. Individual Production Specification\n3. Approved canon requirements\n4. Linked Style Guide\n5. This Component Spec\nA Production Specification may refine the Ephemera Sheet for a particular asset, but should not silently redefine the fundamental production behavior of the component."},{"key":"shared_prompt","label":"Shared Prompt","source":"Prompt Payload","content":"Create a portrait-oriented printable Ephemera Sheet containing eight clearly separated, individually usable paper ephemera pieces from the working world of The Curator’s Desk. The pieces should feel accumulated through practical botanical, archival, stationery, and curatorial work rather than designed as a matching decorative stationery set. Maintain meaningful variation in scale, shape, orientation, paper stock, edge treatment, visual density, and function while preserving a cohesive Volume I identity. Each piece must have a clear physical boundary, sufficient cutting clearance, and complete visual integrity when removed from the sheet."},{"key":"front_prompt","label":"Front Prompt","source":"Prompt Payload","content":"Arrange exactly eight discrete ephemera pieces on the sheet:"},{"key":"negative_prompt","label":"Negative Prompt","source":"Prompt Payload","content":"Do not render the sheet as one continuous scene or desk composition. Do not merge individual pieces together. No overlapping pieces that obscure cutting boundaries. No continuous decoration crossing from one piece to another. No arbitrary filler objects added merely to occupy empty space. No eight-identical-card grid. No modern sticker-sheet styling, scrapbook-kit styling, commercial craft-store aesthetic, or matching decorative stationery-set appearance. No unsupported names, dates, events, locations, discoveries, historical claims, correspondence, institutional information, or readable invented documents. No pseudo-text. No modern objects or materials. Do not make every piece equally ornate or equally dense. Avoid unrelated decorative clutter."},{"key":"canon_policy","label":"Canon Policy","source":"Canon Records: Stationery House, The Curators","content":"Canon Dependency: Supports Canon\nCanon Record: Stationery House (accepted)\nNarrative Details: . The Stationery House\n•\tFounded in 1884.\n•\tIt is not merely a shop. It is an in-world publishing house, village institution, stationer, and keeper of memory.\nVisual Notes: parchment, ink, moss, sage, brass, walnut, dusty blue, berry, botanical sketches, wax seals, library cards, pressed flowers, maps, and letterpress details. a village hanging-sign logo combined with a botanical publisher’s seal and monogram.\nEmotional Register: Confidence\nCanon Notes: •\tProducts should feel as though they come from inside Wychcombe rather than from a generic online store.\n•\tIts visual language includes parchment, ink, moss, sage, brass, walnut, dusty blue, berry, botanical sketches, wax seals, library cards, pressed flowers, maps, and letterpress details\nCanon Record: The Curators (accepted)\nNarrative Details: The Curators are a Wychcombe institution or role-group responsible for preserving, interpreting, arranging, or controlling access to records and objects.\nEmotional Register: Withholding"},{"key":"print_and_output_requirements","label":"Print And Output Requirements","source":"Production Specification","content":"Orientation: portrait\nFront/Back Style: single-sided\nWriting Space: 20%\nReview Criteria: Does the sheet contain exactly eight clearly separated, individually usable ephemera pieces?\nDoes each piece remain visually complete and understandable when cut away from the sheet?\nAre there clear, practical cutting boundaries and sufficient separation between pieces, with no unintended decoration, shadows, botanicals, or textures bridging them?\nDo the eight pieces provide meaningful variety in size, shape, orientation, paper treatment, function, and visual density while still belonging unmistakably to the same Volume I collection?\nDo the pieces feel accumulated through genuine botanical, archival, stationery, and curatorial work rather than designed as a matching commercial stationery or scrapbook set?\nAre the required piece types represented: botanical specimen label, pressed botanical specimen card, archival/catalog card, correspondence fragment, Stationery House label or publisher’s mark, botanical field-note card, archive/library identification tag, and evidence/document fragment?\nDoes the artwork follow the linked Volume I Style Guide, including illustrated rather than photographic treatment, muted natural pigments, tactile period materials, and restrained archival character?\nIs usable writing or annotation space preserved on appropriate pieces, totaling approximately 20% of the functional ephemera area?\nIs readable text limited to intentional, supported content, with no pseudo-text or invented narrative documents?\nDoes the Stationery House piece use only information supported by the linked accepted Canon Record?\nIs the sheet free of unsupported names, dates, events, locations, discoveries, historical claims, modern objects, arbitrary filler, and unrelated decorative clutter?\nAre all important text, botanical details, labels, marks, and narrative elements safely positioned for cutting and final reproduction?\nDoes the final sheet remain visually strong and legible both at full-sheet view and when the individual pieces are viewed separately?"},{"key":"world_rules","label":"World Rules","source":"World Bible","content":"Discoveries in Wychcombe emerge through evidence, relationship, sequence, place, and interpretation rather than arbitrary revelation.\nThe Wychcombe timeline is a layered chronology that distinguishes dated fact, approximate sequence, remembered history, disputed accounts, and narrative reveal order.\nHidden Discoveries tracks concealed material, layered clues, recoverable evidence, and revelations that become visible through sequence, context, or physical interaction.\nIn Wychcombe, memory lives through people, places, objects, habits, documents, omissions, and repeated acts of preservation."}],"generated_filename":"WYC_V00_ESWYCEPH_Master_v001.png","notion_upload_id":null},{"id":"07040cd4-dd8b-4211-b998-1657a0754e5e","production_spec_id":"0c51adac-c7cd-4416-a69c-923feaf058c8","operation":"validate_and_compile","status":"validation_failed","dry_run":false,"payload_version":"PP-2.0","compiled_prompt":"[MANDATORY RENDERING STYLE]\nThe governing Style Guide rendering medium is mandatory and overrides lower-priority rendering directions. STYLE GUIDE: Victorian Garden Journals Type: Full Suite Scope: A complete style guide covering visual language, typography, tone of voice, and production constraints for this world. All production work references this document. ## Visual Language ### Colour Palette Family Primary Use Antique Ivory / Foxed Cream Paper base, margins, writing areas Moss / Olive / Fern Botanical accents and quiet patterning Walnut / Charcoal / Sepia Wood, linework, typography, structural depth Weathered Brass Tools, keys, hardware, restrained highlights Terracotta / Muted Burgundy Rare warm accents, pots, wax seals ### Illustration Style Illustrated, tactile, plausible objects and botanicals Soft greenhouse or muted library daylight Diffused morning or overcast light Restrained warm lamplight when period-appropriate Composition** One primary focal area One or two secondary narrative clusters Supporting depth materials Quiet zones for usability Negative space is required. Preserve margins, open wood grain, quiet linen, glass light, and writable zones. Keep critical elements inside a 0.25-inch visual safety zone for 12 × 12 papers. ### Texture & Materials Watercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite Aged rag paper integration, visible paper texture Muted natural pigments, low-to-moderate saturation Softened edges, restrained engraved detail Soft archival daylight or restrained period lamplight Intentional, reviewed labels and marks only Period-appropriate natural and archival materials ## Typography & Tone ### Typeface Direction Primary: Cormorant Garamond / Playfair Display — old-style serif. Annotations: condensed or small-caps variant. Numerals: old-style figures. Display: Playfair Display SC, tracked. Secondary (UI/captions): Source Sans Pro. ### Type Hierarchy H1 Display: Playfair Display SC, 48pt+, tracked +200, Ink Navy. H2: Cormorant Garamond italic, 24pt. H3: Source Sans Pro SemiBold, 16pt. Body: Cormorant Garamond 11–12pt, 1.5× leading. Caption: Source Sans Pro 8pt. Micro: 7pt, tracked +100. ### Prose Voice Victorian naturalist-scholar — learned, precise, quietly passionate. Observation celebrated enthusiasm restrained. Author has decades of field experience and knows the names of things. First person sparingly third person for description second person only as invitation. Register: Personal / intimate ### Reference Phrases \"A remarkable specimen, noteworthy for its…\" \"The collector will observe that…\" \"For those who find themselves drawn to…\" \"There is much here that rewards patience.\" \"Drawn from the archive of…\" ## Constraints Clean digital white, polished synthetic surfaces Commercial saturation, bright synthetic color High-contrast commercial lighting, theatrical glow Pseudo-text, barcodes, contemporary labels Modern plastics, ballpoint pens, electrical devices, synthetic ribbon ### Production Rules All artwork at 300 DPI for print, 144 DPI digital. CMYK for print (ISO Coated v2), sRGB for digital. Bleed: 3mm. Safe zone: 5mm. All fonts embedded and outlined before handoff. Paper simulation reduced for digital-only SKUs. All artwork approved in greyscale. All copy through tone review before publication. PROMPT MODULE: Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I Type: Atmospheric Grounding Injection point: shared_prompt ## Prompt Content ### Primary Create the scene within a quiet Victorian botanical and archival atmosphere shaped by scholarly intimacy, inherited responsibility, botanical care, gentle discovery, and methodical observation. The environment should feel accumulated through genuine use rather than manufactured as a decorative vintage setting. Favor aged rag paper, foxed parchment, worn walnut or oak, weathered brass, iron-gall ink, graphite, linen, natural twine, terracotta, old greenhouse glass, pressed botanicals, and restrained traces of soil or moss where appropriate. Use muted natural pigments and low-to-moderate saturation. Lighting should feel like soft greenhouse or muted library daylight, diffused morning or overcast light, with restrained period lamplight only when appropriate. Preserve quiet visual space and a sense of thoughtful observation. ### Alternative / Condensed Quiet Victorian botanical-archival atmosphere scholarly intimacy and gentle discovery accumulated rather than manufactured muted natural pigments aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, pressed botanicals and period materials soft greenhouse or library daylight restrained, tactile, observational, and non-theatrical. ## Targeting ### Applicable Component Types Hero Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera, Pocket, Envelope, Tag, Label, Hidden Discovery, Packaging and Marketing assets. Use wherever the Volume I visual atmosphere should remain consistent across the collection. ### Canon Dependency Hint Supports Canon. ### Priority / Injection Order Inject early in shared_prompt, after World & Collection Context and before component-specific composition, object, botanical, typography, or production instructions. ## Quality & Interactions ### Good Output Indicators The image feels quiet, tactile, and accumulated through use rather than staged or commercially styled. Materials read distinctly and plausibly, such as aged paper, worn wood, weathered brass, glass, terracotta, linen, or pressed botanicals when present. Color remains muted and natural, with cream, botanical greens, walnut, charcoal, sepia, and restrained warm accents rather than bright or synthetic color. The overall atmosphere supports scholarly observation, botanical care, and gentle discovery while leaving sufficient visual quiet for the component's intended function. ### Bad Output Indicators (reject if present) Lighting becomes cinematic, theatrical, magical, excessively dark, high-contrast, or dominated by glow or fog effects. Colors become highly saturated, jewel-toned, synthetically green, heavily sepia, or otherwise inconsistent with the muted natural palette. The scene becomes generic antique-shop clutter, excessive vintage decoration, gothic fantasy, horror, cottagecore spectacle, or luxury stationery. Aging becomes uniform grunge or excessive distressing rather than plausible evidence of handling, use, and material age. ### Module Interactions Pair with World & Collection Context modules for Wychcombe and Volume I, component-specific composition modules, approved object or botanical modules, typography modules, and production/output modules. The governing Style Guide remains authoritative for visual language. World and canon modules remain authoritative for factual content. Production Specification requirements take precedence for the individual asset's dimensions, function, required content, and output constraints. If another module introduces conflicting atmosphere or visual treatment, resolve the conflict in favor of the governing Style Guide rather than blending incompatible styles. PROMPT MODULE: Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I Type: Style Reference ## Usage Apply to all Volume I visual Production Specs that must follow the illustrated archival visual language. Attach when generating Hero Papers, Decorative Papers, Coordinating Papers, Journal Cards, Ephemera Sheets, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other illustrated Volume I assets. This module governs rendering style only component-specific composition and production behavior remain controlled by the linked Component Spec and Production Spec. Render all applicable Volume I visual assets as unmistakably hand-illustrated Victorian botanical and archival artwork. Use watercolor and gouache washes, fine ink linework, restrained graphite construction, subtle engraving-inspired hatching, visible aged rag-paper texture, softened illustrated edges, and muted natural pigments. Wood grain should appear illustrated, brass should read through warm pigment and restrained highlights, glass through softened transparency and linework, botanicals through botanical drawing and painted form, and paper through visible fiber, tooth, foxing, and controlled aging. Hand-illustrated Victorian botanical-archival style only watercolor, gouache, fine ink, graphite, restrained engraving detail, visible aged paper texture, softened illustrated edges, muted natural pigments Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, and End Papers. Apply to all Volume I visual assets intended to use the illustrated archival language. Compatible with all canon dependency levels: None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module controls rendering style rather than canon content. High priority. Inject early in the Style System section, after governing world and collection context and the linked Style Guide, but before Atmospheric Grounding, Material World, component-specific instructions, and Production Spec visual content. Watercolor or gouache washes, fine ink linework, graphite construction, or restrained engraving-style marks are visibly present and coherent across the asset. Edges, shadows, highlights, and textures have a softened illustrated quality with muted natural pigments and visible paper integration. All major elements share the same illustrated visual language. Objects use realistic reflections, lighting, shadows, or surface textures that make them appear photographed rather than illustrated. Pair with Atmospheric Grounding for mood and lighting, Material World for material behavior, Negative Space modules for composition and usability, and Technical Constraint modules for production requirements and exclusions. The linked Style Guide remains the governing visual-language reference. Component Specs control component-specific format and production behavior. Production Specs control the individual asset's purpose, required content, composition, and canon references. PROMPT MODULE: Material World — Volume I Type: Material World Apply to Volume I visual Production Specs where physical materials contribute to the illustrated archival world. Use this module to maintain consistent material character across paper, wood, brass, glass, ink, botanicals, textiles, terracotta, twine, wax, and related period-appropriate materials. Render Volume I materials as tactile, aged through plausible use, and integrated into the illustrated archival environment. Materials should show restrained variation, handling, age, and natural imperfection rather than artificial distressing. Paper: fine visible fiber and subtle tooth antique ivory through foxed cream restrained foxing, edge wear, folds, handling darkening, faint stains, and ink absorption where appropriate. Paper must remain usable and believable Wood: walnut and other period-appropriate woods with visible illustrated grain, softened edges, matte surfaces, handling wear, and restrained age variation. Brass: weathered brass with soft patina, warm restrained highlights, handling variation, and age appropriate to frequently used tools and fittings. Glass: old greenhouse, bottle, lens, or cabinet glass with softened transparency, subtle clouding, slight waviness, condensation when contextually appropriate, and restrained reflections. Ink and graphite: iron-gall or period-plausible ink with natural variation, slight fading, absorption into paper, fine linework, and occasional handling evidence. Graphite should remain soft, matte, and visibly drawn. Botanicals: pressed specimens, cuttings, seed heads, ferns, herbs, and botanical studies should show believable fibers, veins, flattening, drying, curling, and natural discoloration. Terracotta, twine, ribbon, linen, wax, and related materials: preserve recognizable material character through restrained texture, wear, pigment variation, fibers, impressions, and handling. Materials should feel accumulated through work, preservation, correspondence, botanical study, and ordinary use. Aging must communicate history through specific physical evidence rather than generic “vintage” decoration. Tactile illustrated materials with restrained use-based aging: fine-fiber antique paper, worn matte walnut, softly patinated brass, slightly clouded period glass, absorbed and faded ink, soft graphite, naturally dried botanicals, weathered terracotta, fibrous twine and linen, and handled wax. Preserve believable material differences. Apply to Volume I visual components containing illustrated physical materials, surfaces, botanicals, archival objects, or paper-based elements. Material treatment should scale appropriately to the component rather than requiring every listed material to appear. Compatible with None, Supports Canon, Canon Reference, and Canon Defining. This module governs the visual behavior of materials and aging rather than canon facts. Inject in the Style System section after Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I and before Atmospheric Grounding and component-specific content. The Style Lock determines the rendering medium Material World determines how physical materials appear within that medium. Component Specs and Production Specs determine which materials and objects are actually required. Materials are visually distinct and immediately recognizable: paper reads as fibrous paper, wood as wood, brass as aged metal, glass as glass, and botanicals as preserved or living plant material. Aging appears plausible and use-based, with restrained foxing, handling wear, softened edges, patina, fading, fibers, folds, and natural discoloration where appropriate. age and wear appear accumulated through handling, storage, botanical study, correspondence, and archival work. The overall material palette feels cohesive, tactile, muted, and period-plausible while preserving meaningful differences between individual materials. Uniform grunge, heavy staining, burned edges, excessive tearing, artificial aging, or distressed effects applied indiscriminately across the asset. Glossy, plastic, chrome-like, synthetic, or digitally polished surfaces inconsistent with the Volume I material language. Materials lose their physical identity, such as brass appearing plastic, wood appearing digitally smooth, paper resembling flat beige texture, or botanicals appearing artificial. Aging becomes decorative rather than plausible, making the asset resemble generic “vintage” craft imagery instead of objects with specific histories of use and preservation. Pair with Atmospheric Grounding — Volume I for lighting and environmental mood, and with Negative Space or Technical Constraint modules where applicable. Material World governs how specified materials appear Component Specs and Production Specs control required materials, objects, composition, format, and production behavior. Canon and Production Spec requirements take precedence when they establish a specific object's material or condition. When instructions conflict, preserve this order: Canon and explicit Production Spec facts → Component production requirements → Illustrated Archival Style Lock → Material World → Atmospheric refinements. PROMPT MODULE: Period & Digital Exclusions — Volume I Type: Technical Constraint Injection point: negative_prompt Use across Hero Paper, Decorative Paper, Coordinating Paper, Journal Card, Ephemera Sheet, Washi Tape, Notepaper, End Papers, and other Volume I visual components. The result must remain materially plausible, illustrated, restrained, and consistent with the governed Volume I visual language. Apply to all Volume I visual components governed by the illustrated archival language. This module suppresses incompatible visual treatments and anachronisms rather than supplying canon content. Inject into the negative_prompt after the positive Style System instructions have established the intended illustrated archival treatment. Apply alongside Illustrated Archival Style Lock — Volume I as its exclusion guardrail. Maintain these exclusions throughout generation, while allowing explicit canon, Production Spec, and Component Spec requirements to take precedence where they intentionally require an otherwise excluded element. The image remains convincingly within the illustrated Victorian archival language, with period-plausible objects, materials, typography, lighting, and surface treatment. Aging and historical character feel restrained and materially plausible rather than relying on generic vintage effects. Any visible text, labels, marks, or institutional identifiers appear intentional and supported by the Production Spec or linked canon rather than invented 