ast, blue digital shadows, geometric sans, rounded fonts, any typeface that reads as modern in primary roles, Sentences structured and complex but never opaque, ### Words & Phrases to Avoid, fantasy plants, malformed tools, heavy sepia, generic antique-shop clutter, random grunge, any visual treatment that breaks the illustrated Victorian archival system, ### Negative Constraints (Do Not), reducing a full-page composition to card size, A card may combine functions when appropriate, but should not become visually or narratively overloaded, Decorative elements must not interfere with practical handwriting areas, Cards not intended for writing are not required to reserve writing space, generic decorative filler added solely to occupy unused space, simply duplicate one card template with different decorative motifs, making every card equally dense, equally symmetrical, identically composed, invent names, dates, quotations, correspondence, botanical identifications, specimen numbers, locations, signatures, historical claims, document contents, other factual-looking information, When exact wording is not governed, use blank writing areas, empty fields, restrained non-readable handwriting traces, abstract ink marks, other non-semantic treatments, create false narrative significance merely to make a card appear more interesting, Double-sided cards should have intentional relationships between front and reverse without requiring perfect registration of fine details, placing critical information directly against trim edges, Borders must account for normal production variation so minor cutting differences do not appear as obvious design errors, A card should communicate its primary function without requiring another component to make it visually complete, details, linework, typography too fine for reliable print reproduction, This Component Spec does not define individual card content, card-set size, collection-specific style, palette, botanical subjects, recurring objects, re